window.EXAM_QUESTIONS = [{"id": 2, "domain": "organization_wide_planning_and_deployment", "question": "A hospital system is building its first Six Sigma deployment and the steering committee asks how the method earned its reputation for reducing defects. The Black Belt describes the origin of the approach and the results that made it popular. Which account is accurate?", "options": {"A": "The method was developed at Motorola and later spread widely after adoption at General Electric.", "B": "The method was developed at General Electric and later licensed to suppliers through a formal program.", "C": "The method was developed by the Japanese Union of Scientists and Engineers for national quality awards.", "D": "The method was developed at Bell Labs and later published as statistical quality control standards."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The method was developed at Motorola and later spread widely after adoption at General Electric): Six Sigma started at Motorola in the middle 1980s, where Bill Smith connected defect reduction to cost, and it became widely known after General Electric adopted it under Jack Welch. Bell Labs is linked to Shewhart's early work in statistical quality control, not to the origin of Six Sigma. The Japanese Union of Scientists and Engineers is connected with the Deming Prize and Japanese total quality practice. The option that credits General Electric with the origin reverses the two companies that matter most in the story."}, {"id": 5, "domain": "organizational_process_management_and_measures", "question": "A call center answers 62 percent of calls within 30 seconds while its best competitor answers 84 percent in the same window. The operations manager wants a realistic target and a way to learn how the gap can be closed. Which approach fits this situation?", "options": {"A": "Set the target equal to the competitor result and press agents to work faster on calls.", "B": "Run a competitive benchmark against the best performer and study the practices behind the gap.", "C": "Survey current customers about their satisfaction and set the next target from the survey results.", "D": "Compare the center with its own past results and set an incremental target for next quarter."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Run a competitive benchmark against the best performer and study the practices behind the gap): Benchmarking compares the process with a strong performer, then studies how that performer achieves the result so the practice can be adapted for this center. The competitor figure alone sets an aspiration, and the study of practices is what turns the gap into a plan. A customer survey measures satisfaction rather than the process capability that produces answer speed. Comparing only with the center's own history sets an incremental target and leaves the external gap in place."}, {"id": 6, "domain": "organizational_process_management_and_measures", "question": "A bank plans to close two branches and move that work to a digital channel. The improvement team must show how the change affects customers, employees, and regulators before it proceeds. Leaders want each group's needs, risks, and measures stated in one view. Which analysis meets that need?", "options": {"A": "A control chart of the branch transaction volume recorded over the last twelve months.", "B": "A force field analysis that lists the driving and restraining forces for the planned change.", "C": "A failure mode and effects analysis scored by the process owners and the quality team.", "D": "A stakeholder analysis that records each group's needs, influence, and measures of success."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A stakeholder analysis that records each group's needs, influence, and measures of success): A stakeholder analysis lists each group, what it needs from the change, how much influence it holds, and how success will be measured for it, which is exactly the view the team was asked to produce. A control chart looks at process variation over time and says nothing about the three groups. Force field analysis examines the pressures for and against a change but does not organize needs by group. A failure mode analysis studies how a change could fail rather than who is affected by it."}, {"id": 7, "domain": "organizational_process_management_and_measures", "question": "A hospital compares its medication error rate with published rates from similar hospitals in the same region. Later it studies a hotel chain that scores highest on guest problem resolution to learn better complaint handling. How should the Black Belt classify these two studies?", "options": {"A": "The first is competitive benchmarking and the second is functional benchmarking across industries.", "B": "The first is internal benchmarking and the second is competitive benchmarking within the sector.", "C": "The first is functional benchmarking and the second is internal benchmarking across company departments.", "D": "The first is process benchmarking and the second is a customer satisfaction survey project."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The first is competitive benchmarking and the second is functional benchmarking across industries): Comparing with direct peers in the same sector is competitive benchmarking, and studying a leading organization in a different industry is functional benchmarking. The value of the second study comes from a process that is not a direct competitor but performs the same function well. Internal benchmarking compares units inside one organization, which neither study does. Process benchmarking is not the label that separates a peer comparison from a cross industry one."}, {"id": 12, "domain": "team_management", "question": "A Green Belt leading a small improvement team knows the process well but has never run a hypothesis test and cannot read a control chart. The Black Belt must decide what training the project needs before the analyze phase begins. Which training plan fits best?", "options": {"A": "Send the whole team to a general communication workshop before any technical work starts.", "B": "Have the Black Belt run the analysis alone and give the team a summary.", "C": "Train the Green Belt on the specific tests and charts the project will use, when needed.", "D": "Delay the project until the Green Belt completes a full statistics certificate program."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Train the Green Belt on the specific tests and charts the project will use, when needed): Team training works best when it is tied to the tools the project will actually use and delivered close to the point of need, because that is when the skill gets applied. A general communication workshop does not cover the hypothesis test or the control chart this project requires. Running the analysis away from the team removes the learning and leaves the team unable to explain the result. A full statistics certificate delays the project well beyond its scope."}, {"id": 17, "domain": "define", "question": "A project has eleven tasks, some running in parallel and some that must wait for a predecessor. The Black Belt needs the shortest possible schedule and must know which tasks can slip without delaying the finish. Which tool fits best for the team to use?", "options": {"A": "A Pareto chart that ranks each task by the cost of completing it on the schedule.", "B": "A responsibility matrix that lists each task beside the person who will perform it.", "C": "A process map that shows the steps in the order the customer experiences them.", "D": "A critical path analysis that shows the longest path and the float on each task."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A critical path analysis that shows the longest path and the float on each task): Critical path analysis finds the longest chain of dependent tasks, which sets the shortest possible schedule, and it reports the float on each task so the team can see what can slip without moving the finish date. A Pareto chart ranks counts by size and does not model sequence. A responsibility matrix shows who does what. A process map shows the flow as the customer experiences it but does not compute the shortest schedule."}, {"id": 21, "domain": "measure", "question": "The histogram shows 40 fill weight readings from a bottling line plotted against the lower and upper specification limits, which are marked on the chart. The team must decide whether to shift the process mean, reduce variation, or work on both. Which statement about this process is supported by the chart shown?", "options": {"A": "The process is centered on target and every reading falls inside the specification limits.", "B": "The process is capable because the tallest bar sits near the nominal target value.", "C": "The mean sits high and both tails cross a limit, so spread and centering both matter.", "D": "The readings are skewed low, so the fill head should be adjusted upward to reduce defects."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The mean sits high and both tails cross a limit, so spread and centering both matter): The histogram shows a cluster of readings centered a little above the nominal target, with two readings below the lower specification limit and three readings above the upper limit. Two readings plus three readings is five out of forty, so the process loses about 12.5 percent of its output, and the loss comes from both the position of the mean and the spread. The tallest bar sits inside the limits, but that does not make the process capable. The distribution is not skewed low, and shifting the mean alone would not remove the low tail.", "image": "question_images/q_21.png"}, {"id": 25, "domain": "measure", "question": "A call center records the number of calls that arrive per minute and finds that arrivals are independent, with a stable average rate that does not change during the shift. Which probability distribution should the analyst use to model arrivals per minute?", "options": {"A": "The normal distribution, because counts of events tend to follow a bell shaped curve.", "B": "The binomial distribution, because each minute has a fixed number of trials and outcomes.", "C": "The uniform distribution, because calls arrive at an even rate across each minute.", "D": "The Poisson distribution, because it models counts of independent events in an interval."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The Poisson distribution, because it models counts of independent events in an interval): The Poisson distribution models the number of independent events that occur in a fixed interval when the average rate is stable, which matches calls arriving per minute. The binomial distribution needs a fixed number of trials with two outcomes for each one. The normal distribution models a continuous measurement rather than a count. The uniform distribution describes values spread evenly across a range, which does not match arrivals that vary from minute to minute."}, {"id": 26, "domain": "measure", "question": "A filling process has a mean of 502 mL and a standard deviation of 1.5 mL. The lower specification is 494 mL and the upper specification is 506 mL. The Black Belt must report the capability of this process. What is the Cpk?", "options": {"A": "Cpk is 1.33, which shows the process is well centered between the two limits.", "B": "Cpk is 0.89, which shows the process is not centered between the limits.", "C": "Cpk is 2.67, which shows the process spread is well inside the limits.", "D": "Cpk is 1.78, which shows the process is capable and nearly centered."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cpk is 0.89, which shows the process is not centered between the limits): Cp is the tolerance width divided by six standard deviations, so 506 minus 494 is 12, and 12 divided by 9 gives 1.33. Cpk uses the nearer specification, so the upper side is 506 minus 502, which is 4, and 4 divided by 4.5 is 0.89, while the lower side is 502 minus 494, which is 8, and 8 divided by 4.5 is 1.78. The smaller figure governs, so Cpk is 0.89 and the process is not centered. The option giving 1.33 reports Cp, and the option giving 1.78 reports the lower side alone."}, {"id": 29, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A team tests whether a new intake form reduces processing time. The test returns a p value of 0.03 against an alpha of 0.05. A manager asks what the result means for the decision and for the risk of being wrong. Which statement is correct?", "options": {"A": "There is a 3 percent chance the new form is the better of the two options.", "B": "The result is not statistically significant because 0.03 is smaller than the alpha of 0.05.", "C": "The difference is unlikely under the null hypothesis, so the team can reject it now.", "D": "The observed difference is too small to act on until a second study confirms it."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The difference is unlikely under the null hypothesis, so the team can reject it now): A p value of 0.03 is the chance of seeing a difference this large when the null hypothesis is true, and 0.03 sits below the alpha of 0.05, so the null hypothesis is rejected. The p value is not the chance that the new form is better, and a smaller p value is evidence against the null rather than a reason to keep it. The result does speak to the decision, so waiting for a second study is a choice about risk rather than a requirement of the test."}, {"id": 30, "domain": "analyze", "question": "Data from a molding process show that parts differ far more between shifts than within any single shift. The team has been treating all of that variation as random noise. Which conclusion about the source of variation does this pattern support?", "options": {"A": "The variation is random, so no assignable cause can be found in these data.", "B": "The measurement system is at fault because shifts use different gauges each day.", "C": "The process is stable, so the between shift difference can be left alone for now.", "D": "A shift related cause is likely, so the team should stratify the data by shift."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A shift related cause is likely, so the team should stratify the data by shift): When variation between shifts is larger than variation within a shift, the shift itself is a likely source, so stratifying by shift is the step that separates the groups and shows where the cause sits. Treating the variation as random would leave that source hidden. The measurement system is not indicated here, because the study compares shifts rather than operators or gauges. A stable process would show variation spread evenly rather than concentrated between shifts."}, {"id": 33, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A process FMEA team scores a failure mode with severity 8, occurrence 5, and detection 4. The team must decide how the score ranks this failure mode before assigning improvement work. What is the risk priority number for the register?", "options": {"A": "The risk priority number is 17, the sum of severity, occurrence, and detection.", "B": "The risk priority number is 20, the product of occurrence and detection.", "C": "The risk priority number is 40, the product of severity and occurrence only.", "D": "The risk priority number is 160, the product of the three rating scales."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The risk priority number is 160, the product of the three rating scales): The risk priority number is severity times occurrence times detection, so 8 times 5 is 40, and 40 times 4 is 160. That single number ranks the failure mode against the others in the register so the team can see where to work first. Adding the three ratings gives 17, which is not how the scales combine. Using only severity and occurrence gives 40, and using only occurrence and detection gives 20, and neither of those covers all three scales."}, {"id": 35, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A machine stops four times in one week, and each stop shows a different error code. The maintenance log lists the operator, the shift, the machine settings, and the material lot for each event. Which analysis step fits this situation best?", "options": {"A": "Stratify the stops by shift, operator, and material lot to look for a pattern.", "B": "Ask the operator to recall what happened before each stop over the past week.", "C": "Replace the machine controller unit, since the four stops suggest an electronic fault.", "D": "Run a design of experiments on the machine settings and the material lots."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stratify the stops by shift, operator, and material lot to look for a pattern): The log already records the shift, the operator, the settings, and the material lot, so splitting the four events by those factors is the step that may reveal a shared condition behind the stops. Recalling events from memory adds a weaker record to data the team already holds. Replacing the controller acts on a guess before any pattern is tested. A designed experiment comes later, after the likely cause has been narrowed."}, {"id": 36, "domain": "improve", "question": "A team wants to find which of three process factors affect yield. Each factor will be tested at two settings, and the team will run the experiment in random order with two repeats at each condition. Which element of this plan is called replication?", "options": {"A": "Testing three different factors in one experiment and measuring the yield result.", "B": "Running the same combination of settings more than once to estimate error.", "C": "Running the experiment in a random order so time based drift spreads out.", "D": "Testing each factor at two settings so the effect of each factor is visible."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Running the same combination of settings more than once to estimate error): Replication is a repeat of the same treatment combination, and it gives the experiment an estimate of pure error so the observed effects can be judged against normal variation. Randomizing the run order and testing each factor at two levels are parts of the design, but neither one creates the repeat. Testing three factors at once describes the size of the experiment rather than the repeat itself."}, {"id": 42, "domain": "improve", "question": "A line is planned for 480 minutes, loses 60 minutes to breakdowns, and runs at 80 percent of its rated speed while producing parts with a 95 percent first pass yield. The plant manager asks for overall equipment effectiveness. What is the OEE?", "options": {"A": "About 66.5 percent, the product of availability, performance, and quality.", "B": "About 70 percent, the availability figure multiplied by the performance rate.", "C": "About 83 percent, the availability figure multiplied by the first pass yield.", "D": "About 87.5 percent, which is the average of the three component rates."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 66.5 percent, the product of availability, performance, and quality): Availability is 480 minus 60, which is 420, divided by 480, which is 0.875. Performance is 0.80 and quality is 0.95, so the product is 0.875 times 0.80 times 0.95, which is 0.665, or about 66.5 percent. The option giving 70 percent stops after availability and performance, the option giving 83 percent stops after availability and quality, and the option giving 87.5 percent averages the three rates instead of multiplying them."}, {"id": 45, "domain": "control", "question": "A manager sees points bouncing randomly inside the control limits for four weeks and asks the Black Belt to tighten the limits so the chart catches problems sooner. What should the Black Belt explain about this request before any change is made?", "options": {"A": "Tighter limits will detect special causes sooner and reduce the number of missed signals.", "B": "Limits based on the process data will update on their own as more subgroups are plotted.", "C": "The limits should be set at the specification limits so the chart matches the customer limits.", "D": "Tightening limits reacts to common cause variation and would raise variation over time."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Tightening limits reacts to common cause variation and would raise variation over time): Points that bounce randomly inside the limits come from common cause variation, which belongs to the design of the process, and reacting to it with tighter limits is tampering that tends to add variation rather than remove it. Control limits are computed from the process data and are not adjusted to make the chart more sensitive. Setting the limits at the specification limits confuses control limits with customer limits. Only a real change in the process justifies new limits, and the run described shows no such change."}, {"id": 49, "domain": "design_for_six_sigma", "question": "A financial company is designing a new online account opening process because no existing process can meet the target, and the design must be right the first time. The requirements are not yet fully defined. Which roadmap fits this situation best?", "options": {"A": "DMAIC, because it can be applied to a new process by treating design as improvement.", "B": "PDCA, because it cycles through plan, do, check, and act while a design takes shape.", "C": "DMADV, because it defines, measures, analyzes, designs, and then verifies each new process.", "D": "DMADOV, which is DMAIC with an added optimization step placed before the final control phase."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (DMADV, because it defines, measures, analyzes, designs, and then verifies each new process): A new process with requirements that are not yet set follows the design for Six Sigma path, and DMADV carries the work through define, measure, analyze, design, and verify so the design is proven before launch. DMAIC improves a process that already exists, and PDCA is a general improvement cycle. DMADOV is also a design path, but it is not DMAIC with an added optimize step, so that description of the roadmap is wrong."}, {"id": 57, "domain": "organizational_process_management_and_measures", "question": "A call center collects customer complaints and classifies each one by type and severity. Leaders want a measure that shows how much of the total complaint volume the largest few categories account for, so they can direct the next project. Which tool best supports this analysis?", "options": {"A": "A Pareto chart that ranks complaint categories by count and shows cumulative share.", "B": "A control chart that plots weekly complaint counts against the historical process mean.", "C": "A run chart that shows whether complaint counts drift up or down.", "D": "A histogram that shows the distribution of complaint handling times."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A Pareto chart that ranks complaint categories by count and shows cumulative share): A Pareto chart sorts categories from largest to smallest and adds a cumulative line, so leaders can see how much of the total volume the top few categories explain. A control chart tracks a measure over time against control limits, a run chart shows a trend or a shift, and a histogram shows the shape of a single distribution. None of those three answers the question about category share."}, {"id": 59, "domain": "team_management", "question": "A Black Belt forms a project team with members from operations, finance, and information technology. Two senior members dominate every meeting while the rest stay silent, and decisions stall for weeks. The Belt wants more balanced input. Which action best improves the team decision process?", "options": {"A": "Use a structured method such as nominal group technique to collect ideas first.", "B": "Ask the two dominant members to speak last at every future meeting.", "C": "Reduce the team to the three members who attend most often.", "D": "Let the sponsor make the final decision when the team cannot reach agreement quickly."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use a structured method such as nominal group technique to collect ideas first): A structured method asks each member to write ideas first and then shares them in turn, which reduces the chance that two voices control the room. Writing before speaking gives quiet members time to form a view, and it stops the first strong opinion from anchoring the discussion. Asking the dominant members to speak last is a small tweak that does not change the underlying pattern, and cutting the team or handing decisions to the sponsor removes the input the team was formed to provide."}, {"id": 60, "domain": "team_management", "question": "A plant forms a cross functional team to cut changeover time. The team includes an operator, a maintenance planner, a scheduler, and a quality engineer. The Black Belt must define who can approve changes and who reports progress. Which activity establishes these roles?", "options": {"A": "Writing a detailed meeting agenda that lists the topics for each weekly session.", "B": "Publishing a project newsletter that describes the team to the plant.", "C": "Reserving a conference room for the team on a standing weekly basis.", "D": "Creating a team charter that names the sponsor, roles, and decision rights."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Creating a team charter that names the sponsor, roles, and decision rights): A charter records the problem, goal, scope, roles, and the decisions each role can make, so the team knows who approves a change and who reports progress. An agenda, a newsletter, and a room booking are logistics, and none of them assigns authority. Clear decision rights shorten debates because people know where a question belongs."}, {"id": 63, "domain": "team_management", "question": "A project team has missed two tollgate dates. Members say they cannot attend meetings because of daily work, and the sponsor has not attended in six weeks. The Black Belt reviews the obstacles. Which factor is most likely blocking progress?", "options": {"A": "The team lacks the advanced statistical software training needed for this work.", "B": "The tollgate schedule was set before the charter was written.", "C": "The project scope was defined too narrowly for the team.", "D": "Leadership has not freed time or shown active sponsorship for the team."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Leadership has not freed time or shown active sponsorship for the team): Missed tollgate dates plus absent members plus a sponsor who has not attended for six weeks point to a leadership and time problem, which no tool or scope change will fix. A sponsor who stays away cannot remove barriers, and members who cannot leave daily work have no protected time for project tasks. Software training, a schedule written before the charter, and a narrow scope are common project issues, but the evidence in this situation points to the support role."}, {"id": 66, "domain": "define", "question": "A retail bank wants to reduce account opening errors. Leaders ask the Black Belt to define what counts as a defect and what counts as an opportunity. The Belt proposes a clear operational definition. Which statement describes a valid operational definition?", "options": {"A": "A defect is any error the branch manager decides to report.", "B": "A defect is any application with at least one missing required document.", "C": "A defect is any customer complaint about the account opening process at the branch.", "D": "A defect is any account that takes longer than the team expected."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A defect is any application with at least one missing required document): An operational definition states exactly what is counted, in terms the collector can apply the same way every time, so a missing required document is a concrete and repeatable rule. It also lets the team count opportunities, which is the number of chances a defect could occur, so a defect rate can be computed. Letting a manager decide case by case, counting complaints, or using a vague expectation all produce counts that drift between people and days."}, {"id": 78, "domain": "measure", "question": "A laboratory measures the same reference sample ten times on one instrument. The readings are close to each other but all sit five units above the true value. The Black Belt reviews the measurement system. What do these results show?", "options": {"A": "The system has poor repeatability and acceptable accuracy for the task.", "B": "The system has good accuracy and poor repeatability across the ten repeated measurements.", "C": "The system is both accurate and repeatable, since the readings vary little.", "D": "The system is repeatable but biased, since every reading sits above the true value."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The system is repeatable but biased, since every reading sits above the true value): Repeatability is the closeness of repeated readings to each other, and the ten readings are close, so the system is repeatable. Accuracy is closeness to the true value, and every reading sits five units high, so the system carries a bias that can be corrected by calibration. Options A and B swap the two properties."}, {"id": 80, "domain": "analyze", "question": "An analyst studies the link between line speed and fill weight. The scatter plot shows a strong downward trend, and the correlation is negative 0.85. A manager says the slower line causes heavier fills. What caution should the Black Belt raise?", "options": {"A": "A correlation of negative 0.85 is too weak to support any conclusion.", "B": "The sign of the correlation coefficient is incorrect for a downward trend in the data.", "C": "Correlation supports association, and a cause needs more evidence than one study.", "D": "Fill weight should be plotted on the x axis to prove the direction of cause."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Correlation supports association, and a cause needs more evidence than one study): A correlation of negative 0.85 shows a strong downward association, so as speed rises, fill weight tends to fall. Association alone does not show which variable drives the other, and some third factor could move both. The remaining options misread the strength or the sign of the correlation."}, {"id": 84, "domain": "analyze", "question": "An FMEA team scores a failure mode with severity 8, occurrence 4, and detection 6. The team debates whether to act, since the product of the three scores is 192. A facilitator notes that severity drives the response. Which action is most reasonable?", "options": {"A": "Wait for the next design cycle, since the score is below the mark.", "B": "Reduce detection first, since inspection is the quickest item to change.", "C": "Ignore the failure mode, because the occurrence score is fairly low.", "D": "Act on the high severity mode, since a serious effect needs attention."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Act on the high severity mode, since a serious effect needs attention): Severity 8 means a serious effect on the customer or the process, and the risk priority number is 8 times 4 times 6, which equals 192. A high severity mode deserves action even when the total score sits below a local threshold, and the team should reduce severity, occurrence, or detection depending on what the design allows. Detection is often the least effective lever on its own."}, {"id": 86, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A process yields a defect rate of 4 percent at step one and 5 percent at step two. A manager adds the two rates and reports a 9 percent defect rate for the line. The Black Belt checks the calculation. Which statement corrects the report?", "options": {"A": "The combined defect rate is about 8.8 percent, not the simple sum of the steps.", "B": "The combined defect rate is 9 percent, since both steps add to the total.", "C": "The combined defect rate is 20 percent, because the rates multiply together.", "D": "The combined defect rate is 0.2 percent, since the two steps share the same units."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The combined defect rate is about 8.8 percent, not the simple sum of the steps): If the two steps are independent, the chance a unit passes both is 0.96 times 0.95, which equals 0.912, so the chance it fails at least one step is 1 minus 0.912, which equals 0.088, or 8.8 percent. Adding the rates overstates the total because some units would fail both steps. Multiplying alone or dividing the rates both give the wrong figure."}, {"id": 91, "domain": "improve", "question": "A machine runs for 420 minutes in a shift that lasts 480 minutes. It produces 900 parts, and 60 are scrapped. Ideal cycle time is 0.42 minutes per part. The Black Belt computes overall equipment effectiveness. Which value is correct?", "options": {"A": "About 87.5 percent, which is the availability component of the overall equipment effectiveness.", "B": "About 90 percent, which is the performance figure for the machine.", "C": "About 73.5 percent, after availability, performance, and quality are multiplied.", "D": "About 93 percent, which is the quality figure for the good parts."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 73.5 percent, after availability, performance, and quality are multiplied): Availability is 420 divided by 480, which equals 0.875. Performance is 0.42 times 900 divided by 420, which equals 378 divided by 420, or 0.90, and quality is 840 good parts divided by 900, or 0.9333. Multiplying 0.875 by 0.90 by 0.9333 gives 0.735, or about 73.5 percent, so the other options just quote a single factor."}, {"id": 92, "domain": "improve", "question": "A team wants to cut changeover time on a packaging line. The current changeover takes 90 minutes. The team films the work, separates internal tasks from external ones, and moves several tasks offline. The Black Belt reviews the approach. Which method are they applying?", "options": {"A": "Single minute exchange of die, which converts internal tasks to external ones.", "B": "Total productive maintenance, which measures availability across the whole line.", "C": "Statistical process control, which monitors the changeover time on a chart.", "D": "Failure mode and effects analysis, which ranks the risks in the setup."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Single minute exchange of die, which converts internal tasks to external ones): Single minute exchange of die, usually shortened to SMED, studies a changeover, separates tasks that must happen while the machine is stopped from tasks that can happen while it runs, and moves as many as possible to the outside. That is exactly what filming, sorting, and moving tasks offline describe. Total productive maintenance, control charting, and failure mode analysis serve other purposes."}, {"id": 96, "domain": "control", "question": "A hospital monitors hand hygiene compliance. Each day the staff record 200 opportunities and count the number that fail. Health leaders want a chart that shows the daily proportion of failures so they can spot a shift. Which chart type fits this measure?", "options": {"A": "An X bar and R chart, because each day gives a subgroup of 200 staff.", "B": "A c chart, because the count of failures varies from day to day.", "C": "A p chart, because the measure is a proportion of failing opportunities.", "D": "A u chart, because it counts the total defects per unit across many days."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A p chart, because the measure is a proportion of failing opportunities): The measure is the fraction of opportunities that fail, and a p chart is built for the proportion of defective or nonconforming units in a sample. An X bar and R chart works with measured values rather than proportions, a c chart counts defects in a constant inspection unit, and a u chart counts defects per unit. The p chart fits the reported measure."}, {"id": 98, "domain": "control", "question": "A process runs in statistical control but produces 2 percent defects. The team wants to reduce the defect rate, not just watch it on a chart. The Black Belt reviews the next step with the process owner. Which action fits the situation?", "options": {"A": "Narrow the control limits so the chart flags more points as special.", "B": "Reduce common cause variation by changing the process design itself.", "C": "Increase inspection so more of the defects are caught before shipping.", "D": "Recalculate the limits with recent data to bring the rate down."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reduce common cause variation by changing the process design itself): A stable process with a 2 percent defect rate has common cause variation, so the way to lower the rate is to change the process design rather than tighten limits or inspect more. Narrowing limits flags ordinary points as special, and extra inspection catches defects without preventing them. Recalculating limits with recent data does not change the process."}, {"id": 101, "domain": "organization_wide_planning_and_deployment", "question": "A distribution center logs repeated late shipments that trace back to how orders are released to the picking floor. Leaders ask a Black Belt to decide whether this work should run as a Six Sigma project or as a simpler continuous improvement effort. Which screening consideration should guide the choice between the two approaches?", "options": {"A": "The number of people who complained about the problem.", "B": "The size of the gap and the complexity of its likely causes.", "C": "The budget already set aside for outside consultants this year.", "D": "The seniority of the manager who first reported the issue."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The size of the gap and the complexity of its likely causes): Six Sigma pays off when the gap from the goal is large and the cause is not already obvious, because the method spends real time on measurement and analysis. A small problem with a known cause is handled faster with a short improvement exercise. Counting complaints (A), checking the consultant budget (C), and weighing the reporter's seniority (D) describe the politics around a problem rather than whether it needs the full method."}, {"id": 107, "domain": "organizational_process_management_and_measures", "question": "A project to automate invoice matching costs 120,000 dollars and is expected to return 45,000 dollars each year for four years. The finance team discounts the future returns at the company's required rate. Which financial measure should the Black Belt calculate to judge whether the project adds value?", "options": {"A": "Return on investment, which divides total gain by cost.", "B": "Payback period, which measures how fast the cost returns.", "C": "Cost benefit analysis, which lists benefits beside the costs.", "D": "Net present value, which discounts the future cash flows."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Net present value, which discounts the future cash flows): Net present value converts each year's return into today's dollars using the required rate, then subtracts the investment, so it answers whether the project creates value. Return on investment (A) ignores the timing of the cash flows, and payback period (B) ignores the time value of money and any cash flow after the point of payback. Cost benefit analysis (C) can list and weigh benefits, but it does not by itself apply the discount rate the finance team supplied."}, {"id": 108, "domain": "organizational_process_management_and_measures", "question": "A software company tracks only revenue each quarter, and the leadership team cannot see how the daily work of its teams connects to strategy. They ask for measures covering customers, internal processes, learning, and financial results. Which business measure framework does this request describe?", "options": {"A": "The balanced scorecard, which links four perspectives to strategy.", "B": "The Kano model, which classifies customer requirements by delight.", "C": "The SIPOC map, which frames suppliers, inputs, and outputs.", "D": "The CTQ tree, which turns customer needs into measurable requirements."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The balanced scorecard, which links four perspectives to strategy): The balanced scorecard groups measures into financial, customer, internal process, and learning and growth perspectives so daily work connects to strategy. The Kano model (B) classifies requirements by how they affect satisfaction, and the SIPOC map (C) frames a process. The CTQ tree (D) turns customer needs into measurable requirements, which is a single deliverable rather than a company-wide measurement framework."}, {"id": 110, "domain": "team_management", "question": "A team must choose one of six possible solutions. The facilitator asks each member to write ideas in silence, records every idea, has the group discuss each one, and then asks members to rank the options privately. Which decision-making method is the facilitator using?", "options": {"A": "Multi-voting, where members cast several sticky dots on the leading ideas.", "B": "Consensus building, where the group talks until everyone agrees.", "C": "Brainstorming, where members call out ideas in an open discussion.", "D": "The nominal group technique, which mixes silent writing with ranking."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The nominal group technique, which mixes silent writing with ranking): The nominal group technique has members write ideas alone, records every idea, discusses them as a group, and then ranks or rates them privately to reach a decision. Multi-voting (A) narrows a list with repeated votes rather than silent generation and private ranking. Consensus building (B) and open brainstorming (C) do not follow the silent write, discuss, then rank sequence described."}, {"id": 115, "domain": "define", "question": "A software team hears customers say they want reports they can trust. The team translates that vague wish into measurable requirements about accuracy, latency, and completeness before writing code. Which tool organizes voice of the customer data into these measurable requirements?", "options": {"A": "The SIPOC map, which lists suppliers, inputs, outputs, and process steps.", "B": "The CTQ tree, which breaks broad needs into measurable requirements.", "C": "The RACI model, which assigns roles for each project task.", "D": "The Gantt chart, which schedules project tasks over time."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The CTQ tree, which breaks broad needs into measurable requirements): The critical-to-quality tree starts with a broad customer need and drills down to the measurable requirements that define success. A SIPOC map (A) frames the process, and the RACI model (C) assigns roles. A Gantt chart (D) schedules tasks, so none of those tools produces the measurable accuracy, latency, and completeness requirements the team needs."}, {"id": 117, "domain": "define", "question": "A project touches purchasing, quality, and production. The Black Belt must clarify who performs each task, who approves the final decision, who gives advice, and who receives regular updates. Which project management tool should the Black Belt use to record these roles?", "options": {"A": "The work breakdown structure, which divides the project into deliverable work packages.", "B": "The RACI model, which maps responsible, accountable, consulted, and informed roles.", "C": "The activity network diagram, which sequences the project tasks.", "D": "The toll-gate review, which approves the project at set milestones."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The RACI model, which maps responsible, accountable, consulted, and informed roles): RACI names the person who does the work, the one who owns the outcome, the people who give input, and the people who must be kept informed. A work breakdown structure (A) lists deliverables, an activity network (C) sequences tasks, and a toll-gate review (D) approves phases. Only RACI answers the who question the Black Belt is asking."}, {"id": 119, "domain": "define", "question": "A project is halfway through its timeline. The sponsor wants a formal checkpoint to confirm that the analysis deliverables are complete before the team moves into the improve phase. The checkpoint will also decide whether to release the next round of funding. Which project management tool fits this purpose?", "options": {"A": "A toll-gate review, which checks progress before the next phase.", "B": "A Gantt chart, which displays the task schedule across the timeline.", "C": "A work breakdown structure, which lists deliverable work packages.", "D": "A RACI model, which assigns roles for each project activity."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A toll-gate review, which checks progress before the next phase): A toll-gate review is a scheduled checkpoint where the sponsor confirms that the phase deliverables are complete and the project may continue or receive more funding. A Gantt chart (B) displays the schedule, a work breakdown structure (C) lists deliverables, and a RACI model (D) assigns roles. Only the toll-gate review carries the go or no-go decision described."}, {"id": 121, "domain": "measure", "question": "The histogram shows fill weights from a bottling line against a lower specification limit of 97 and an upper specification limit of 103. The distribution sits mostly inside the limits but a short tail of readings falls below 97. What does the chart suggest about the process?", "options": {"A": "The process is centered exactly on the target weight.", "B": "The readings cluster tightly near the center of the limits.", "C": "The upper limit is the main source of the trouble.", "D": "A small share of product falls below the lower limit."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A small share of product falls below the lower limit): The histogram shows 3 of 48 readings in the 95 to 97 bin, which is 6.25 percent of the output below the lower specification limit of 97. Nothing in the chart shows a problem at the upper limit, so option C is wrong, and the readings do not sit tightly at the center. A tail below the lower limit points to a process that is running slightly low or drifting.", "image": "question_images/q_121.png"}, {"id": 122, "domain": "measure", "question": "A hospital rates patient pain on a scale of 1 to 10, where 4 is worse than 2 but the distance between scores is not meaningful. Analysts plan to compare the median score across two units. Which measurement scale does this pain score use?", "options": {"A": "Nominal, because the scores only label separate groups of patients.", "B": "Ordinal, because the scores rank order but spacing is not equal.", "C": "Interval, because equal distances separate each pair of scores.", "D": "Ratio, because the scale has a true zero point at the base."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ordinal, because the scores rank order but spacing is not equal): Ordinal data can be ranked from better to worse, but the gap between 2 and 4 cannot be assumed to equal the gap between 6 and 8. Nominal data (A) has no order at all, and interval data (C) requires equal spacing between points. Ratio data (D) also needs a true zero, which a 1 to 10 pain scale does not have."}, {"id": 125, "domain": "measure", "question": "A bakery must produce 600 loaves during a 10 hour shift. The customer order rate sets the pace the line must match, and the line currently produces one loaf every 70 seconds. Which process flow metric describes the pace the line has to match?", "options": {"A": "Cycle time, the pace at which the line currently works.", "B": "Throughput, the count of loaves produced per shift.", "C": "Work in process, the number of loaves waiting between steps.", "D": "Takt time, the pace set by customer demand."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Takt time, the pace set by customer demand): Takt time is available work time divided by customer demand, so 10 hours is 36,000 seconds, and 36,000 divided by 600 loaves gives 60 seconds per loaf. The line needs to make one loaf every 60 seconds to keep up. Its current 70 second pace (A) is slower than takt, which means it will fall behind demand unless the line improves."}, {"id": 131, "domain": "analyze", "question": "Data from 20 plants show a strong positive correlation between the number of safety training hours and the number of reported near misses. A manager concludes that the training causes near misses. What should the Black Belt explain about this conclusion?", "options": {"A": "A strong correlation proves that the two variables are directly linked.", "B": "The correlation must be negative for a cause to exist.", "C": "Correlation alone cannot show which variable drives the other.", "D": "Training hours should be dropped from any further analysis."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Correlation alone cannot show which variable drives the other): A correlation shows that two measures move together, but it does not establish which one causes the other, and a third factor such as plant size or reporting culture could drive both. Option A overstates what a correlation can prove. A negative correlation (B) is not required for a causal link, and dropping training hours (D) would throw away useful information instead of testing the relationship."}, {"id": 141, "domain": "improve", "question": "A stamping press takes 90 minutes to change over between jobs, and the plant wants to run smaller batches. A team separates internal and external setup tasks, moves preparation work off line, and cuts the changeover to 20 minutes. Which technique produced this gain?", "options": {"A": "TOC, which focuses on the constraint that limits output.", "B": "OEE, which measures availability, performance, and quality rates.", "C": "SMED, which shortens changeover by moving work outside the stop.", "D": "Kaizen blitz, which runs a short intense improvement event."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (SMED, which shortens changeover by moving work outside the stop): Single-minute exchange of die separates internal setup, which needs the machine stopped, from external setup, which can be done while the machine runs. Moving preparation off line is the classic SMED move, and the changeover fell from 90 minutes to 20 minutes. TOC (A), OEE (B), and kaizen blitz (D) are different improvement concepts and do not describe this specific changeover method."}, {"id": 146, "domain": "control", "question": "A team samples five consecutive parts from one machine every hour and plots the subgroup means for the control chart. The operator asks why the team does not simply plot every single part as it is made. Which reason best explains the sampling plan?", "options": {"A": "Subgroups hide the variation inside each hour from the control chart.", "B": "Subgroups show within hour variation and make shifts easier to see.", "C": "Subgroups are used only when every part must be inspected.", "D": "Subgroups replace the need for any measurement system study."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Subgroups show within hour variation and make shifts easier to see): Rational subgrouping collects items close together in time so that variation within the subgroup reflects ordinary short-term noise, while shifts between subgroups stand out. Option A turns that claim around, because the range chart reports the variation inside each hour rather than hiding it. Plotting every part by itself would mix the sources of variation and make the shifts harder to see, not easier. Subgroups do not remove the need for a measurement study (D), and they are not limited to full inspection (C)."}, {"id": 147, "domain": "control", "question": "A hospital wants to track the share of surgical kits that arrive incomplete each day. Each day the number of kits reviewed changes, so the sample size is not constant. The team counts each kit only as complete or incomplete. Which control chart fits this situation?", "options": {"A": "A p chart, which tracks the proportion of defectives.", "B": "An np chart, which needs a constant sample size each period.", "C": "A c chart, which counts defects in one constant sized unit.", "D": "An X-bar and R chart, which tracks measured part values."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A p chart, which tracks the proportion of defectives): A p chart works with the proportion of defective units and allows the sample size to change from period to period. An np chart (B) needs a constant sample size, which the hospital does not have. A c chart (C) counts defects in a constant sized unit, and an X-bar and R chart (D) needs measured values, so neither fits a daily count of complete or incomplete kits."}, {"id": 154, "domain": "organization_wide_planning_and_deployment", "question": "A bank has chartered eight improvement projects for the year, all aimed at reducing loan processing time. The Black Belt is asked to use a SWOT analysis and contingency planning as part of strategic deployment across the retail division. Which action fits that request?", "options": {"A": "List the bank's internal strengths and weaknesses but stop before naming any risk.", "B": "Rank each project by its payback period and fund only the fastest returning ones.", "C": "Assign every project a Green Belt and report progress at the monthly review.", "D": "Plan for funding delays, staff losses, and a possible change in lending rules."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Plan for funding delays, staff losses, and a possible change in lending rules.): SWOT analysis names internal strengths and weaknesses beside external opportunities and threats, and contingency planning then prepares a response for the threats that could disrupt the deployment. Naming the specific risks with their responses is what turns the analysis into a plan. Option A stops inside the listing and names no risk, and options B and C are portfolio and staffing tasks with no risk response."}, {"id": 155, "domain": "organizational_process_management_and_measures", "question": "A call center wants to reduce average handle time. The manager suggests comparing the center against a similar department in the same company, then against a top performing rival in the same industry. What two benchmarking types does the manager describe for this study?", "options": {"A": "Internal benchmarking, followed by competitive benchmarking against a rival.", "B": "Process benchmarking, followed by functional benchmarking across many industries.", "C": "Internal benchmarking, followed by generic benchmarking with a supplier.", "D": "Competitive benchmarking, followed by internal benchmarking of sister units."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Internal benchmarking, followed by competitive benchmarking against a rival.): Benchmarking types are named by what is compared and against whom. Comparing two units inside one company is internal benchmarking, and comparing with a direct competitor is competitive benchmarking. Process benchmarking compares work practices rather than results, and generic benchmarking compares with organizations outside the industry, so options B and C name the wrong pair, while option D reverses the order."}, {"id": 161, "domain": "team_management", "question": "A logistics company needs a team that can redesign a cross site process within four months. Members come from three plants, two of which work overnight shifts, and the sponsor wants one accountable leader. Which team structure fits these constraints best?", "options": {"A": "A virtual cross functional team with a named leader and set meeting times.", "B": "A self directed work team that sets its own goals, methods, and meeting schedule.", "C": "A virtual team of volunteers who meet when their shifts allow.", "D": "A kaizen event team that completes the whole redesign in one week."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A virtual cross functional team with a named leader and set meeting times.): Members sit in three plants and work different shifts, so the team needs a virtual design, and the sponsor wants one accountable leader, which points to a virtual cross functional team with named leadership. A self directed team sets its own direction, a volunteer virtual team has no clear accountability, and a one week kaizen event cannot deliver a four month redesign."}, {"id": 162, "domain": "team_management", "question": "A project team must choose among four improvement ideas. The quietest members hold useful technical detail, and one vocal member dominates the discussion. The Black Belt asks each person to write ideas privately, then collects and ranks them. Which decision method is used?", "options": {"A": "Brainstorming, where members call out ideas in a free flowing session.", "B": "Multivoting, where the group cuts a long list down by repeated votes.", "C": "Consensus, where the group discusses until everyone can support a choice.", "D": "Nominal group technique, where ideas are written first and then ranked."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Nominal group technique, where ideas are written first and then ranked.): The nominal group technique asks members to record ideas privately before any discussion, which keeps quiet members from being overshadowed. The list is then clarified and ranked, often with a weighted vote. Brainstorming is open and verbal, multivoting trims an existing list, and consensus discusses until agreement forms without the written first step."}, {"id": 166, "domain": "define", "question": "A bicycle maker asks riders what they want. Riders mention a lighter frame and a comfortable seat, then add that a built in light would please them but they would not miss it. The Black Belt sorts these into Kano categories for the requirements list. Which sorting is correct?", "options": {"A": "A lighter frame and the seat are delighters, while the light is a basic requirement.", "B": "A lighter frame is a delighter, while the seat is basic and the light is performance.", "C": "A lighter frame and the seat are performance needs, while the light is a delighter.", "D": "A lighter frame is a delighter, the seat is performance, and the light is basic."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A lighter frame and the seat are performance needs, while the light is a delighter.): Kano groups requirements by how satisfaction responds as more of the feature is delivered. Stated wants such as a lighter frame and a comfortable seat are performance needs, where better delivery raises satisfaction. A feature that pleases buyers who would not miss it is a delighter, and basic needs are unspoken expectations whose absence would cause strong dissatisfaction."}, {"id": 170, "domain": "measure", "question": "A filling line targets 100 grams per package. The specification runs from 97 to 103 grams. The histogram of 200 packages shows a mound centered near 101 grams with a visible tail past the upper limit. What does the picture indicate about this process?", "options": {"A": "The process is centered, so the upper and lower tails are balanced.", "B": "The process is stable and all of the packages fall inside the specification.", "C": "The process runs high, so the upper specification tail is the concern.", "D": "The process spread is narrow, so no package can fall outside limits."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The process runs high, so the upper specification tail is the concern.): The histogram is a mound whose center sits above the 100 gram target and close to the 103 gram upper limit, and part of the output lies beyond that limit. A shift toward one specification limit lowers Cpk even when the spread stays inside the tolerance band. Option A misreads the shape, option B ignores the packages past the limit, and option D calls the spread narrow even though the histogram shows readings beyond both limits.", "image": "question_images/q_170.png"}, {"id": 181, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A team tests whether a new seal reduces leak rate. The test finds no significant difference, yet the new seal truly performs better. The Black Belt explains that the study failed to detect a real effect. Which error has occurred in this study?", "options": {"A": "A type I error, because the team rejected a true null hypothesis.", "B": "An alpha error, because the significance level was set too low.", "C": "A power error, because the sample size was chosen in advance.", "D": "A type II error, because the test missed a real difference."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A type II error, because the test missed a real difference.): A type I error means rejecting a true null hypothesis, and a type II error means failing to reject a false null hypothesis. The new seal truly works, so the null hypothesis is false and the test missed it, which is a type II error. Options A and B describe the opposite mistake, and option C names power, which is the chance of detecting a real effect."}, {"id": 188, "domain": "improve", "question": "A workshop floor has tools stored in three different cabinets, no labels, and cleaning supplies mixed with parts. The team sorts, sets in order, shines, and then writes a standard for each station. Which method are they applying on the floor?", "options": {"A": "The 5S method, which organizes the workplace and sustains the gains.", "B": "The kanban method, which signals when more material must be pulled.", "C": "The poka yoke method, which makes an error impossible at the station.", "D": "The kaizen blitz method, which completes changes within a single week."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The 5S method, which organizes the workplace and sustains the gains.): The five steps are sort, set in order, shine, standardize, and sustain, and the scenario follows the first four. Writing a standard for each station is the step that holds the new arrangement in place. Kanban is a pull signal, poka yoke prevents a specific error, and a kaizen blitz is a short focused event rather than a workplace organization method."}, {"id": 189, "domain": "improve", "question": "A clinic's appointment process has a bottleneck at the single triage desk, and other steps sit idle waiting for it. The team adds a second triage station and finds that throughput rises until a different step becomes the limit. Which concept explains this result?", "options": {"A": "The theory of constraints, because the system rate follows its slowest step.", "B": "The law of large numbers, because more stations reduce random variation overall.", "C": "Little's law, because throughput equals work in process divided by cycle time.", "D": "The central limit theorem, because averages from more stations become normal."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The theory of constraints, because the system rate follows its slowest step.): The theory of constraints holds that a chain is limited by its weakest link, so process output rises only until the next bottleneck appears. That is what happened after the second triage station was added. Option B concerns sample behavior, option C relates flow, work in process, and time without explaining the moving limit, and option D describes the distribution of averages."}, {"id": 191, "domain": "improve", "question": "An engineer wants to study the effect of temperature, pressure, and cycle time on yield. Testing one factor at a time would take seven runs. A full factorial at two levels would take eight runs and show interactions. Which reasoning favors the factorial design?", "options": {"A": "It tests the factors together and shows how their effects interact.", "B": "It uses fewer total runs and requires less time on the production floor.", "C": "It removes the need for replication and randomizing the run order.", "D": "It shows which single factor matters most without combining any settings."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It tests the factors together and shows how their effects interact.): A factorial design changes the factors together, which lets the analysis estimate main effects and interactions. Testing one factor at a time hides interactions and gives no evidence about combined settings. Option B is false because eight runs are more than seven, option C ignores replication and randomization, and option D describes the one factor at a time approach."}, {"id": 194, "domain": "control", "question": "A supplier's control chart shows every point inside the control limits, yet 3 percent of the parts fall outside the customer's specification. The manager concludes that the process is fine. How should the Black Belt correct that conclusion for the manager?", "options": {"A": "Control limits are set by the customer's specification, so the chart is wrong.", "B": "Rebuild the chart with wider limits so the out of spec parts fit.", "C": "Control limits describe variation, and capability compares that to specs.", "D": "Specification limits and control limits agree whenever the process is stable."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Control limits describe variation, and capability compares that to specs.): Control limits come from the process data and show how much variation common causes produce. Specification limits come from the customer, and capability indices such as Cpk compare the process spread and centering against them. A process can be stable and still fail to meet specifications, so option A reverses the source of the limits, option B changes the chart rather than the process, and option D states a relationship that does not hold."}, {"id": 197, "domain": "control", "question": "A Black Belt computes capability from 30 subgroups collected over two weeks while the process runs in a stable state. The customer asks for both short term and long term capability from that same study. Which pair of indices tells those two stories?", "options": {"A": "Cp and Cpk, because the pair separates spread from centering for the process.", "B": "Pp and Ppk, because both indices describe the long term performance of the process.", "C": "Cp and Cpm, because both indices come from the within subgroup variation.", "D": "Cpk and Ppk, because they separate within subgroup and overall variation."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cpk and Ppk, because they separate within subgroup and overall variation.): Cpk uses the within subgroup standard deviation, which reflects short term capability, while Ppk uses the overall standard deviation from every measurement, which reflects long term performance. Comparing the two shows how much the process drifts or shifts over time. Options A and C draw both indices from the within subgroup data, so they cover only the short term, and option B draws both from the overall data, so it covers only the long term."}, {"id": 199, "domain": "design_for_six_sigma", "question": "A company designs a new handheld scanner and wants to launch it with a defect level near six sigma. The design team asks which roadmap to follow. Which roadmap fits work that starts from a new design rather than an existing process?", "options": {"A": "DMAIC, because it defines, measures, and improves an existing process.", "B": "DMADV, because it defines, measures, analyzes, designs, and verifies.", "C": "PDCA, because it plans, does, checks, and acts on a repeating cycle.", "D": "RACI, because it assigns responsible, accountable, consulted, and informed roles."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (DMADV, because it defines, measures, analyzes, designs, and verifies.): Design for Six Sigma uses DMADV or DMADOV, which begin with customer requirements and then design a new product or process before verifying it. DMAIC improves a process that is already running. PDCA is a general improvement cycle, and RACI is a role assignment tool rather than a design roadmap."}, {"id": 203, "domain": "organization_wide_planning_and_deployment", "question": "A hospital records many small delays across admissions, and the delays come from travel, searching, and handoffs rather than from variation in a measured output. The leadership team asks which improvement approach fits the problem. Which combination of method and reason is most sound?", "options": {"A": "Use lean methods, because the waste sits in flow and waiting rather than in process variation.", "B": "Use a hypothesis test, because the delays must be proven statistically significant before action.", "C": "Use design of experiments, because flow problems respond well to planned factor changes.", "D": "Use process capability studies, because the delays reflect a wide specification spread overall."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use lean methods, because the waste sits in flow and waiting rather than in process variation): Lean targets steps that do not add value, so travel, searching, and handoffs are classic targets for value stream mapping, 5S, and standard work. Six Sigma suits problems where a measured output varies and the cause of that variation is unknown. A hypothesis test or a capability study can describe the delays, but neither one removes the wasted motion and waiting by itself. Planned experiments fit when several factors must be compared, not when the steps are simply in the wrong order."}, {"id": 204, "domain": "organization_wide_planning_and_deployment", "question": "A Black Belt finishes a strong pilot that cuts invoice errors by half, yet three months later most clerks have returned to the old routine. The pilot team blames stubborn operators. Which explanation and action best fit change management practice?", "options": {"A": "The clerks were not trained well, so repeat the same training session a second time.", "B": "The results were overstated, so recheck the invoice error data from the original pilot period.", "C": "The pilot team moved too fast, so shorten the next pilot to a single department.", "D": "Readiness and stakeholder concerns were skipped, so build support and communication now."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Readiness and stakeholder concerns were skipped, so build support and communication now): Change management asks the team to assess readiness, analyze stakeholders, plan communication, and involve the people who will live with the new method. When a solution fades after the pilot, the usual cause is that the affected staff never owned the change, not that the data was wrong. Retraining in the same way repeats the original mistake, and a shorter pilot would not address the resistance either. Checking the numbers is reasonable housekeeping, but the relapse pattern points to adoption, not measurement."}, {"id": 206, "domain": "organizational_process_management_and_measures", "question": "A bank call center reviews monthly customer satisfaction scores, complaint counts, average handling time, and first call resolution rates. Leaders notice that satisfaction scores fall several weeks before the complaint total begins to rise. Which classification best describes satisfaction scores and complaint counts in this setting?", "options": {"A": "Both are lagging indicators, because both are reported at month end.", "B": "Both are leading indicators, because each one tracks customer opinion directly each month.", "C": "Satisfaction is a leading indicator, and complaints are a lagging indicator.", "D": "Satisfaction is a lagging indicator, and complaints are a leading indicator."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Satisfaction is a leading indicator, and complaints are a lagging indicator): A leading indicator moves before the outcome it predicts, so the early drop in satisfaction warns of trouble that shows up later as formal complaints. A lagging indicator records a result after the fact, which is what the complaint total does. The reporting calendar does not decide the label, because a monthly measure can still lead or lag the outcome. Customer opinion is not automatically a leading measure either, since the timing of the change relative to the outcome is what matters."}, {"id": 207, "domain": "organizational_process_management_and_measures", "question": "A project team claims twelve thousand dollars of monthly savings by avoiding overtime hours that never appeared in the approved budget. Finance accepts the new figure only after the team separates hard costs from soft benefits. Which description matches that distinction?", "options": {"A": "Hard costs are estimates of future savings, and soft benefits are the audited totals.", "B": "Hard costs come from customer surveys and supplier quotes, and soft benefits come from internal estimates.", "C": "Hard costs are one time project expenses, and soft benefits are the monthly labor totals.", "D": "Hard costs appear in the profit and loss statements, and soft benefits are cost avoidance."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hard costs appear in the profit and loss statements, and soft benefits are cost avoidance): Hard savings show up as measurable changes in the accounting records, such as lower material spend or reduced labor on the profit and loss statement. Cost avoidance, like overtime that was never spent, is a soft benefit because the money never left the accounts. One time project expenses are simply the cost of the project, not a class of savings. The other options attach the two labels to the wrong sources or to the wrong time frame."}, {"id": 208, "domain": "organizational_process_management_and_measures", "question": "A project will cost a hospital forty thousand dollars in year one and is expected to return fifteen thousand dollars per year for four years. The finance director wants a simple payback estimate before approving the charter. Using straight payback, how long will the project take to recover its cost?", "options": {"A": "Two years and eight months, because forty thousand over fifteen thousand is 2.67.", "B": "One year and four months, because forty thousand divided by thirty thousand is 1.33.", "C": "Four years, because the benefits continue for the full four year period.", "D": "Two years, because the yearly benefit is close to half of the project cost."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Two years and eight months, because forty thousand over fifteen thousand is 2.67): Straight payback divides the initial cost by the annual benefit, so 40,000 divided by 15,000 equals 2.67 years. Two full years recover 30,000, and the remaining 10,000 is recovered in about 0.67 of a year, which is eight months. The four year figure is the benefit period, not the payback point. The one year and four months option divides by two years of benefit at once, which double counts the cash flow."}, {"id": 213, "domain": "team_management", "question": "A Black Belt arrives at a project review to find eleven people, no agenda, and no one who can approve the overtime needed for the next trial. The meeting ends with three action items and no owners. Which meeting practice would have helped most?", "options": {"A": "Sending an agenda in advance and inviting the people who can decide.", "B": "Booking a longer room and asking each attendee to speak once during the meeting.", "C": "Recording the whole meeting and sending the file to every manager.", "D": "Starting on time and closing with a summary of the discussion held."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Sending an agenda in advance and inviting the people who can decide): The body of knowledge names agendas, on time starts, pre work, and the right people and resources as the core meeting practices. An agenda sets the purpose and the time for each item, and including the person who can approve overtime removes the main obstacle before the meeting begins. Recording the session or adding speaking turns would not supply the missing decision authority. Starting on time is useful, yet this meeting also failed because the needed decision maker was absent."}, {"id": 216, "domain": "define", "question": "A draft problem statement says that the warehouse is a mess and that everyone should simply work harder. The Black Belt must rewrite it before the charter review with the sponsor next week. Which rewrite follows the rules for a good problem statement?", "options": {"A": "Mislabeled cartons rose from two percent to seven percent since March, adding delay.", "B": "Warehouse staff need better training because they keep making careless mistakes all day.", "C": "The warehouse supervisor should be replaced so that accuracy can improve next quarter.", "D": "Everyone must work harder so that shipping accuracy returns to the old level soon."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Mislabeled cartons rose from two percent to seven percent since March, adding delay): A usable problem statement names the measurable gap, the process or location, and the time frame, and it avoids blame and guesswork about causes. The rise from two percent to seven percent since March supplies a baseline, a size of the gap, and a start date. Claiming that staff are careless or that someone should be replaced states a cause and a solution before any data has been collected. Telling everyone to work harder gives the team nothing to measure."}, {"id": 217, "domain": "define", "question": "A charter goal states that the team will improve customer service and reduce complaints soon. The sponsor rejects the wording and asks for a SMART goal that ties to the same problem. Which rewritten goal meets the SMART standard most fully?", "options": {"A": "Improve customer service by working closely with the training department this year.", "B": "Reduce complaints by a lot before the next quarterly business review meeting.", "C": "Cut complaint volume from 420 to 210 per quarter by December 31 this year.", "D": "Make every customer happy with the service they receive from our support staff."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cut complaint volume from 420 to 210 per quarter by December 31 this year): SMART stands for specific, measurable, actionable, relevant, and time bound. Cutting complaints from 420 to 210 per quarter by a stated date names the measure, the baseline, the target, and the deadline, so the team can tell later whether the goal was met. Improving service through training is vague about the measure and the size of the change. Reducing complaints by a lot and pleasing every customer give no number, and neither one can be verified at the end of the period."}, {"id": 230, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A Belt plans a survey to estimate the share of customers who would use a new service. The team wants a margin of error of five percentage points at ninety five percent confidence and expects the share to be near half. What sample size is needed, using the standard formula?", "options": {"A": "385, because 1.96 squared times 0.25 divided by 0.05 squared is 384.16.", "B": "196, because the z value for ninety five percent confidence is 1.96.", "C": "1,000, because ten percent of the expected population is a safe share.", "D": "768, because 1.96 squared times 0.5 divided by 0.05 squared is 768.32 for this problem."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (385, because 1.96 squared times 0.25 divided by 0.05 squared is 384.16): For a proportion, the standard sample size formula is z squared times p times one minus p, divided by the margin of error squared. With ninety five percent confidence z is 1.96, and the most demanding case for an unknown share is p of 0.5, so p times one minus p is 0.25. That gives 3.8416 times 0.25 divided by 0.0025, which equals 384.16, and rounding up gives 385. The 768 option substitutes 0.5 for the whole variance term, and the 196 option confuses the z value with the sample size."}, {"id": 236, "domain": "improve", "question": "A Belt runs a two factor experiment in a plant where the afternoon shift is known to run hotter than the morning shift. The team wants the shift effect kept out of the factor estimates without abandoning the design. Which design principle applies?", "options": {"A": "Replication, because running each treatment twice makes the estimate more precise.", "B": "Confounding, because mixing the shift effect into a factor saves experimental runs.", "C": "Randomization, because running the trials in a chance order guards against drift.", "D": "Blocking, because runs are grouped by shift so the shift effect is removed."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Blocking, because runs are grouped by shift so the shift effect is removed): Blocking places runs that share a known nuisance condition into groups, so the shift effect is estimated separately instead of being folded into the factor effects. Randomizing the run order helps against unmeasured drift in time, yet it does not remove a shift difference the team already knows about. Confounding mixes an effect into a factor on purpose and would make the estimates harder to read. Replication improves precision, but the results would still carry the shift difference."}, {"id": 247, "domain": "control", "question": "A project cuts changeover time in half, and the team is ready to close the project. The new method depends on a start up checklist that operators must follow every morning. Which control deliverable transfers that routine to the process owner?", "options": {"A": "A Pareto chart, because it ranks the remaining changeover problems for the owner.", "B": "A forecast, because it predicts how many changeovers the line will need next year.", "C": "A control plan, because it names the measure, the check, and the responsible role.", "D": "A project charter, because it states the goal that the project team agreed to meet."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A control plan, because it names the measure, the check, and the responsible role): A control plan documents what will be monitored, how often, by whom, and what action follows when the measure drifts, which is how a new routine stays in place after handoff. The start up checklist becomes part of that plan, and the process owner takes responsibility for keeping it. A charter defines the project while it is running, so it does not govern daily work after closure. A Pareto chart or a forecast can support decisions later, but neither one assigns the routine to an owner."}, {"id": 251, "domain": "organization_wide_planning_and_deployment", "question": "A regional hospital system wants to know where it stands before it invests in a large improvement program. Leaders ask the Black Belt to assess the organization against a lean six sigma maturity model. What should the Black Belt explain about the value of that assessment?", "options": {"A": "It ranks each department against the others so leaders can reward the strongest performers.", "B": "It shows the current capability level and the next realistic step for the system.", "C": "It removes the need for project selection because the model names the best projects.", "D": "It confirms that the organization can deploy the full program across every unit."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It shows the current capability level and the next realistic step for the system.): A maturity model describes how well the organization uses Six Sigma and lean practices today and what capability level comes next, so it guides the order of investment. The model is not a scoreboard for ranking departments against each other. It does not hand leaders a ready list of projects, and it does not certify that every unit is ready for a full deployment."}, {"id": 254, "domain": "organization_wide_planning_and_deployment", "question": "A Six Sigma program at a bank stalls after four months. Project teams say they cannot get data from the information technology group, and two sponsors stop attending the monthly reviews. What underlying cause of Six Sigma failure should the Black Belt raise with the leadership team first?", "options": {"A": "The portfolio holds more projects than the belts can finish this year.", "B": "The training curriculum skipped the statistical tools the teams now need.", "C": "Management support faded, and the teams lack the resources they need.", "D": "The problems selected for the projects were too simple to matter."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Management support faded, and the teams lack the resources they need.): Sponsors who stop attending reviews and a functional group that withholds data are symptoms of weak management support and missing resources, the two causes ASQ names most often for program failure. A portfolio that is too large can slow a program, but the scenario shows sponsorship and access problems rather than overload. A thin training curriculum and easy problem choices can also hurt a program, but neither explains why sponsors left or why data stopped flowing."}, {"id": 257, "domain": "organizational_process_management_and_measures", "question": "A call center director watches the monthly customer satisfaction score, which arrives three weeks after the calls happen. The director asks the Black Belt for a measure that shows trouble while there is still time to act. Which measure should the Black Belt recommend?", "options": {"A": "The number of complaints closed last quarter, reported in the monthly review.", "B": "The total annual spending on service training for the call center staff.", "C": "The daily first call resolution rate, reviewed each morning by the team.", "D": "The yearly customer retention rate reported in the annual shareholder letter."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The daily first call resolution rate, reviewed each morning by the team.): A leading indicator moves before the outcome, and a daily resolution rate gives the team time to correct problems while the effect is still small. A quarterly complaint count, annual training spending, and a yearly retention rate are lagging or infrequent measures, so they report results long after the chance to intervene has passed. Only the daily measure gives the director something to act on in the same week."}, {"id": 260, "domain": "team_management", "question": "A Black Belt must add two people to a project team that will redesign an emergency department intake process. The sponsor suggests picking the two most senior nurses because seniority shows commitment. What selection criteria should the Black Belt weigh when choosing team members?", "options": {"A": "Personal friendship with the Black Belt and long service in the department.", "B": "Willingness to attend meetings and a quiet manner during discussion sessions.", "C": "Subject matter expertise, openness to change, influence, and available time.", "D": "Formal rank in the nursing hierarchy and years since the last promotion."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Subject matter expertise, openness to change, influence, and available time.): Selection criteria should cover what the person knows, whether the person supports the change, whether the person can sway peers, and whether the person has time for the work. Friendship and seniority are not selection criteria, and quiet attendance does not predict contribution. Rank and time since the last promotion describe status rather than the skills and influence the project needs."}, {"id": 263, "domain": "team_management", "question": "During a design review, the team agrees with the Black Belt's proposal after only five minutes. Nobody raises the two risks that the data clearly show. The Black Belt suspects groupthink and wants the team to examine the decision again. Which action addresses this group dynamic best?", "options": {"A": "Ask the team to vote again and treat the second result as final.", "B": "Assign one member to argue the case against the proposal openly.", "C": "Move the decision to the sponsor and close the review meeting.", "D": "Add two more supporters of the proposal to strengthen the apparent agreement."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Assign one member to argue the case against the proposal openly.): Groupthink is the tendency of a team to agree without testing the decision, and assigning a member to challenge the proposal forces the debate that was missing. A second vote without new discussion repeats the same pressure. Passing the decision to the sponsor removes the team from the review, and adding more supporters deepens the problem instead of resolving it."}, {"id": 264, "domain": "define", "question": "A bank wants to learn why small business customers close their accounts. The project team proposes a twelve question online survey sent only to customers who still hold accounts. What weakness should the Black Belt point out about this data collection plan?", "options": {"A": "The sample excludes the customers who left, so it cannot explain the exits.", "B": "Twelve questions is an unusual length for a survey of business customers.", "C": "Online surveys tend to collect data more slowly than telephone interviews do.", "D": "Business customers dislike surveys that arrive during the working week."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The sample excludes the customers who left, so it cannot explain the exits.): The project asks why customers close accounts, but the plan surveys only current customers, so the people with the answer are missing and the sample is biased. Survey length is a design choice rather than the core flaw. Online surveys usually arrive faster than telephone interviews, and the week when a survey arrives does not decide whether the sample can answer the question."}, {"id": 266, "domain": "define", "question": "A project charter states that the warehouse is slow and must get faster. No baseline, no target, and no timeline appear anywhere in the document. The Black Belt returns the charter to the team. What is the main reason this problem statement fails?", "options": {"A": "It describes the warehouse instead of the customers who finally receive the orders.", "B": "It does not mention the names of the team members who wrote it.", "C": "It focuses on speed without covering the cost of the improvement work.", "D": "It lacks a measured baseline, a clear goal, and a time frame for results."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It lacks a measured baseline, a clear goal, and a time frame for results.): A usable problem statement names how bad the problem is today, how much improvement is expected, and by when, and this charter has none of those elements. Naming the customers is helpful but is not the missing piece. Team member names belong in a different section, and the cost of the work supports the business case rather than defining the problem."}, {"id": 272, "domain": "measure", "question": "A plant ships parts on three shifts, and defect rates differ by shift. The Black Belt must draw 120 parts from this week's output so every shift appears in the sample in proportion to its volume. Which sampling method should be used?", "options": {"A": "Simple random sampling that selects 120 parts from the full weekly output.", "B": "Systematic sampling that takes every tenth part as it leaves the line.", "C": "Stratified sampling that takes a proportional set from each shift group.", "D": "Block sampling that takes all 120 parts from one selected day of output."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stratified sampling that takes a proportional set from each shift group.): Stratified sampling divides the population into groups that differ in an important way and then samples each group in proportion, which is exactly what the three shifts require. Simple random sampling can under represent a shift by chance. Systematic sampling follows a fixed interval without regard to shift, and taking all parts from one day concentrates the sample instead of spreading it."}, {"id": 275, "domain": "measure", "question": "Two inspectors count scratches on painted panels and report different totals for the same batch. One counts a scratch shorter than two millimeters, and the other does not. Which element should the Black Belt add to the data collection plan to fix this disagreement?", "options": {"A": "A larger sample size that covers many more panels taken from the same batch.", "B": "An operational definition that states exactly what counts as a scratch.", "C": "A control chart that tracks the counts reported by both inspectors.", "D": "A second shift of inspectors who can compare notes with each other."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (An operational definition that states exactly what counts as a scratch.): Two inspectors disagree because each applies a different rule, so the plan needs a precise written definition with a size threshold and a reference sample that both inspectors use. A larger sample does not fix a disagreement about what to count. A control chart would track the two different counts without reconciling them, and more inspectors would multiply the same ambiguity."}, {"id": 276, "domain": "measure", "question": "A team samples delivery times from a warehouse. The individual times follow a skewed distribution, but the team needs to use a confidence interval for the mean. The Black Belt explains that the sampling distribution of the mean behaves differently as sample size grows. What should the explanation say?", "options": {"A": "The sampling distribution stays skewed no matter how large the sample becomes.", "B": "The sampling distribution matches the shape of the population at every size.", "C": "The sampling distribution looks more normal as the sample size increases.", "D": "The sampling distribution becomes wider as more observations enter each sample."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The sampling distribution looks more normal as the sample size increases.): The central limit theorem says the distribution of sample means approaches a normal shape as sample size grows, even when the individual values come from a skewed population, which is why the confidence interval procedure works. The distribution does not stay skewed at large sample sizes. It does not copy the population shape at every size, and it becomes narrower rather than wider as the sample grows."}, {"id": 278, "domain": "measure", "question": "A four step assembly process has first pass yields of 0.98, 0.95, 0.97, and 0.99. The plant manager asks for the rolled throughput yield because the steps run in series. What is the rolled throughput yield, and what does the value mean for the line?", "options": {"A": "0.97, which is the plain average of the four individual step yields.", "B": "0.89, which is the chance a unit passes all four steps without rework.", "C": "0.99, which is the yield of the best step in the four step series.", "D": "3.89, which is the sum of the four individual step yields added together."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (0.89, which is the chance a unit passes all four steps without rework.): Rolled throughput yield multiplies the step yields because a unit must survive every step, so 0.98 times 0.95 times 0.97 times 0.99 equals 0.8940, which is about 0.89. The plain average of the four yields is 0.9725, and that number overstates the line. The best single step is 0.99, and 3.89 is the sum of the four yields, so neither of those answers the question."}, {"id": 279, "domain": "measure", "question": "A machine shop's control chart shows natural process limits of 24.6 to 25.4 millimeters. The customer drawing allows 24.8 to 25.3 millimeters. The shop manager says the process is in control, so the parts must be acceptable. What should the Black Belt explain?", "options": {"A": "Control limits and specification limits are interchangeable when the process stays stable.", "B": "The process is capable because it stayed in control during the study period.", "C": "The process limits can be adjusted to match the customer drawing limits.", "D": "The process spread exceeds the drawing limits, so the line is not capable."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The process spread exceeds the drawing limits, so the line is not capable.): Control limits come from the process itself and describe how it behaves, while the specification limits come from the customer, and here the natural range of 24.6 to 25.4 is wider than the allowed 24.8 to 25.3, so the process will make nonconforming parts. Control limits and specification limits are different things. Staying in control means the process is stable rather than capable, and process limits cannot be adjusted to match a drawing."}, {"id": 280, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A team measures line speed and fill weight at 20 settings and finds a correlation coefficient of negative 0.92. The process owner says this proves that faster filling causes lighter fills. What should the Black Belt say about that claim?", "options": {"A": "The coefficient is far too weak to support any statement about the two variables.", "B": "The coefficient proves causation because it is close to negative one.", "C": "The coefficient shows a strong association, but causation needs more evidence.", "D": "The coefficient shows that fill weight causes the line speed to change."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The coefficient shows a strong association, but causation needs more evidence.): A correlation of negative 0.92 shows a strong negative relationship, and the negative sign means higher speed tends to pair with lower fill weight, but correlation alone cannot prove that one variable causes the other. The coefficient is not weak. A value near negative one does not establish causation, and the data give no reason to reverse the direction and claim that fill weight drives line speed."}, {"id": 281, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A pharmacy tracks 220 dispensing errors over a quarter and plots them on a Pareto chart. Missing labels account for 84 errors, wrong dose for 61, late delivery for 38, damaged cartons for 22, and other causes for 15. What does the chart show?", "options": {"A": "Two causes account for about 66 percent of the errors, so they come first.", "B": "The five causes each contribute roughly the same share of the total errors.", "C": "The largest cause accounts for more than half of all the recorded errors.", "D": "The smallest cause should be attacked first because it is easiest to fix."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Two causes account for about 66 percent of the errors, so they come first.): The chart totals 84 + 61 + 38 + 22 + 15 = 220 errors, and the two largest bars add to 84 + 61 = 145, which is 145 divided by 220 = 65.9 percent, so missing labels and wrong dose are the vital few. The causes are not equal in size. The largest single cause is 84 divided by 220 = 38 percent, not more than half, and attacking the smallest cause first ignores the Pareto principle.", "image": "question_images/q_281.png"}, {"id": 283, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A finance team builds a cause and effect diagram to explain why monthly reports arrive late. The team has grouped possible causes under six headings, including methods, machines, people, materials, measurement, and environment. What should the Black Belt do next with this diagram?", "options": {"A": "Treat every listed cause as a confirmed root cause and start fixing them all.", "B": "Convert the diagram into a control chart that monitors the report cycle time.", "C": "Delete the categories that hold few causes and keep only the largest group.", "D": "Use the diagram to select candidate causes and then test them with data."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use the diagram to select candidate causes and then test them with data.): A cause and effect diagram organizes possible causes into categories so the team can choose the most likely ones and confirm them with evidence before changing the process. Every listed cause is a hypothesis rather than a confirmed root cause. The diagram is not a control chart, and deleting categories that hold fewer causes removes possible explanations before any data have been collected.", "image": "question_images/q_283.png"}, {"id": 285, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A fault tree models a pump failure with two independent basic events that feed an OR gate. The seal leak event has a probability of 0.05, and the bearing seizure event has a probability of 0.03. What is the probability that the top event occurs?", "options": {"A": "About 0.08, because the two event probabilities are added together.", "B": "About 0.002, because the two event probabilities are multiplied together.", "C": "About 0.05, because the larger of the two events controls the result.", "D": "About 0.03, because the smaller of the two events sets the failure rate."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 0.08, because the two event probabilities are added together.): An OR gate fails when either input occurs, and for two independent events the exact rule is 0.05 + 0.03 minus 0.05 times 0.03, which equals 0.08 minus 0.0015, so the result is 0.0785 and the simple addition gives a close answer that rounds to about 0.08. The product of the two events, 0.0015, describes an AND gate rather than an OR gate. Neither the larger nor the smaller event alone sets the top event probability."}, {"id": 287, "domain": "improve", "question": "A team plans an experiment on a coating line and runs all the trials with the higher temperature setting on Monday morning. The oven was serviced over the weekend. What design principle did the team overlook when it set the run order?", "options": {"A": "Replication, which repeats each treatment combination more than one time in a study.", "B": "Randomization, which spreads the effect of uncontrolled factors across the runs.", "C": "Blocking, which groups similar experimental units into separate sets of runs.", "D": "Power, which sets the chance of detecting a real effect when one exists."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Randomization, which spreads the effect of uncontrolled factors across the runs.): Randomizing the run order keeps an outside event, such as the weekend oven service, from lining up with one factor level, so its effect does not masquerade as a treatment effect. Replication repeats treatment combinations but does not fix a run order that confounds time with temperature. Blocking handles known sources of variation by grouping similar units, and power describes the sensitivity of the test rather than the order of the runs."}, {"id": 288, "domain": "improve", "question": "A value stream map of an order entry process shows five steps. The value added time totals 12 minutes and the non-value added time totals 48 minutes, giving a lead time of 60 minutes. The team wants to cut the lead time in half. Where should the team focus?", "options": {"A": "Reduce the value added work, which fills 60 minutes of the total lead time.", "B": "Balance the work across the five steps so each one takes 12 minutes.", "C": "Remove the non-value added time, which is 80 percent of the lead time.", "D": "Add a sixth step so the work spreads across more people on the team."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Remove the non-value added time, which is 80 percent of the lead time.): The map shows 12 minutes of value added work and 48 minutes of non-value added time, and 48 divided by 60 equals 0.80, so most of the lead time is waiting and handling that the customer does not pay for. Value added work is 12 minutes rather than 60, so that first option misreads the map. Balancing the steps would not remove the waiting, and adding a step usually adds lead time rather than removing it.", "image": "question_images/q_288.png"}, {"id": 292, "domain": "improve", "question": "A plant wants to attack small, steady improvements across many work cells over the next year, with each team meeting weekly for an hour. A manager suggests holding one week long event instead to fix a single cell fast. How should the Black Belt distinguish the two approaches?", "options": {"A": "Kaizen blitz is the weekly routine, and kaizen is the one week focused event.", "B": "Both approaches require a full week of dedicated time away from daily work.", "C": "Kaizen is ongoing small improvement, while a kaizen blitz is a short event.", "D": "Kaizen blitz replaces standard work, and kaizen replaces the control plan."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Kaizen is ongoing small improvement, while a kaizen blitz is a short event.): Kaizen is continuous, incremental improvement built into daily work, while a kaizen blitz gathers a team for a short, focused burst on one area. The first option swaps the two definitions. A blitz uses dedicated time, but ongoing kaizen does not require a week away from the daily work. Neither method replaces standard work or a control plan, which are tools for holding the gains."}, {"id": 299, "domain": "design_for_six_sigma", "question": "A company is designing a new online claims portal rather than fixing an existing process. A manager proposes using DMAIC because the team already knows that method well and the schedule is tight. How should the Black Belt respond to this suggestion?", "options": {"A": "DMAIC and DMADV are basically interchangeable, so either roadmap would fit this work.", "B": "DMAIC should be used first, and DMADV can follow once the portal is live.", "C": "DMADV fits new design work, while DMAIC fits improving an existing process.", "D": "DMADV applies only to hardware, so a software portal needs the DMAIC path."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (DMADV fits new design work, while DMAIC fits improving an existing process.): DMADV defines, measures, analyzes, designs, and validates a new offering, while DMAIC improves a process that already exists, and the claims portal is new work. The two roadmaps are not interchangeable because their phases answer different questions. Running DMAIC first would improve a process the company has not built yet, and DMADV is not limited to hardware."}, {"id": 302, "domain": "organization_wide_planning_and_deployment", "question": "A team proposes a project that should cut scrap by 300,000 dollars a year. The company also expects faster answers for customers, which no one can price yet. Finance asks the Black Belt to separate the two benefits in the business case. Which terms fit these two kinds of benefit?", "options": {"A": "Both benefits are hard savings, because customers asked for more rapid answers.", "B": "Both benefits are soft savings, because neither one appears in a budget line.", "C": "Scrap reduction is a hard saving, and faster answers are a soft saving.", "D": "Scrap reduction is a soft saving, and faster answers are a hard saving."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Scrap reduction is a hard saving, and faster answers are a soft saving): Hard savings show up as real money in a budget line, and cutting scrap by 300,000 dollars a year does that. Soft savings are real benefits that are hard to price, such as faster answers that may raise loyalty but cannot be posted to an account. Calling both benefits hard ignores that the faster answers have no agreed dollar value, and calling both soft ignores the measurable scrap reduction. The split in C is the one a finance team can defend."}, {"id": 305, "domain": "organizational_process_management_and_measures", "question": "A call center tracks four cost accounts: refunds paid to customers after errors, wages for inspectors who sample calls, training for agents, and rework of call notes. The quality manager asks the Black Belt to sort these into cost of quality categories. Which grouping is correct?", "options": {"A": "Refunds are external failure, inspections are prevention, and the training is appraisal.", "B": "Refunds are external failure, inspections are appraisal, and training is prevention.", "C": "Refunds are appraisal, inspections are external failure, and training is prevention.", "D": "Refunds are prevention, inspections are internal failure, and training is appraisal."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Refunds are external failure, inspections are appraisal, and training is prevention): A refund paid after a customer is harmed is an external failure cost. Sampling calls to catch errors is appraisal, because the work is checking products or services. Training that keeps errors from happening is prevention. Option A calls inspection prevention and training appraisal, C calls refunds appraisal, and D calls refunds prevention, so each one shifts the categories in the wrong direction."}, {"id": 307, "domain": "organizational_process_management_and_measures", "question": "A bank's loan process crosses four departments, and each department measures its own step. Customers see the whole journey, and no one owns the total time. The Black Belt recommends appointing a process owner. What should that owner be accountable for?", "options": {"A": "The performance of one department and the budget that supports that department.", "B": "The measurement plan for each department and the reviews that follow it.", "C": "The customer complaints that reach the call center each quarter.", "D": "The end to end performance of the process and customer results."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The end to end performance of the process and customer results): A process owner is accountable for the whole cross functional process, including the total time a customer experiences, not for a single department step. Option A describes a department manager, who owns one part of the flow, and option B describes a measurement coordinator. Customer complaints that reach the call center are one symptom rather than the full responsibility, so C is too narrow. The owner exists so that someone answers for the complete journey."}, {"id": 310, "domain": "team_management", "question": "A team of ten must pick three causes to study from a list of twenty. Open discussion favors the loudest voices, and quiet members stay silent. The facilitator wants every member to rank the causes privately before the group discusses them. Which tool fits this need?", "options": {"A": "A force field analysis that lists the forces helping and blocking the proposed change.", "B": "A nominal group technique that collects private rankings before group discussion.", "C": "A fishbone diagram that sorts the causes into six standard categories.", "D": "A control chart that shows whether the cause counts are stable over time."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A nominal group technique that collects private rankings before group discussion): Nominal group technique asks members to write and rank ideas on their own, then the group discusses the pooled list, which gives quiet members the same weight as talkative ones. A fishbone diagram sorts causes into categories but does not collect private rankings, and a force field analysis weighs the forces for and against a change. A control chart tracks stability over time rather than choosing among causes. Because the goal is equal input before discussion, the private ranking step is the deciding feature."}, {"id": 311, "domain": "team_management", "question": "Two engineers on a process team disagree about the root cause. The argument turns personal, and the meeting stalls. The Black Belt reminds the team of its ground rules: attack the problem, not the person, and support claims with data. What should the Black Belt do next?", "options": {"A": "End the discussion and assign the root cause decision to the team leader.", "B": "Ask both engineers to present their views to the sponsor for a ruling.", "C": "Move to the next agenda item and revisit the disagreement next month.", "D": "Refocus both engineers on the data and the shared problem statement."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Refocus both engineers on the data and the shared problem statement): Ground rules that ask people to attack the problem rather than the person work best when the facilitator pulls the discussion back to evidence and the agreed problem statement. Ending the discussion or handing the decision to the sponsor removes the team's ownership, and deferring the issue for a month leaves the conflict to grow. Because the disagreement is technical in origin, the data is the neutral ground both engineers can examine. The Black Belt keeps the team on the problem rather than on the personalities."}, {"id": 316, "domain": "define", "question": "A bicycle maker lists customer wants: light weight, low price, and a smooth ride. The team then maps these wants to engineering features such as frame material and tire width, scoring each link as strong, medium, or weak. Which tool organizes this work?", "options": {"A": "A process map that shows the steps used to build each bicycle frame.", "B": "A control plan that lists the checks made during frame assembly.", "C": "A house of quality that links customer wants to engineering features.", "D": "A Pareto chart that ranks the customer wants by how often they appear."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A house of quality that links customer wants to engineering features): The house of quality is the main matrix of quality function deployment, and it maps what customers want to measurable engineering features with strong, medium, or weak links. A process map shows the steps of a process rather than wants and features, and a Pareto chart ranks categories by frequency. A control plan lists the checks and reactions used to hold a process in place after an improvement. Because the team is scoring links between wants and features, the house of quality is the right tool."}, {"id": 319, "domain": "define", "question": "A project has four phases: define, measure, analyze, and improve. The team needs to see the sequence of tasks, who owns each one, and how the phases overlap in time. The sponsor also wants to know the critical path. Which planning tool fits best?", "options": {"A": "A Gantt chart with task owners and bars that show the schedule.", "B": "A Pareto chart that ranks tasks by the time each one takes.", "C": "A control chart that tracks progress against the plan each week.", "D": "A histogram that shows how many tasks fall in each phase."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A Gantt chart with task owners and bars that show the schedule): A Gantt chart lays tasks out on a timeline, shows who owns each task, and displays overlaps between phases, which is what the sponsor asked for. A Pareto chart ranks categories by count or cost, and a histogram shows the shape of a set of measurements. A control chart tracks a measure over time against control limits rather than showing a project schedule. Because the request includes owners, timing, and overlap, the Gantt chart is the fit."}, {"id": 320, "domain": "define", "question": "A team collects sixty customer comments on sticky notes. The notes cover billing, wait times, parking, staff knowledge, and the phone menu. The team wants to group similar comments into themes before choosing what to study. Which tool should the team use first?", "options": {"A": "A tree diagram that breaks each collected comment into smaller cause branches.", "B": "A matrix diagram that scores comments against project goals.", "C": "An affinity diagram that groups related comments into natural themes.", "D": "A process decision program chart that maps risks in the plan."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (An affinity diagram that groups related comments into natural themes): An affinity diagram takes many separate ideas or comments and sorts them into groups that share a theme, which is the first step after a large collection of customer notes. A tree diagram breaks a broad goal into smaller pieces once the themes are known, and a matrix diagram scores items against criteria. A process decision program chart maps what could go wrong in a plan. Because sixty comments need grouping before any choice, the affinity diagram comes first."}, {"id": 324, "domain": "measure", "question": "An auditor must check 200 shipping records from a month of 5,000. The records sit in numbered boxes, and the auditor wants each record to have the same chance of being chosen. A colleague suggests taking the first 200 files from the top box. Which approach meets the goal?", "options": {"A": "Draw a simple random sample from the full list of 5,000 records.", "B": "Take the first 200 records from the top box on the shelf.", "C": "Take 200 records from the box closest to the auditor's desk.", "D": "Ask the shipping clerks to hand over 200 records they think are typical."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Draw a simple random sample from the full list of 5,000 records): A simple random sample gives every record the same chance of being picked, which is the auditor's goal. Taking files from the top box or the nearest box is a convenience sample that reflects one part of the month. Asking clerks for records they think are typical invites personal judgement and bias. Only the random draw from the full list keeps the selection free of those patterns."}, {"id": 326, "domain": "measure", "question": "The table shows five repeated readings of the same reference part from one operator: 10.2, 10.4, 10.1, 10.3, and 10.0. The reference value is 10.2. The engineer wants to know how close the readings sit to the reference. Which measure should the engineer compute?", "options": {"A": "Repeatability, from the spread of readings taken by the single operator.", "B": "Bias, from the difference between the average reading and the reference value.", "C": "Linearity, from how the measurement error grows across the full operating measurement range.", "D": "Stability, from how the average drifts when measured across several days."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Bias, from the difference between the average reading and the reference value): Bias is the gap between the average of many readings and the true or reference value, which is what the engineer wants to judge. The five readings average 10.2, which equals the reference value, so bias is near zero for this setup. Repeatability describes the spread among the readings, and linearity describes how error changes across the range, while stability describes drift over time. The question asks how close the readings sit to the reference, so bias is the measure to compute.", "image": "question_images/q_326.png"}, {"id": 329, "domain": "measure", "question": "A quality engineer models two counts. One counts the number of defective parts in a sample of 100, where each part is either good or bad. The other counts the number of scratches on a painted panel, with no fixed number of chances. Which pair of distributions fits these counts?", "options": {"A": "Normal for the defective parts, and binomial for the scratches on the panel.", "B": "Poisson for the defective parts, and normal for the scratches on the panel.", "C": "Binomial for the defective parts, and Poisson for the scratches on the panel.", "D": "Exponential for the defective parts, and binomial for the scratches on the panel."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Binomial for the defective parts, and Poisson for the scratches on the panel): The binomial distribution fits a count of successes in a fixed number of trials where each trial is good or bad, which matches 100 parts with a pass or fail result. The Poisson distribution fits counts of events in an area, volume, or time when the number of chances is not fixed, which matches scratches on a panel. The normal distribution models continuous measurements, and the exponential distribution models waiting times. The pairing in C matches each count to its generating process."}, {"id": 330, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A study of 30 stores finds a correlation of minus 0.82 between the number of staff on the floor and the number of customer complaints. A manager plans to cut staffing further to reduce complaints. What should the Black Belt say about the finding?", "options": {"A": "The link is strong and negative, but staffing levels may not cause the complaints.", "B": "The link is weak, so the manager should ignore complaints data in future reviews.", "C": "The link is strong and positive, so adding more staff would raise the complaint counts.", "D": "The link proves that staffing cuts will reduce complaints by about 82 percent."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The link is strong and negative, but staffing levels may not cause the complaints): A correlation of minus 0.82 is strong and negative, meaning stores with more staff tend to have fewer complaints. Correlation alone cannot show that one thing causes the other, because store size, service model, or customer mix could drive both values. Cutting staff further might remove the people who resolve complaints, which could raise the count instead of lowering it. The manager needs a designed test or more evidence before acting on the link."}, {"id": 331, "domain": "analyze", "question": "The scatter diagram plots line speed against fill weight for 12 runs. The points rise steadily from left to right with little scatter around a straight line. A team member says the chart proves that faster lines cause heavier fills. What is the best reply?", "options": {"A": "The chart proves the claim, since the points follow a straight rising line.", "B": "The chart shows a strong positive link, and a designed experiment would confirm cause.", "C": "The chart shows no relationship, since the points scatter widely around the fitted line.", "D": "The chart shows a negative link, so faster lines would give lighter fills."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The chart shows a strong positive link, and a designed experiment would confirm cause): The points rise steadily with little scatter, which shows a strong positive association between line speed and fill weight. Association is not proof of cause, because a third factor such as product temperature could move both values, and only a designed experiment can separate the effect. Option A treats the pattern as proof, C claims there is no relationship, and D reverses the direction of the link. The careful reading is a strong link that still needs a designed test.", "image": "question_images/q_331.png"}, {"id": 332, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A team tests whether a new seal reduces leak rate. The null hypothesis says the leak rates are equal. The test returns a p value of 0.03 against an alpha of 0.05. The team leader asks what the result means. Which statement is correct?", "options": {"A": "There is a 3 percent chance that the null hypothesis is true for this process.", "B": "The new seal is proven to cut the leak rate by exactly 3 percent.", "C": "The result is significant at 0.05, so the team rejects the null hypothesis.", "D": "The result is not significant, since 0.03 sits below the 0.05 alpha."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The result is significant at 0.05, so the team rejects the null hypothesis): The p value is the chance of seeing data at least this extreme if the null hypothesis were true, and 0.03 is below the 0.05 alpha, so the team rejects the null. A p value of 0.03 does not mean there is a 3 percent chance that the null is true, which is a common misreading. It also does not prove that the seal cuts leaks by exactly 3 percent, because the size of the effect comes from the data rather than the p value. Option D reverses the comparison rule."}, {"id": 333, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A plant inspects 500 units per day. A test flags a supplier batch as out of spec when the batch is fine, and the team ships it back. A second test misses a bad batch and the batch goes to production. Which error is the missed bad batch?", "options": {"A": "A type I error, since the test did not catch the problem in time.", "B": "A sampling error, since the sample did not represent the whole batch.", "C": "An alpha error, since the team set the significance level too high.", "D": "A type II error, since the test failed to detect a real problem."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A type II error, since the test failed to detect a real problem): A type II error, also called beta, happens when a test fails to detect a real difference and a bad batch passes. A type I error, also called alpha, is the opposite case where a good batch is wrongly rejected, which is the first test in the stem. Alpha and beta are the probabilities of those two errors rather than the errors themselves, so C uses loose wording. Because the bad batch was missed, the second case is the type II error."}, {"id": 336, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A machine stops several times a week. The team's first fix replaces a fuse each time. A five whys session asks why the fuse blows, why the motor overheats, why the cooling fan stalls, and why the fan belt slips. What does the session reveal?", "options": {"A": "The fuse is the root cause, since it fails before the machine stops.", "B": "Each answer is a separate root cause that the team must fix on its own.", "C": "The slipping belt is the root cause, and the fuse is only a symptom.", "D": "The session found no root cause, since the machine stops for several reasons."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The slipping belt is the root cause, and the fuse is only a symptom): Root cause analysis keeps asking why until it reaches the condition that, once fixed, stops the problem from returning. In this chain the fuse blows because the motor overheats, the motor overheats because the fan stalls, and the fan stalls because the belt slips, so the belt is the root cause. The fuse failing is a symptom that repeats because the underlying condition remains. Option A stops at the symptom, and B treats each answer as a separate cause."}, {"id": 337, "domain": "improve", "question": "A two factor experiment tests temperature and pressure, each at two levels, with all four combinations run twice. Temperature shows a main effect of 6 degrees, pressure shows 1 degree, and the interaction is small. The team reviews the results before setting the next test. What should the team conclude?", "options": {"A": "Pressure is the key factor, since it was varied along with temperature.", "B": "Neither factor matters much, since the interaction turned out to be small.", "C": "The team should test more levels of pressure before reading the effects.", "D": "Temperature drives the response, so the team should focus on that factor."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Temperature drives the response, so the team should focus on that factor): A main effect is the change in the average response when one factor moves from its low level to its high level, averaged over the levels of the other factor. Temperature shows a 6 degree shift while pressure shows 1 degree, so temperature has the larger effect on the response. A small interaction means the effect of temperature holds at both pressure levels, which supports focusing on temperature. Options A, B, and C ignore the size of the two main effects."}, {"id": 340, "domain": "improve", "question": "A clinic gets wrong patient wristbands because two similar name labels sit side by side on a printer tray. The team redesigns the tray so only one label stock fits, and the printer cannot feed the wrong sheet. Which lean concept does this change illustrate?", "options": {"A": "Standard work, since operators follow a written sequence of steps.", "B": "Heijunka, since the workload is leveled across the week.", "C": "Mistake proofing, since the design blocks the wrong label.", "D": "Andon, since a signal tells the team when a problem appears."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Mistake proofing, since the design blocks the wrong label): Mistake proofing, also called poka yoke, changes the process so the wrong action is hard to perform. Making the tray fit one label stock removes the chance of feeding the wrong sheet, which is a classic mechanical device. Standard work documents the best sequence but does not stop the wrong label, and an andon is a signal that a problem has already appeared. Leveling the workload is a different lean idea, so B does not fit."}, {"id": 344, "domain": "control", "question": "A billing team counts the number of incorrect invoices in each daily batch of 200. Batch sizes stay constant from day to day, and the team wants to track the count of defective invoices per batch. The analyst at the meeting lists several chart types. Which control chart fits this data?", "options": {"A": "An individuals chart, since each day gives a single count of bad invoices.", "B": "A c chart, since it tracks the count of defects across a constant area of inspection.", "C": "An np chart, since it tracks the number of defective units per constant sample.", "D": "A u chart, since it tracks defects per unit when sample size varies."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (An np chart, since it tracks the number of defective units per constant sample): The np chart plots the number of defective units in a sample, and it needs a constant sample size, which the daily batch of 200 provides. A p chart tracks the proportion defective, a c chart tracks the count of defects in a constant area or unit, and a u chart tracks defects per unit when the sample varies. Option A describes an individuals chart, which fits continuous measurements over time. Because the team counts defective invoices in equal batches, the np chart is the match."}, {"id": 350, "domain": "design_for_six_sigma", "question": "A design team sets a target of 50 newtons for a spring clip. Parts vary in wire thickness and heat treatment, and the clip must hold its force across both sources of variation. The team must choose an approach before the first prototype is built. Which design approach meets this need?", "options": {"A": "Tolerance design, which sets the target at the nominal value and tightens the allowed limits.", "B": "Robust design, which makes the design less sensitive to the sources of variation.", "C": "Design for test, which adds test points so failures can be found later.", "D": "Design for maintainability, which shortens the time needed for routine repair work."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Robust design, which makes the design less sensitive to the sources of variation): Robust design sets the design so that normal variation in inputs, such as wire thickness and heat treatment, has less effect on the output force. Tolerance design tightens the allowed range of each input instead, which usually raises cost. Design for test adds test points, and design for maintainability shortens repair time, so neither one addresses sensitivity to input variation. Because the goal is a design that holds its target across the two sources, robust design is the fit."}, {"id": 353, "domain": "organization_wide_planning_and_deployment", "question": "A software company has trained twenty Green Belts, but two years later only one improvement project has finished. An internal review finds that managers keep pulling team members onto urgent releases. Which root cause of deployment failure does this pattern describe?", "options": {"A": "An overuse of control charts on processes that rarely vary.", "B": "A choice to run projects without any Green Belt training.", "C": "A lack of management support that leaves teams without real resources.", "D": "A project portfolio that holds too many small process improvement ideas at once."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A lack of management support that leaves teams without real resources): The body of knowledge names weak management support and missing resources as common causes of Six Sigma failure, and taking trained people away for urgent work removes the very capacity the program was built on. The training already exists, so a missing-training cause does not fit, and neither an overuse of control charts nor a broad idea portfolio explains why finished projects are so few."}, {"id": 357, "domain": "organizational_process_management_and_measures", "question": "A Black Belt proposes a project that will cut scrap by $180,000 a year. A manager asks whether the savings are hard or soft. The plant finance lead confirms that scrap cost appears directly on the profit and loss statement. How should the Black Belt classify the benefit?", "options": {"A": "A soft benefit, because the savings depend on future scrap volume.", "B": "A soft benefit, because scrap is an internal measure of quality.", "C": "A soft benefit, because the savings cannot be traced to the profit statement.", "D": "A hard benefit, because the scrap cost sits on the profit statement."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A hard benefit, because the scrap cost sits on the profit statement): A hard benefit is one that can be traced to the profit and loss statement and verified by finance, and scrap cost recorded there meets that test. Volume uncertainty does not turn a recorded cost into a soft benefit, and scrap is not merely an internal score. The scrap cost already sits on the profit statement, so the option that calls the saving untraceable to that statement contradicts the facts."}, {"id": 364, "domain": "define", "question": "A hospital wants to shorten the time from discharge order to patient leaving the unit. The Black Belt maps everyone who touches that step. Leaders ask which group counts as an internal customer of the discharge process. Which answer is most sound?", "options": {"A": "The patient who is waiting for the discharge paperwork to arrive.", "B": "The insurer that pays the hospital for the completed stay.", "C": "The transport aide who receives the patient after the order.", "D": "The regulator that inspects the hospital discharge records."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The transport aide who receives the patient after the order): An internal customer is the next step or the next person inside the organization who receives the output of the process, so the transport aide who takes over once the order is written fits that definition. The patient, the insurer, and the regulator are all affected by the process, but they sit outside the organization and are external customers."}, {"id": 365, "domain": "define", "question": "A software team wants to learn why users abandon a checkout screen. The product owner suggests a short online survey sent to everyone who left the page. The Black Belt supports the survey but asks for a second method. Which method best adds depth?", "options": {"A": "A focus group of eight users who abandoned the same screen.", "B": "A larger survey sent to every user of the whole site.", "C": "A review of the click logs for the entire checkout page.", "D": "A benchmark of the checkout screens that are used by three rival retailers."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A focus group of eight users who abandoned the same screen): The survey can show how many users left and what they say, but a focus group lets the team ask follow-up questions and hear the reasons behind the behavior, which adds the depth the Black Belt is asking for. A larger survey adds more of the same responses, click logs show where users stopped rather than why, and a rival benchmark studies another company instead of these users."}, {"id": 368, "domain": "define", "question": "A team is chartered to cut late deliveries from one warehouse. During a review meeting, a member suggests adding a second warehouse and a new inventory system to the project. Which response best follows good project scope practice at this stage?", "options": {"A": "Accept the ideas, since a wider scope makes the project more valuable.", "B": "Add the ideas and ask the sponsor for a longer project timeline.", "C": "Park the ideas in a separate list and keep the current scope.", "D": "Drop the original goal and pursue the largest of the new ideas."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Park the ideas in a separate list and keep the current scope): A charter defines the boundary of the project, and widening it midstream puts the original goal and the timeline at risk, so good practice is to record the new ideas for later review and finish what was agreed. Quietly expanding, extending the timeline, or abandoning the original goal all break the scope discipline that makes the project measurable."}, {"id": 371, "domain": "measure", "question": "A bank audits loan files each month. To build the sample, the auditor opens the sorted customer list, picks a random starting point, and then takes every twentieth file on the list. Which sampling method does this describe for the monthly audit?", "options": {"A": "Stratified sampling, because the list is sorted by customer type.", "B": "Systematic sampling, because a fixed interval selects the files.", "C": "Cluster sampling, because whole groups of files are chosen together.", "D": "Simple random sampling, because the starting point is random."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Systematic sampling, because a fixed interval selects the files): Systematic sampling picks a random start and then takes every kth item from the list, which is exactly the pattern described. Stratified sampling divides the population into groups first, cluster sampling selects whole groups, and simple random sampling gives every file an equal chance with no fixed interval, so the interval is the clue that names the method."}, {"id": 372, "domain": "measure", "question": "A histogram of fill weights shows a mound centered at about 99.2 grams, with readings spread from about 95 to 103 grams. The lower specification limit is 97 and the upper is 103. Which first action should the Black Belt suggest for this process?", "options": {"A": "Widen the specification limits so that the lower tail falls inside them again.", "B": "Shift the mean up a little so the lower tail clears the limit.", "C": "Reduce the process mean so the chart looks more symmetric.", "D": "Take no action, since the mean already sits near the target."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Shift the mean up a little so the lower tail clears the limit): The histogram mound sits at about 99.2 grams, below the 100 gram target, and the bin counts show 6 readings between 95 and 97, so part of the lower tail is already below the 97 limit. The upper side has room, because no readings reach above 103. Moving the mean up a little uses that room and cuts the risk at the low end, while widening the limits hides the problem, reducing the mean pushes more weight toward the limit, and taking no action leaves the low tail where it is.", "image": "question_images/q_372.png"}, {"id": 376, "domain": "measure", "question": "The box plot compares hold time in minutes for the day and night shifts. The day shift median is 8 minutes, the night shift median is 15 minutes, and the whole night box sits above the day box. What does the chart show?", "options": {"A": "The two shifts have the same spread and share the same center value.", "B": "Day shift has more outliers than the night shift does.", "C": "Night shift hold times run higher than day shift hold times.", "D": "The night shift box is wider than the day shift box."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Night shift hold times run higher than day shift hold times): The night shift median of 15 minutes sits well above the day shift median of 8 minutes, and the whole night box sits above the day box, so the night shift runs higher on this measure. The boxes do not share a center or a spread, no outliers are plotted for either shift, and the day box, drawn from 5 to 10 minutes, is wider than the night box, drawn from 13 to 17 minutes.", "image": "question_images/q_376.png"}, {"id": 378, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A Black Belt computes a correlation of 0.82 between training hours and the units produced per operator. A manager says the training clearly caused the higher output. Which response best describes what the correlation statistic actually supports in this study?", "options": {"A": "Training caused the gain, since the correlation is strong.", "B": "A strong association exists, but causation needs more evidence.", "C": "The two variables are unrelated, since the correlation falls short of 1.0.", "D": "The correlation proves the direction of cause and effect."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A strong association exists, but causation needs more evidence): A correlation of 0.82 describes a strong linear association between the two variables, and it does not by itself show that one causes the other. A hidden factor such as experience could drive both. The statement that training caused the gain and the claim that the correlation proves cause both go beyond the statistic, and the claim that the variables are unrelated ignores the strong positive value."}, {"id": 381, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A plant runs the same molding job on three machines and records a key dimension for twelve parts from each one. The team wants to know whether the machine means differ. Which test fits this question and this data set?", "options": {"A": "A one-way ANOVA comparing the three machine means at once.", "B": "A chi-square test of independence on the machine counts.", "C": "A paired t test between the first and the last machine.", "D": "A test of equal proportions that compares the three machine yields together."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A one-way ANOVA comparing the three machine means at once): One-way analysis of variance compares the means of three or more groups on one continuous response, which matches three machines and a measured dimension. A chi-square test works on counts or categories, a paired t test compares two related groups on a continuous measure, and a test of proportions needs a proportion as the response, so none of those three fits this data."}, {"id": 389, "domain": "improve", "question": "The value stream map traces a loan application through five steps. Value-added work totals 22 minutes, and waiting and rework add 158 minutes, for a total lead time of 180 minutes. Which conclusion follows from the map for the team?", "options": {"A": "Value-added time dominates the lead time, so the work steps are slow.", "B": "Value-added time is a small share, so wait time is the main target.", "C": "The five steps are balanced, so no single step is worth improving.", "D": "The lead time is already close to the value-added time for the process."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Value-added time is a small share, so wait time is the main target): Value-added work totals 22 minutes out of a 180 minute lead time, which is about 12 percent, so the waiting and rework that make up the other 158 minutes dominate the process. That makes the delay between steps the largest opportunity. Value-added time does not dominate, the steps are not balanced, and the lead time is far larger than the value-added time.", "image": "question_images/q_389.png"}, {"id": 398, "domain": "control", "question": "A team reduces a shaft diameter spread so that the process sigma is now one third of its old value. The Black Belt asks the team to repeat the gauge study before closing the project. Which reason best supports that request?", "options": {"A": "A smaller process spread means the gauge must resolve finer differences.", "B": "The gauge study stays valid because the parts and operators did not change.", "C": "The gauge is likely to wear out faster on a tighter process range.", "D": "The original gauge study was probably biased by the old operators."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A smaller process spread means the gauge must resolve finer differences): As a process improves, its spread shrinks, and a gauge that was good enough for the old spread may be too coarse to resolve the new one, so the study is repeated to confirm the measurement system still suits the job. The old study cannot simply be reused, gauge wear does not explain the request, and there is no reason to assume the original study was biased."}, {"id": 400, "domain": "design_for_six_sigma", "question": "A design team sets each part tolerance so that every part at its worst allowed size still fits in the assembly. The method guarantees a fit, but the tolerances are costly. Which alternative design approach should the Black Belt describe to the team?", "options": {"A": "Worst-case tolerancing, which adds every part at its extreme limit.", "B": "Design for test, which adds inspection points to the assembly line.", "C": "Design for maintainability, which eases access to the parts during routine service work.", "D": "Statistical tolerancing, which combines part distributions instead of worst cases."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Statistical tolerancing, which combines part distributions instead of worst cases): The scenario describes worst case tolerancing, where every part is assumed to sit at its extreme limit at the same time, which guarantees a fit but forces tight and costly tolerances. Statistical tolerancing uses the distributions of the part dimensions and the way they combine, so the same fit can often be held with looser individual tolerances. Worst case tolerancing is the current method, and design for test and design for maintainability address other design goals."}, {"id": 406, "domain": "organizational_process_management_and_measures", "question": "A plant must ship 240 units during a 480 minute shift to meet customer demand. The line currently produces one unit every three minutes. The Black Belt is asked to compare the line with takt time. What does the comparison show?", "options": {"A": "Takt time is three minutes, so the line matches customer demand exactly.", "B": "Takt time is two minutes, and the line runs faster than demand requires.", "C": "Takt time is two minutes, so the line is too slow to meet demand.", "D": "Takt time is one minute, so the line must double its current output rate."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Takt time is two minutes, so the line is too slow to meet demand): Takt time is the available work time divided by customer demand, so 480 minutes divided by 240 units equals 2.0 minutes per unit. The line takes 3 minutes per unit, which is longer than the 2 minute takt, so it falls behind demand and cannot ship 240 units in the shift without overtime or a faster rate. A takt of three minutes would match the current line, but it would not match the demand."}, {"id": 412, "domain": "team_management", "question": "A new cross-functional team is about to begin, and the Black Belt wants to head off side conversations, late starts, and unclear assignments before they become habits. The team will meet weekly for four months. Which step should the Black Belt take first?", "options": {"A": "Invite the sponsor to every meeting so the team stays focused on the goal.", "B": "Have the team agree on ground rules for meetings, roles, and commitments.", "C": "Assign each member a topic to present so everyone has a speaking part.", "D": "Wait until the problems appear so the rules reflect real team behavior."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Have the team agree on ground rules for meetings, roles, and commitments): Ground rules are set at the start so the team writes its own expectations for attendance, start times, participation, and follow through, which is much easier than correcting habits months later. The team also confirms roles and assignments in that same session. Waiting for problems, adding a sponsor to every meeting, or handing out presentation topics does not build the shared expectations the team needs."}, {"id": 415, "domain": "define", "question": "A hotel chain asks guests about a new feature. Guests say they would not be unhappy if it were missing, but they are delighted when it is present. The feature adds value only when it is there. Which Kano category does this feature represent?", "options": {"A": "Attractive, because guests are delighted when it is present and do not miss it.", "B": "Must-be, because guests expect the feature and notice it only when it fails.", "C": "One-dimensional, because satisfaction rises in step with how much of the feature is delivered.", "D": "Indifferent, because guests show no reaction whether the feature is present or absent."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Attractive, because guests are delighted when it is present and do not miss it): An attractive quality element creates satisfaction when it is present, but its absence causes no dissatisfaction, which matches what the guests described. A must-be element does the opposite and is noticed only when it is missing, a one-dimensional element lifts satisfaction in proportion to how much is delivered, and an indifferent element draws no reaction either way. Attractive features are useful differentiators, though they can fade into expectations over time."}, {"id": 425, "domain": "measure", "question": "A warehouse measures the time orders wait before picking, by shift. The box plot shows day shift times with a median of 8 minutes and a high of 13. Night shift times have a median of 10 minutes, a high of 20, and one plotted point at 26. What should the Black Belt conclude?", "options": {"A": "The two shifts perform the same, since both medians fall near 10 minutes.", "B": "The night shift is faster, because it handles the larger order volumes during each hour.", "C": "The night shift runs slower and higher overall, with one unusually long wait.", "D": "The day shift needs attention, because its times spread over a wider band."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The night shift runs slower and higher overall, with one unusually long wait): The night shift median is 10 minutes against 8 on days, its upper quartile and high are both larger, and the point at 26 minutes sits beyond the whisker as an outlier. Taken together, the night shift waits run longer and show more spread. The medians are not the same, the day shift is the tighter of the two, and nothing in the plot shows order volumes.", "image": "question_images/q_425.png"}, {"id": 426, "domain": "measure", "question": "A process has a Cp of 1.60 and a Cpk of 0.95. The Black Belt is asked what the two numbers say together about the process. Which statement correctly compares the two capability indices and explains the gap in this case?", "options": {"A": "The process spread fits the limits, and the process is also centered well.", "B": "The process spread is too wide, and the center sits on the target value.", "C": "Both indices agree, so the process is capable at the current setting.", "D": "The spread could fit the limits, but the process center is off target."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The spread could fit the limits, but the process center is off target): Cp compares the specification width with the process spread and ignores the center, so a Cp of 1.60 says the spread is narrow enough. Cpk also accounts for how far the mean sits from the nearer limit, and its much lower value of 0.95 shows the process is off center. The two indices disagree, so the fix is to move the mean toward the target rather than to widen the limits."}, {"id": 427, "domain": "measure", "question": "A plant produces 500 units in a run. Inspection finds 40 units with defects, and 15 of those units each contain two defects while the other 25 contain one. What is the defects per unit value for this production run?", "options": {"A": "0.11, because 55 total defects divided by 500 units is 0.11.", "B": "0.08, because 40 defective units divided by 500 units is 0.08.", "C": "1.11, because 500 units divided by 450 good units is about 1.11.", "D": "9.09, because 500 units divided by 55 defects reverses the defects per unit ratio."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (0.11, because 55 total defects divided by 500 units is 0.11): Start by counting all defects, not just the defective units. The 15 units with two defects contribute 30 defects and the 25 units with one defect contribute 25, giving 55 defects in total. Defects per unit is 55 divided by 500, which equals 0.11. The 0.08 figure counts units rather than defects, and the other two options divide in the wrong direction."}, {"id": 431, "domain": "analyze", "question": "An FMEA team scores a failure mode with severity 8, occurrence 5, and detection 4. The team is considering an action that would cut detection to 2 and leave the other two scores unchanged. What would the new risk priority number be?", "options": {"A": "80, because 8 times 5 times 2 equals 80 when multiplied out.", "B": "40, because severity times occurrence leaves the detection score out of the product.", "C": "160, because the original scores stay the same after the action.", "D": "15, because the three new scores are added together instead of multiplied."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (80, because 8 times 5 times 2 equals 80 when multiplied out): The risk priority number is severity times occurrence times detection, so the original score is 8 times 5 times 4, which equals 160. After the action, detection drops to 2, so the new score is 8 times 5 times 2, which equals 80, a cut of half. The other options either leave detection out of the product, keep the old detection score, or add the three scores instead of multiplying them."}, {"id": 436, "domain": "improve", "question": "An engineer wants to study how oven temperature and bake time affect the hardness of cookies. Temperature will be tested at two settings and bake time at three settings. The engineer plans a full factorial experiment to see how they interact. In DOE terms, what are temperature and hardness called?", "options": {"A": "Temperature is a factor, and hardness is the response variable.", "B": "Temperature is a level, and hardness is a treatment condition in the design.", "C": "Temperature is the response, and hardness is an independent factor in the study.", "D": "Temperature is a factor, and hardness is a blocking variable in the design."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Temperature is a factor, and hardness is the response variable): In a design of experiments, the factors are the inputs the team sets or changes, such as temperature and bake time, and the response is the output that is measured, which here is cookie hardness. Factor levels are the specific settings, so two temperature settings and three time settings give six treatment combinations in a full factorial. Hardness is not a factor or a block, and temperature is not a level."}, {"id": 441, "domain": "improve", "question": "A plant finds that the paint booth limits output to 300 units a day while the assembly line can produce 500. The plant manager proposes buying a second assembly line. What should the Black Belt advise, based on the theory of constraints?", "options": {"A": "Buy the second assembly line, because added capacity lowers the unit cost.", "B": "Slow the assembly line to 300 units a day so the two areas match.", "C": "Move operators from the paint booth to the assembly line to balance the load.", "D": "Improve the paint booth first, since it sets the output of the whole plant."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Improve the paint booth first, since it sets the output of the whole plant): In the theory of constraints, the slowest step sets the throughput of the entire system, so the paint booth at 300 units a day controls plant output. Adding capacity anywhere else, including a second assembly line, will not raise output because the constraint has not moved. Deliberately slowing a non constraint lowers throughput, and shifting people away from the constraint can make it worse."}, {"id": 446, "domain": "control", "question": "A project team has improved a welding process and is handing it over to the area supervisor. The Black Belt wants the gains to hold after the team moves on to other work. Which document should transfer the controls to the process owner?", "options": {"A": "The project charter, because it names the team and the sponsor.", "B": "The lessons learned file, because it records everything the team did during the project.", "C": "The value stream map, because it shows where the delays were.", "D": "The control plan, because it lists the measures, limits, and responses."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The control plan, because it lists the measures, limits, and responses): A control plan names each characteristic to monitor, the method and frequency, who checks it, and what to do when a limit is crossed, so it carries the day to day controls to the process owner. The charter starts the project, the lessons learned file captures history, and the value stream map describes the process flow. None of those three tells the supervisor what to watch and how to react."}, {"id": 453, "domain": "organization_wide_planning_and_deployment", "question": "A hospital system shortens its room cleaning cycle so beds turn over sooner. Within a month the emergency department holds admitted patients longer, and the transport team falls behind on scheduled runs. What does this outcome best illustrate about the relationships among business systems and processes?", "options": {"A": "The cleaning process was monitored with the wrong sampling plan.", "B": "Faster room turnover increases the risk of an infection control failure.", "C": "An improvement in one process shifted demand onto linked processes.", "D": "The transport team should have been included in the project charter."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (An improvement in one process shifted demand onto linked processes.): Processes inside one system share inputs, people, and capacity, so a change in one place moves load somewhere else. Faster room turnover pushed work onto the emergency department and the transport team, which shows that the project boundary was narrower than the system it touched. Options A and D describe measurement or charter choices rather than the system effect, and option B raises a risk that the story does not show."}, {"id": 454, "domain": "organization_wide_planning_and_deployment", "question": "A Black Belt closes a project that cut claim rework by half. At the closeout review a finance analyst agrees the improvement is real but reports that the department budget is unchanged. What role does the finance function play in validating project results?", "options": {"A": "Finance decides whether the project team may actually close the project.", "B": "Finance sets the new control limits for the claim charts.", "C": "Finance takes over ownership of the improved claim process.", "D": "Finance confirms that reported benefits are real and traceable."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Finance confirms that reported benefits are real and traceable.): Finance validates the money side of a project by checking that claimed savings match the accounts, that the baseline was fair, and that the benefit can be traced to a line in the records. A benefit that is real but not yet visible in a budget is still a valid saving when the trail is documented. Options A, B, and C hand finance duties that belong to the sponsor, the belt, or the process owner."}, {"id": 457, "domain": "organizational_process_management_and_measures", "question": "A utility company wants one page that shows financial results, customer satisfaction, internal process performance, and employee learning together. The Black Belt recommends a balanced scorecard instead of a page of financial measures alone. What is the main benefit of that view?", "options": {"A": "It shows how movement in one perspective affects the others.", "B": "It replaces financial measures with customer and employee data.", "C": "It ranks departments so leaders can allocate bonuses by score.", "D": "It keeps the four measure sets separate for independent owners."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It shows how movement in one perspective affects the others.): A balanced scorecard sets financial, customer, process, and learning measures side by side so leaders can see how a change in one area travels into the others. That line of sight is the reason to keep the four views on one page. Option B removes financial measures rather than balancing them, option C turns the scorecard into a ranking device, and option D separates the views that the scorecard exists to connect."}, {"id": 458, "domain": "organizational_process_management_and_measures", "question": "A Black Belt compares two projects that each cost 80,000 dollars today, using an eight percent discount rate. Project A returns 30,000 dollars a year for four years, while Project B returns 130,000 dollars in one payment at the end of year four. Which measure separates the two options properly?", "options": {"A": "Total cash returned, because Project B pays back more dollars overall.", "B": "Simple payback, because both projects recover their cost within three years.", "C": "Return on investment, because it compares total profit with the original cost.", "D": "Net present value, because it discounts each payment by when it arrives."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Net present value, because it discounts each payment by when it arrives.): NPV converts every future payment into today's dollars, so a large payment in year four counts for less than the same money spread across earlier years. At eight percent the four year annuity factor is 3.31213, so Project A is worth 30,000 times 3.31213, or 99,364 dollars, and nets 19,364 after the 80,000 cost. Project B is worth 130,000 divided by 1.08 to the fourth power, and 1.08 to the fourth power is 1.36049, so the present value is 95,554 and the net is 15,554. Project B returns more total dollars, 130,000 against 120,000, and a higher return on investment, so only the discounted measure shows that the earlier cash flow from Project A is worth more. Simple payback does not separate the two on the terms option B gives either, because Project B pays nothing back until the end of year four."}, {"id": 463, "domain": "team_management", "question": "A Black Belt must keep one warehouse project visible to three audiences as the work moves from define into control. Executives want monthly results, operators need daily process changes, and finance analysts verify the claimed benefits. What should the communication plan specify for each group?", "options": {"A": "The message, the medium, and how often each group is updated.", "B": "The same weekly email for all three groups, since one channel is simpler.", "C": "A single steering committee meeting that every group attends monthly.", "D": "The final project report sent once, after the closeout review finishes."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The message, the medium, and how often each group is updated.): A communication plan states who needs to hear what, in which form, and how often, and the answers change with the audience. Executives want a periodic summary, operators need daily detail at the process, and finance needs evidence tied to the accounts. Option B gives every group the same channel, option C replaces the plan with one meeting, and option D leaves everyone without information until the project ends."}, {"id": 466, "domain": "define", "question": "A team plans to install a new labeling system in three weeks. Several steps depend on a vendor shipment, and one test may fail if the room temperature drifts. The sponsor wants a tool that shows the plan and what the team will do if something goes wrong. Which tool fits?", "options": {"A": "A process decision program chart, which pairs each step with failure responses.", "B": "An activity network diagram, which shows task order and the critical path.", "C": "A work breakdown structure, which lists every deliverable that the project must produce.", "D": "A Gantt chart, which shows each task against a calendar timeline."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A process decision program chart, which pairs each step with failure responses.): The process decision program chart takes a plan and asks what could go wrong at each step, then records a countermeasure beside each possible failure. That structure fits a short installation with a vendor dependency and a test that can fail. The activity network diagram sequences tasks, the work breakdown structure lists deliverables, and the Gantt chart places tasks on a calendar, so none of them carries the failure response pairs."}, {"id": 469, "domain": "define", "question": "A project has run for four months. The market shifted, the sponsor changed roles, and the team has added two new measures to the dashboard. The Black Belt asks for a formal meeting to revisit the charter with the stakeholders. Why is that meeting needed?", "options": {"A": "To close the project early when the sponsor leaves the organization.", "B": "To confirm the goal, scope, and measures still fit the current situation.", "C": "To reassign the project to a different belt who reports to the new sponsor.", "D": "To raise the savings target now that the team has time to spare."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (To confirm the goal, scope, and measures still fit the current situation.): A charter is a living agreement, so periodic reviews check that the problem, the goal, the boundaries, and the measures still match the business and the sponsor. A shift in the market or a change of sponsor is a normal trigger for that review. Options A, C, and D change the project for reasons the story does not support."}, {"id": 471, "domain": "measure", "question": "A claims center builds its capacity plan from the published routing of six steps and reports that each adjuster handles 24 claims a day. A Black Belt follows one claim end to end and counts two re-inspection loops the routing does not show. What does that finding mean for the capacity plan?", "options": {"A": "The plan understates capacity, because the rework steps add extra headcount to the plant floor.", "B": "The plan is unaffected, because rework happens only when an error escapes.", "C": "The plan is accurate, because capacity is counted in claims rather than steps.", "D": "The plan overstates capacity, because each claim needs more work than the routing shows."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The plan overstates capacity, because each claim needs more work than the routing shows.): The published routing describes the standard path, and the re-inspection loops are hidden work that consumes the same adjusters a second time. A plan built on the published path counts claims that the process cannot really finish in the stated hours. Options A and C treat the extra loops as harmless, and option B assumes rework is rare when the belt just watched two loops on one claim."}, {"id": 480, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A market analyst has answers to eleven survey questions from 400 customers and wants to know which questions group together as underlying factors. A second task requires sorting stores into two known performance bands from the same kinds of data. Which pair of multivariate tools fits these two jobs?", "options": {"A": "Factor analysis for sorting stores, and discriminant analysis for grouping questions.", "B": "Factor analysis for grouping questions, and discriminant analysis for sorting stores.", "C": "Multiple regression for grouping questions, and analysis of variance for sorting stores.", "D": "Analysis of variance for grouping questions, and a chi square test for sorting stores."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Factor analysis for grouping questions, and discriminant analysis for sorting stores.): Factor analysis looks for the underlying factors that make a set of variables move together, which fits the eleven survey questions. Discriminant analysis sorts cases into known groups from a set of measures, which fits placing stores into two known performance bands. Option A swaps the two tools, and options C and D name tools that answer different questions."}, {"id": 486, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A hospital improvement team lists the risks to a new medication reconciliation process before the pilot starts. One entry reads that a single supplier provides the only barcode scanner model that works with the hospital labeling system. Which risk type does this entry describe?", "options": {"A": "Security risk, because the scanners store patient medication records every day.", "B": "Product risk, because the scanners may fail during the pilot period.", "C": "Project risk, because the pilot schedule depends on delivery of the scanners.", "D": "Supplier risk, because one vendor is the only source for a needed item."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Supplier risk, because one vendor is the only source for a needed item.): Supplier risk covers incoming materials and services, and a single source item is the classic example, because a delay or a quality problem at that one vendor stops the process. Security risk covers protection of people, buildings, and information, product risk covers failure of the offering itself, and project risk covers scope, schedule, cost, and resources. Only the single source condition matches supplier risk."}, {"id": 487, "domain": "improve", "question": "A team must compare four tire formulations, but test vehicles and test routes also affect tread wear. Each formulation can be tested once on each vehicle and once on each route in a single balanced study. Which design fits this situation?", "options": {"A": "A completely randomized design, which assigns every tire by chance.", "B": "A randomized block design with vehicle as the only blocking factor.", "C": "A Latin square, which blocks two nuisance factors at once.", "D": "A two-level fractional factorial with formulation as the factor."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A Latin square, which blocks two nuisance factors at once.): A Latin square blocks two nuisance factors at the same time and gives each treatment one appearance in every row and every column, which is why vehicle and route differences come out before the formulations are compared. A completely randomized design blocks nothing, a randomized block design handles only one blocking factor, and a fractional factorial answers a different question. Only the Latin square meets both blocking needs."}, {"id": 489, "domain": "improve", "question": "The table shows a two factor experiment on coating hardness. Temperature has levels of 150 and 170 degrees, and the catalyst has levels A and B. The four runs read 62.0 for 150 and A, 58.0 for 150 and B, 71.0 for 170 and A, and 68.0 for 170 and B. Which main effect is largest?", "options": {"A": "Catalyst, at plus 9.5 degrees of hardness on average.", "B": "Temperature, at plus 9.5 degrees of hardness on average.", "C": "Temperature, at plus 3.5 degrees of hardness on average.", "D": "Catalyst, at about minus 3.5 degrees of hardness on average."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Temperature, at plus 9.5 degrees of hardness on average.): The average response at 150 degrees is 62.0 plus 58.0 divided by 2, which is 60.0, and the average at 170 degrees is 71.0 plus 68.0 divided by 2, which is 69.5. The temperature effect is 69.5 minus 60.0, which is 9.5. The catalyst averages are 66.5 for level A and 63.0 for level B, so that effect is 63.0 minus 66.5, or minus 3.5. Temperature therefore moves the response by 9.5, which is larger than 3.5, and options A, C, and D either label or size the effects incorrectly.", "image": "question_images/q_489.png"}, {"id": 490, "domain": "improve", "question": "A plant makes three product types on one line. The schedule builds a large batch of each type in turn, so customers of the later types wait while early batches sit finished, and suppliers see a lumpy load. Which lean technique should the team apply to this schedule?", "options": {"A": "Single minute exchange of die, which shortens the changeover between batches.", "B": "Poka-yoke, which prevents a wrong part from reaching the next station.", "C": "Total productive maintenance, which keeps each machine in its best condition.", "D": "Heijunka, which levels the product mix and the volume across the day."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Heijunka, which levels the product mix and the volume across the day.): Heijunka levels production by building a repeating mix in proportion to demand instead of a large batch of each type in turn, which shortens the wait for every product and smooths the load on suppliers. SMED makes frequent changeovers practical, which supports leveling, but it does not level the schedule by itself. Poka-yoke prevents errors and total productive maintenance protects equipment availability, so neither one addresses the batch pattern."}, {"id": 493, "domain": "improve", "question": "A team walks a plant and finds three things: finished pumps stored in a rack for two weeks, operators walking to a far tool crib each hour, and a step where clerks retype data the scanner already captured. Which wastes do these represent?", "options": {"A": "Inventory, transportation, and defects, in the order the findings appear.", "B": "Overproduction, motion, and overprocessing, in the order the findings appear.", "C": "Waiting, motion, and overproduction, in the order the findings appear.", "D": "Inventory, motion, and overprocessing, in the order the findings appear."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Inventory, motion, and overprocessing, in the order the findings appear.): Finished pumps waiting in a rack are inventory because the material sits idle, operators walking to a far tool crib are motion, and clerks retyping data the scanner already captured is overprocessing, since the second entry adds no value. Options A, B, and C each misclassify at least one of the three findings, so they do not match the order in which the wastes appear."}, {"id": 494, "domain": "control", "question": "A team charts the fill volume of a beverage line. One proposal pulls five consecutive bottles from a single filler head each hour and plots the average. A second proposal pulls bottles from all six filler heads into one hourly subgroup. Which proposal fits rational subgrouping?", "options": {"A": "The mixed plan, because a subgroup should carry every source of variation.", "B": "The single head plan, because each subgroup stays inside one set of conditions.", "C": "The mixed plan, because six heads make the subgroup more representative.", "D": "The single head plan, because bottles from one head differ only by special causes."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The single head plan, because each subgroup stays inside one set of conditions.): Rational subgrouping groups units so variation inside a subgroup comes from common causes and differences between subgroups can reveal the change the team wants to catch. Five consecutive bottles from one head share a short period, one machine, and one set of conditions. Mixing six heads into one subgroup hides any difference between the heads, which defeats the purpose of the chart, so options A, C, and D work against the principle. Bottle to bottle differences inside one head's hourly subgroup are common cause variation, and a special cause is what appears as a shift from one subgroup to the next, so option D states the idea backward."}, {"id": 495, "domain": "control", "question": "The control chart plots twelve subgroup averages from a filling line. The center line is 12.0, the upper control limit is 13.0, and the lower control limit is 11.0. Subgroup 9 sits at 13.4, and the other eleven points lie between 11.4 and 12.6. What action does the chart call for?", "options": {"A": "Look for a special cause at subgroup 9 and fix it before recalculating limits.", "B": "Recalculate the control limits without subgroup 9 and continue running the process.", "C": "Widen the control limits so subgroup 9 falls inside the band.", "D": "Adjust the filler each hour because the process is out of control."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Look for a special cause at subgroup 9 and fix it before recalculating limits.): A point beyond a control limit is a signal that something outside the usual system acted on the process, so the first step is to find and remove that special cause. Only after the cause is understood and corrected does the team consider whether the limits should be recomputed. Option B skips the investigation, option C hides the signal, and option D adjusts a process when a single point is the issue.", "image": "question_images/q_495.png"}, {"id": 497, "domain": "control", "question": "A gauge contributes 0.09 millimeters of variation against a process spread of 0.40 millimeters, a share the team accepted. After an improvement, the process spread falls to 0.20 millimeters while the gauge is unchanged. What should the Black Belt do next?", "options": {"A": "Keep the current gauge, because its variation did not change during the project.", "B": "Repeat the measurement study, because the gauge now supplies 45 percent of the spread.", "C": "Widen the specification limits so that the gauge share returns below the usual 30 percent.", "D": "Stop using the gauge and estimate the characteristic by visual inspection instead."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Repeat the measurement study, because the gauge now supplies 45 percent of the spread.): Gauge share is gauge variation divided by total process variation. Before the improvement the share was 0.09 divided by 0.40, which is 22.5 percent, an acceptable figure under the usual guideline. After the improvement the share is 0.09 divided by 0.20, which is 45 percent, above the 30 percent guideline, so the gauge is now the largest single source of variation and real signals hide. Options A, C, and D either keep a gauge that is no longer adequate or change the wrong thing."}, {"id": 503, "domain": "organization_wide_planning_and_deployment", "question": "A distribution center tracks only the dollar value of shipped goods, so leaders cannot see whether quality and customer satisfaction are improving. The Black Belt must suggest a better way to measure progress toward the company strategy. What should the Black Belt recommend?", "options": {"A": "Build a balanced scorecard that links each goal to measures, targets, owners, and review dates", "B": "Track the single financial measure closely and add the other measures once overall profits improve", "C": "Collect many measures every week so no part of the operation escapes attention", "D": "Ask each department to choose its own measures and report them in a monthly summary"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Build a balanced scorecard that links each goal to measures, targets, owners, and review dates): A balanced scorecard ties financial, customer, process, and learning measures to the strategy. It also names a target and an owner for each measure. Watching one financial number hides the drivers of the result. Department measures chosen on their own can conflict, so the scorecard keeps the organization pointed at shared goals."}, {"id": 507, "domain": "organizational_process_management_and_measures", "question": "A hospital compares its medication error rate with published rates from similar hospitals in the same region and finds that it sits above the middle of the group. Leaders want to know how this comparison should be used in the improvement work. What is the correct use of this benchmark data?", "options": {"A": "Use the published rate as the target and copy the top hospital practices without study", "B": "Use the comparison to find a gap, then study the practices behind the best rates", "C": "Treat the comparison as proof that the hospital process is unsafe for every patient", "D": "Wait for the benchmark to be repeated next year before making any process change"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Use the comparison to find a gap, then study the practices behind the best rates): Benchmarking shows where a gap exists and points the team toward organizations that perform better. The next step is to learn how the better performers achieve their results. Copying another site without study can fail because processes and patients differ. A single comparison cannot prove the process is unsafe, and waiting a year wastes the chance to improve now."}, {"id": 509, "domain": "team_management", "question": "A Black Belt is building a team for a project that spans production, quality, and maintenance. The sponsor wants the project to move quickly and asks whether the team should include a member who openly doubts the project will work. How should the Black Belt respond?", "options": {"A": "Include the doubter, because informed challenge improves decisions and builds later support", "B": "Leave the doubter off the team so meetings stay focused and the schedule holds", "C": "Add the doubter but ask the person to raise concerns only after the project closes", "D": "Ask the doubter to join a separate oversight committee that reviews the decisions each quarter"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Include the doubter, because informed challenge improves decisions and builds later support): A skilled skeptic who knows the process can surface risks and assumptions the team would otherwise miss. People who help shape a change also tend to support it later. Leaving the person out removes the challenge but keeps the doubt alive in the wider workforce. Delaying concerns removes their value, and a quarterly side review has no influence on daily decisions."}, {"id": 510, "domain": "team_management", "question": "A plant forms a cross functional team to cut changeover time. It includes an operator, a setup technician, a maintenance planner, and a scheduler. The Black Belt wants one clear statement of what each person will contribute. What should the team produce first?", "options": {"A": "A roles and responsibilities list that states each member's duties and decision rights", "B": "A training plan that teaches every member to perform all four jobs on the line", "C": "A seniority list that shows who will lead each meeting in order of experience", "D": "A detailed budget sheet that divides the project cost among all four participating departments"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A roles and responsibilities list that states each member's duties and decision rights): A roles and responsibilities list tells each member what to do and what they may decide without asking. That prevents gaps and double work. Training everyone in every job may help understanding but does not settle ownership. Seniority does not make a person the right facilitator, and a budget split is a cost matter rather than a team design tool."}, {"id": 511, "domain": "team_management", "question": "Two team members disagree about whether a machine adjustment caused a defect spike. One cites data from the last three shifts, and the other trusts years of experience on the line. The meeting is losing time. What should the Black Belt do next?", "options": {"A": "Choose the member with the most seniority so the discussion can move to solutions", "B": "End the discussion and let the two members settle the question between themselves", "C": "Ask both members to bring evidence and test each claim against the process data", "D": "Record both views in the minutes and let the project sponsor decide at the tollgate"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ask both members to bring evidence and test each claim against the process data): Data resolves disagreements that opinion cannot. Asking each member to bring evidence also keeps both people in the discussion as contributors. Seniority is not evidence about a machine adjustment. Sending the issue to the sponsor delays a decision the team can make with data, and leaving the members alone leaves the question open."}, {"id": 512, "domain": "team_management", "question": "A project team has missed two tollgate dates. Members say they cannot attend the meetings because of daily work, yet they still send helpful written updates to the team. The sponsor wants the project to keep moving. What should the Black Belt recommend?", "options": {"A": "Replace the members with people who have lighter daily workloads and much more free time", "B": "Re-plan the meetings with the sponsor and confirm that members are truly released", "C": "Cancel the tollgate reviews and judge progress only from the written updates", "D": "Add a second weekly meeting so the team can catch up on the missed work"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Re-plan the meetings with the sponsor and confirm that members are truly released): Missed tollgates often mean members were never really freed from daily work. The fix starts with the sponsor resetting priorities and the meeting schedule. Replacing people loses process knowledge and may repeat the same problem. Dropping reviews removes the checkpoints that keep the project honest, and adding meetings does not create time."}, {"id": 513, "domain": "team_management", "question": "A Green Belt must choose between two solutions for a slow discharge process. The team has data on cycle time for both options, but the two senior clinicians on the team disagree about which result matters more. What is the best way to move the team forward?", "options": {"A": "Let the senior clinician with the most years of service make the final choice", "B": "Run both options at the same time on different units and meet again in a year", "C": "Choose the cheaper option and revisit the decision if customer complaints begin to rise", "D": "Agree on the decision criteria and their weights before comparing the two options"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Agree on the decision criteria and their weights before comparing the two options): When experts disagree about results, the team usually lacks agreed decision criteria. Setting the criteria and their weights first turns a debate about opinions into a comparison against shared standards. Seniority does not settle which outcome matters more. Running both options for a year delays care improvement, and choosing on cost alone ignores the performance question."}, {"id": 522, "domain": "measure", "question": "A gauge study reports that repeatability and reproducibility together account for twenty eight percent of the total observed variation in a critical measurement. The team planned to use that gauge for capability work. What should the Black Belt recommend doing next?", "options": {"A": "Use the gauge as it stands because only variation above thirty percent is a problem", "B": "Recheck the gauge once a year and use its readings for decisions in the meantime", "C": "Switch to a more expensive gauge and repeat the whole study after the process stabilizes", "D": "Improve the measurement system first, before trusting its readings for capability work"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Improve the measurement system first, before trusting its readings for capability work): Measurement variation near 30 percent of the total is large against the common rule of thumb. When the gauge consumes that much variation, the team cannot separate process change from measurement noise. Waiting a year leaves the risk in place. Swapping the gauge before finding whether the cause is the gauge, the part, or the operator skips the diagnostic step."}, {"id": 526, "domain": "measure", "question": "A quality engineer checks a filling process with a gauge that is known to give readings one percent low on every part. The team uses the readings to decide whether the process center meets the target. What is the effect of this known gauge error?", "options": {"A": "The readings are noisy, so the team must average more parts to find the center", "B": "The readings are accurate, because a steady offset cancels when many parts are used", "C": "The readings are biased, so the team must correct them before judging the process center", "D": "The readings are unreliable, so the team must replace the gauge before making any judgment"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The readings are biased, so the team must correct them before judging the process center): A reading that runs one percent low on every part is a bias, which means the measurement misses the true value in the same direction each time. Bias shifts the average, so averaging more parts will not remove it. A steady offset does not cancel itself. A correction or known adjustment is needed before the process center can be judged."}, {"id": 528, "domain": "measure", "question": "A team wants a random sample of orders from a warehouse that ships through three shifts. The analyst plans to stand at the dock on Monday morning and take the first ten orders that appear during the first hour. What is the main problem with this plan?", "options": {"A": "The sample is random because the analyst does not pick which orders to include", "B": "The sample is random only if the analyst records every order in a log first", "C": "The sample must be drawn so every order in the week has a chance", "D": "The sample is biased only because Monday orders tend to be smaller than the others"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The sample must be drawn so every order in the week has a chance): A probability sample gives every order in the week a known chance of being picked, which is what makes the result representative. Standing at the dock on Monday morning takes a convenience sample from one place at one time, so it carries whatever pattern that shift and day hold. Leaving the pick to whoever appears first does not make the draw random. Keeping a log records the sample but does not repair how the orders were selected."}, {"id": 530, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A retail analyst finds that stores with more training hours per employee also show higher sales per square meter. Leaders want to increase training in every store based on this result. What should the analyst tell them about the finding?", "options": {"A": "The link shows association, so a controlled study is needed before claiming that training drives sales", "B": "The link proves that training causes higher sales, so the chain should expand training now", "C": "The link is meaningless, because two measures that move together cannot share any genuine causal connection", "D": "The link shows that sales cause training, since busy stores can afford more hours"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The link shows association, so a controlled study is needed before claiming that training drives sales): A correlation shows that two measures move together, not that one causes the other. A controlled study such as a pilot with matched stores can test the causal claim. Saying the link proves cause goes too far. Saying it is meaningless ignores a real pattern, and claiming that sales cause training is a different guess with no more support."}, {"id": 535, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A hospital team lists many possible causes for late discharges. The list includes waiting for transport, incomplete physician notes, pharmacy delays, and cleaning queues. The team cannot test every cause at once. Which method helps rank the causes by how often each one actually contributes?", "options": {"A": "Build a fishbone diagram and work on the branch with the most entries", "B": "Ask the senior nurse to rank the causes from most important to least important", "C": "Collect data on the frequency of each cause and display it in a pareto chart", "D": "Run a hypothesis test on every cause at the same time with one data set"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Collect data on the frequency of each cause and display it in a pareto chart): A pareto chart turns frequency data into a ranking, so the team can see which causes carry the largest share and act there first. A fishbone diagram organizes possible causes but does not weigh them. One person's ranking is an opinion. Running many tests on one data set raises the chance of a false finding."}, {"id": 550, "domain": "design_for_six_sigma", "question": "A product team must set tolerances for two mating parts. Parts from different suppliers will be assembled, so the team compares a worst case stack with a statistical stack. What does the statistical approach assume about the part dimensions in this comparison?", "options": {"A": "The dimensions are fixed at their nominal values, so tolerance stack up does not occur", "B": "The dimensions vary independently and follow known distributions, so extremes rarely line up", "C": "The dimensions sit at the tolerance limits, so the stack is larger than in worst case", "D": "The dimensions vary together in one direction, so the stack equals the arithmetic sum of limits"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The dimensions vary independently and follow known distributions, so extremes rarely line up): Statistical tolerancing assumes the part dimensions vary independently and follow known distributions. That makes the chance of every part sitting at its worst limit very small. The stack can then be estimated with a root sum square style calculation. Assuming dimensions sit at the limits describes the worst case method, and assuming one shared direction would overstate the stack."}, {"id": 553, "domain": "organization_wide_planning_and_deployment", "question": "A distribution company has twenty improvement ideas on its list and enough trained staff for only four projects this year. The sponsor asks for a repeatable way to compare the ideas. Which screening criterion should the leadership team apply first?", "options": {"A": "The number of belts who are available to lead each idea.", "B": "The department that first suggested the idea for a formal review.", "C": "The expected financial benefit and the link to strategic goals.", "D": "The difficulty of the statistical tools each idea requires."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The expected financial benefit and the link to strategic goals.): Screening criteria compare ideas on expected benefit, strategic fit, risk, and effort, and the benefit and the link to the business goals come first because they decide whether an idea deserves resources at all. The number of available belts affects scheduling. The department that suggested the idea and the statistical difficulty of the work are secondary factors rather than the primary screen."}, {"id": 555, "domain": "organizational_process_management_and_measures", "question": "A call center wants to cut its average handle time from nine minutes to six. The manager decides to study the methods used by the two closest competitors in the same market. Which type of benchmarking is the team planning to use?", "options": {"A": "Collaborative benchmarking, because the firms share data openly.", "B": "Competitive benchmarking, because the comparison targets direct rivals.", "C": "Best-practice benchmarking, because the aim is to copy a proven method.", "D": "Breakthrough benchmarking, because the goal is a large performance leap."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Competitive benchmarking, because the comparison targets direct rivals.): Competitive benchmarking compares performance and methods against direct competitors in the same market, which is what the manager plans to do. Collaborative benchmarking involves sharing data with another organization, often from outside the market. Best-practice benchmarking hunts for a proven method wherever it exists, and breakthrough benchmarking aims at a large leap rather than a like-for-like comparison."}, {"id": 557, "domain": "organizational_process_management_and_measures", "question": "A Black Belt finishes a project that reduces scrap by $180,000 per year and also expects fewer customer complaints. The finance team confirms the scrap figure from the profit and loss statement. How should the Black Belt classify the two benefits?", "options": {"A": "Scrap savings are a hard benefit, and fewer complaints are a soft benefit.", "B": "Both benefits are hard because the finance team verified each of them.", "C": "Both benefits are soft until the customer complaints appear in the monthly accounts.", "D": "Scrap savings are a soft benefit, and fewer complaints are a hard benefit."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Scrap savings are a hard benefit, and fewer complaints are a soft benefit.): Hard benefits appear in the accounts as real cash, and the finance team confirmed the scrap figure from the profit and loss statement. Fewer complaints are a soft benefit because the gain is expected rather than yet recorded in the accounts. Calling both hard, calling both soft, or swapping the two labels misreads how the finance function classifies project gains."}, {"id": 558, "domain": "organizational_process_management_and_measures", "question": "A food processor cuts its order cycle from ten days to four, and customers begin ordering larger volumes. The purchasing team worries that suppliers will be squeezed by the faster schedule. What should the Black Belt point out about this change?", "options": {"A": "Suppliers are outside the project scope, so their concerns can wait.", "B": "Higher order volumes offset any strain that suppliers may feel over time.", "C": "The project should stop until every supplier agrees to the new schedule.", "D": "Faster cycles can strain suppliers, so their capacity deserves a review."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Faster cycles can strain suppliers, so their capacity deserves a review.): A shorter internal cycle pushes demand onto suppliers more quickly, so their capacity and lead times become part of the redesigned process. Removing them from scope ignores that they are stakeholders in the change. Higher volumes do not automatically offset strain, and halting the project until every supplier agrees asks for more control than the risk requires."}, {"id": 561, "domain": "team_management", "question": "A team must choose one of nine candidate solutions for a packaging problem, and the team needs a choice that it can defend with data. In the last meeting, the loudest two members pushed their favorites while quieter members said nothing. Which decision-making method should the Black Belt use instead?", "options": {"A": "Majority voting, which finds the option that the most members prefer.", "B": "Consensus building, which asks each member to accept the chosen option without any debate.", "C": "Nominal group technique, which gathers written ideas before the group discusses them.", "D": "An open discussion round, which lets each member speak in turn."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Nominal group technique, which gathers written ideas before the group discusses them.): Nominal group technique has each member write ideas or ratings before any discussion, so the quiet members' views are captured before the vocal members can sway the room. Majority voting still lets the largest bloc decide, consensus asks for agreement without equalizing input, and an open round repeats the very dynamic that produced the problem."}, {"id": 562, "domain": "team_management", "question": "A Black Belt is staffing a project to reduce medication errors in a hospital pharmacy. The sponsor suggests adding a nurse manager from another site who has never worked with the pharmacy team. Which factor matters most when the Black Belt judges this suggestion?", "options": {"A": "Whether the person brings needed expertise and can influence the pharmacy staff.", "B": "Whether the person has free time in the calendar for weekly meetings.", "C": "Whether the person has worked with the Black Belt on a prior project.", "D": "Whether the person holds a senior leadership title somewhere inside the hospital system."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Whether the person brings needed expertise and can influence the pharmacy staff.): Team member selection weighs relevant skills, subject knowledge, the ability to influence the process, and availability, and the nurse manager can bring clinical insight that the pharmacy team lacks. Calendar space and past work with the Black Belt are helpful but secondary. A senior title by itself does not supply the expertise the project needs."}, {"id": 563, "domain": "team_management", "question": "A bank's improvement team will take over the daily monitoring of a new loan process, but an assessment shows that most members cannot read a control chart. The Black Belt is asked to build a short training program. Which step belongs first?", "options": {"A": "Deliver the training and then survey the members about their reactions.", "B": "Turn the identified skill gaps into learning objectives for the sessions.", "C": "Choose a training vendor and compare the prices of the course packages it offers.", "D": "Schedule the sessions at a time when every member can attend."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Turn the identified skill gaps into learning objectives for the sessions.): A training needs assessment starts with the skills gap and converts it into learning objectives, which then shape the plan, the materials, and the delivery. Choosing a vendor and scheduling sessions come later. Delivering the training before the objectives exist would leave the content and the evaluation unplanned."}, {"id": 565, "domain": "define", "question": "A coffee shop chain learns that customers expect clean tables but only notice extra-fast mobile ordering as a pleasant surprise. A team member asks how to label these two features in the Kano model. What should the Black Belt say?", "options": {"A": "Clean tables are a must-be need, and fast mobile ordering is an attractive need.", "B": "Clean tables are an attractive need, and fast mobile ordering is a must-be need.", "C": "Both features are one-dimensional needs because customers rank them on surveys.", "D": "Both features are indifferent needs because customers rarely mention either one in feedback."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Clean tables are a must-be need, and fast mobile ordering is an attractive need.): A must-be need is expected as a basic standard, so a dirty table causes dissatisfaction while a clean one earns no praise. An attractive need delights customers when it is present and causes no complaint when it is missing, which fits the surprise at fast mobile ordering. The one-dimensional and indifferent labels describe different patterns of customer satisfaction."}, {"id": 567, "domain": "define", "question": "A Black Belt reviews a charter extract for a project to shorten emergency room wait times. The exhibit shows the goal, scope, and milestone sections as the team wrote them. Which statement in the extract correctly describes the project scope?", "options": {"A": "Every patient who arrives by ambulance at any of the chain's hospitals.", "B": "The pediatric emergency department and the ambulance dispatch service for that campus.", "C": "The hospital's urgent care clinics and their weekend staffing plans.", "D": "Adult patients at the central campus, from triage through bed assignment."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Adult patients at the central campus, from triage through bed assignment.): The scope section limits the project to one campus and to the adult emergency journey that the goal describes. Ambulance arrivals at every hospital widen the boundaries well beyond the charter. Pediatric services and urgent care clinics appear as out of scope, so those options describe work that this project does not cover.", "image": "question_images/q_567.png"}, {"id": 572, "domain": "measure", "question": "A filling line must deliver bottles between 496 and 504 milliliters. A histogram of 200 readings shows a bell shape centered near 500, and exactly 17 readings fall outside the specification limits. What should the Black Belt conclude about this process?", "options": {"A": "The process is off center, so the first step is to move the mean upward.", "B": "The process is roughly centered but produces about 8.5 percent outside the limits.", "C": "The process is stable, so the specification limits should be widened for the filling line.", "D": "The process is capable, so no further action on the fill volume is needed."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The process is roughly centered but produces about 8.5 percent outside the limits.): The histogram is centered near the target with a bell shape, so the process is not off center. Dividing 17 readings by 200 gives 0.085, which is 8.5 percent outside the limits and too high for a filling operation. Widening the limits or declaring the process capable would accept the loss rather than reduce it.", "image": "question_images/q_572.png"}, {"id": 573, "domain": "measure", "question": "A process is stable and its output is normal. The mean sits three standard deviations above the lower specification limit, and its spread gives a Cp of 1.50. The Black Belt reports a Cpk of 1.00. What explains the difference between the two indices?", "options": {"A": "Cp ignores where the process is centered, and Cpk accounts for that shift.", "B": "Cp uses short-term data, and Cpk uses long-term data from the process.", "C": "Cp describes stability of the process over time, and Cpk describes spread.", "D": "Cp is computed from the range, and Cpk is computed from the standard deviation."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cp ignores where the process is centered, and Cpk accounts for that shift.): Cp compares the specification width with the process spread, so it does not change when the mean moves away from the center. Cpk measures the distance from the mean to the nearer specification limit, so a shifted process scores lower. The mean sits three standard deviations above the lower limit, so that side gives a value of 1.00 and the index cannot exceed it. Short-term and long-term data separate Cp from Pp, and the subgroup range is used for control limits rather than for this index."}, {"id": 574, "domain": "measure", "question": "A printed circuit board passes through four assembly steps with first pass yields of 0.98, 0.97, 0.95, and 0.94. A customer asks how many boards come through all four steps without any rework. What is the rolled throughput yield, rounded to three decimals?", "options": {"A": "0.960, the average of the four first pass yields.", "B": "0.940, the lowest first pass yield among the four steps.", "C": "0.849, the product of the four first pass yields.", "D": "0.903, the product of the first three steps only."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (0.849, the product of the four first pass yields.): Rolled throughput yield multiplies the first pass yields of the steps. Here 0.98 times 0.97 equals 0.9506, times 0.95 equals 0.90307, and times 0.94 equals 0.84888, which rounds to 0.849. Averaging the four yields gives 0.960 and describes a process that does not exist. Taking the lowest yield ignores the losses at the other steps, and stopping after three steps leaves out the fourth."}, {"id": 577, "domain": "measure", "question": "A plant weighs powder on eleven bench scales that are adjusted at the start of each month. An auditor asks for proof that the readings are correct and linked to a national standard. Which metrology practice does the auditor want to see?", "options": {"A": "A gauge R and R study run on the eleven scales last quarter.", "B": "A percent agreement study that compares two operators on each scale.", "C": "A capability study that shows all eleven scales can meet the powder specification limits.", "D": "Calibration records that show traceability back to the national reference standard."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Calibration records that show traceability back to the national reference standard.): Calibration compares an instrument with a reference, and traceability links that reference back through an unbroken chain to a national standard. A gauge R and R study measures the variation of the measurement system rather than its link to a standard. Percent agreement covers attribute judgments, and a capability study addresses whether the process can meet specifications."}, {"id": 580, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A bank wants to know whether the type of loan application error depends on which branch processed it. The team counts errors by branch and by error type and places the counts in a table. Which test should the Black Belt use?", "options": {"A": "A chi square test of independence for the two categorical variables.", "B": "A two sample t test that compares the average errors per branch.", "C": "A one way analysis of variance on the error counts by branch.", "D": "A paired t test that compares each branch result with the overall average."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A chi square test of independence for the two categorical variables.): Both variables are categories, branch and error type, so a chi square test of independence checks whether the counts in the table depart from what independence would predict. The t tests and the analysis of variance compare means of a measured response, which the table of counts by category does not provide."}, {"id": 582, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A Black Belt compares the mean tensile strength of parts from three suppliers and finds that at least one supplier differs from the others. The team prefers one test over three separate two sample comparisons that raise false alarm risk. Which test should the Black Belt use?", "options": {"A": "A chi square test, which compares the defect counts of parts from each supplier.", "B": "A paired t test, which compares two suppliers and then the third.", "C": "A test of proportions, which compares the defect rate of each supplier.", "D": "Analysis of variance, which tests the supplier means at once for equality."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Analysis of variance, which tests the supplier means at once for equality.): Analysis of variance compares three or more group means in one test and holds the overall error rate at the chosen level. Running three separate two sample tests raises the chance of a false alarm, which the team wants to avoid. A chi square test and a test of proportions work with counts and rates rather than measured strengths."}, {"id": 590, "domain": "improve", "question": "A plant manager asks the Black Belt to choose between a week long focused event that a team runs to clear a backlog, and a slow program of small improvements led by the work teams over a year. How should the Black Belt describe the two approaches?", "options": {"A": "The event is kaizen, and the longer program is a kaizen blitz.", "B": "Both approaches are kaizen blitzes because teams lead the work.", "C": "The event is a kaizen blitz, and the longer program is kaizen.", "D": "Both approaches are kaizen because both improve the process."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The event is a kaizen blitz, and the longer program is kaizen.): A kaizen blitz is a short, focused event that a team runs to make rapid changes, while kaizen is the ongoing practice of small improvements by the people who do the work. Swapping the two labels misstates both. Calling both a blitz or calling both kaizen ignores the difference in duration and scale."}, {"id": 592, "domain": "improve", "question": "A team has two candidate layouts for a new packing line. Building both would cost a great deal, and the team cannot agree on which one will handle peak volume. The sponsor wants evidence before releasing the full construction budget. Which implementation step should the Black Belt recommend?", "options": {"A": "Choose the layout that the most senior engineer on the team prefers.", "B": "Build the cheaper layout and adjust it after the first production month.", "C": "Delay the decision until the team reaches full agreement on one layout.", "D": "Run a pilot or a simulation and compare the results of both options."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Run a pilot or a simulation and compare the results of both options.): A pilot or a simulation tests a proposed design on a small scale and produces evidence before the full budget is committed. Choosing a layout by seniority, building the cheaper option and fixing it later, or waiting for complete agreement all make the decision without the data the sponsor requested."}, {"id": 596, "domain": "control", "question": "A project team has cut assembly defects and will hand the line back to the production manager next month. The manager asks how the line will keep the new settings and checks in place after the team leaves. Which control tool answers this request?", "options": {"A": "A lessons learned file, which records what the team discovered during the project.", "B": "A toll-gate review, which checks the project at planned milestone dates.", "C": "A Gantt chart, which shows the handoff tasks on a calendar timeline.", "D": "A control plan, which lists the checks, owners, and reactions for the process."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A control plan, which lists the checks, owners, and reactions for the process.): A control plan names each measurement, its frequency, who performs it, and what to do when a result drifts, and it transfers ownership to the process owner. A lessons learned file records history, a toll-gate review checks project milestones, and a Gantt chart schedules tasks, so none of them keeps a running process under control."}, {"id": 598, "domain": "control", "question": "An operator notices that a point falls just inside the upper control limit and adjusts the process to bring the next points lower. The process then swings back and forth around the target, and variation increases. What should the Black Belt explain?", "options": {"A": "The adjustment is correct because the point was close to the limit.", "B": "Reacting to a point inside the limits is tampering, which adds variation.", "C": "The process shows a special cause that the operator should remove.", "D": "The control limits are too narrow, so the chart should be recalculated right away."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reacting to a point inside the limits is tampering, which adds variation.): Control limits describe the range of common cause variation, so a point just inside them carries no signal that calls for an adjustment. Reacting to such a point adds variation and produces the swinging pattern the operator now sees. The point sits inside the limit, so it does not mark a special cause, and the limits do not need recalculating on this evidence."}, {"id": 599, "domain": "design_for_six_sigma", "question": "A company wants a new handheld scanner that meets customer needs the first time, rather than fixing an existing scanner through repeated improvement cycles. The design must be validated before the first production shipment. Which methodology phase set should the Black Belt propose for this effort?", "options": {"A": "DMADV, which defines, measures, analyzes, designs, and validates the new product.", "B": "DMAIC, which defines, measures, analyzes, improves, and controls the current process.", "C": "PDCA, which plans, does, checks, and acts on one improvement at a time.", "D": "SMED, which reduces the changeover time on the existing production line."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (DMADV, which defines, measures, analyzes, designs, and validates the new product.): DMADV is the Design for Six Sigma roadmap for creating a new product or process that meets its requirements from the start. DMAIC improves an existing process through its improve and control phases. PDCA is a general improvement cycle, and SMED targets changeover time on a line that already exists."}, {"id": 600, "domain": "design_for_six_sigma", "question": "A design team sets a tight tolerance on every part dimension to protect performance, and the cost of each part rises sharply. The team asks whether a cheaper route exists that still meets the performance target. Which design approach should the Black Belt suggest?", "options": {"A": "Design for test, which adds more inspection points to catch bad parts.", "B": "Design for maintainability, which simplifies the routine repairs after the product has been shipped.", "C": "Design for cost, which removes features until the price target is met.", "D": "Robust design, which reduces sensitivity to variation instead of tightening tolerances."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Robust design, which reduces sensitivity to variation instead of tightening tolerances.): Robust design makes performance less sensitive to variation in parts and conditions, so the team can relax costly tolerances without losing the result. Design for test adds inspection, design for maintainability eases repairs, and design for cost trims features. None of those three addresses the sensitivity that drove the tight tolerances."}, {"id": 602, "domain": "organization_wide_planning_and_deployment", "question": "A plant logged four quality costs last quarter: operator training, incoming inspection, rework of failed units, and warranty repairs. The Black Belt must classify each item for the cost of quality report. Into which two categories do rework and warranty repairs fall?", "options": {"A": "Rework is an appraisal cost, and warranty repairs are a prevention cost.", "B": "Rework is an internal failure cost, and warranty repairs are an appraisal cost.", "C": "Rework is an external failure cost, while warranty repairs are an internal failure cost.", "D": "Rework is an internal failure cost, and warranty repairs are an external failure cost."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Rework is an internal failure cost, and warranty repairs are an external failure cost): Rework is found before the product leaves the plant, which makes it an internal failure cost, while warranty repairs are found after the customer receives the product, which makes them external failure costs. Prevention costs cover training and process design, and appraisal costs cover the inspection and testing used to find defects. Option A swaps the failure costs with appraisal and prevention, option B labels warranty repairs as appraisal, and option C reverses the two failure categories."}, {"id": 603, "domain": "organization_wide_planning_and_deployment", "question": "A bank has two improvement efforts running side by side. One team removes waiting time from loan approvals by redesigning the workflow. Another team reduces the number of data entry errors by studying variation in the process. A leader asks the Black Belt how these two efforts fit together. Which answer is correct?", "options": {"A": "Removing waiting is lean work, and cutting errors is Six Sigma work.", "B": "Removing waiting is Six Sigma work, and cutting errors is lean work.", "C": "Both efforts are lean work, because both change the loan process flow.", "D": "Both efforts are Six Sigma work, because both reduce process variation."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Removing waiting is lean work, and cutting errors is Six Sigma work): Lean work targets speed by removing waste such as waiting, so the workflow redesign fits lean, while Six Sigma work targets variation and defects, so the data entry errors fit Six Sigma. Option B reverses the two methods, and options C and D each force both efforts into a single method. The two approaches are often combined in practice, but the question asks which label fits each effort on its own."}, {"id": 614, "domain": "define", "question": "The exhibit shows a charter with a problem, a goal, and two scope sections. A team member asks which section keeps the project from drifting into sales contract rules, which the team cannot control from the billing center. Which section answers that question?", "options": {"A": "The problem section, because it records the current rate of errors.", "B": "The out of scope section, because it names work the team will not do.", "C": "The goal section, because it sets the target for cutting errors.", "D": "The in scope section, because it names the billing process at one site."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The out of scope section, because it names work the team will not do): The out of scope section draws the boundary by naming work the team will not touch, which is what keeps the project from drifting into sales contract rules that the billing team does not control. The problem section gives the current error rate, the goal section gives the target, and the in scope section names what the team will change. Only the out of scope section answers the question about drift.", "image": "question_images/q_614.png"}, {"id": 616, "domain": "define", "question": "A team translates customer wants into engineering targets. The matrix lists customer needs down the side and design requirements across the top, with symbols showing how strongly each design requirement affects each need. A member asks the Black Belt for the name and purpose of this tool. What is it?", "options": {"A": "A failure mode and effects analysis, which ranks risks by severity and detection.", "B": "A value stream map, which separates value added work from waiting time.", "C": "A control plan, which lists how each measure will be checked in production.", "D": "A quality function deployment matrix, which links needs to design choices."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A quality function deployment matrix, which links needs to design choices): The house of quality is the central matrix of quality function deployment, and it places customer needs against design requirements while marking how strongly each requirement affects each need. A failure mode and effects analysis ranks failure risks by severity, occurrence, and detection rather than linking needs to design choices. A value stream map times process steps, and a control plan states how measures will be checked in production, so neither one is the translation matrix described."}, {"id": 617, "domain": "define", "question": "A project has four tasks. Design takes six days, order materials takes two days, build takes five days, and test takes three days. Materials cannot be ordered until design ends, and build cannot start until materials arrive. Test follows build. What is the critical path length?", "options": {"A": "Fourteen days, if material ordering overlaps with the design work.", "B": "Eleven days, using design, build, and test and skipping the ordering.", "C": "Sixteen days, running design, then materials, then build, then test.", "D": "Ten days, because only tasks that use people add elapsed time."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Sixteen days, running design, then materials, then build, then test): The chain is design at 6 days, then material ordering at 2 days, then build at 5 days, then test at 3 days. Adding the chain gives 6 plus 2 plus 5 plus 3, which equals 16 days, and this longest dependent chain is the critical path. Option A assumes material ordering can overlap with design, which the rules forbid, and option B drops the material step entirely. Option D counts only tasks that use people, which ignores the wait for materials."}, {"id": 622, "domain": "measure", "question": "The histogram shows 50 fill weights from a bottling line, with upper and lower specification limits marked. Five of the 50 readings fall outside the specification limits, and the rest cluster near the middle. What does this pattern show the team?", "options": {"A": "The process is centered, so the readings outside the limits do not matter.", "B": "The histogram proves the measurement system is biased and needs repair.", "C": "About 10 percent of fills fall outside the limits, so capability is poor.", "D": "The limits are set too tight and should be widened to fit the spread."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 10 percent of fills fall outside the limits, so capability is poor): Five of the 50 readings sit outside the specification limits, and 5 divided by 50 equals 0.10, or 10 percent. That rate of out of specification fills shows the process does not reliably meet the customer limits. Option A treats centering as if it removes the out of specification readings, option B blames the gauge without evidence, and option D suggests moving the limits instead of fixing the process.", "image": "question_images/q_622.png"}, {"id": 628, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A team tests whether a new label reduces errors. The test returns a p value of 0.03 against an alpha of 0.05. The manager says the result proves the new label works. The Black Belt cautions that a p value shows something narrower. Which statement is correct?", "options": {"A": "It gives the chance the null hypothesis is actually true.", "B": "It gives the chance the new label will work in daily production.", "C": "It gives the share of errors the new label removed in the trial.", "D": "It gives the chance of data this extreme if the null were true."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It gives the chance of data this extreme if the null were true): A p value answers how likely the observed result, or something more extreme, would be if the null hypothesis were true, so a small p value makes the null look unlikely. It does not give the probability that the null is true, which is option A, and it is not the probability that the new label will work in production, which is option B. Option C describes the effect size, a separate quantity that the p value does not report."}, {"id": 629, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A bank compares call handling time for two teams. Team A has a mean of 240 seconds with 30 calls, and Team B has a mean of 255 seconds with 28 calls. The standard deviations are close, near 20 seconds for both. Which test should the Black Belt use to compare the two means?", "options": {"A": "A chi square test, comparing the two teams by category.", "B": "A paired t test, comparing each call against the one before it.", "C": "A two sample t test, comparing the two team means directly.", "D": "A regression of handling time against call volume for both teams."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A two sample t test, comparing the two team means directly): The two teams are separate groups, and the question compares their average handling times, so an independent two sample t test fits. A chi square test compares counts in categories rather than two means. A paired t test applies when each item is measured twice, such as one call before and after a change, and a regression of time on call volume answers a different question about a relationship between two variables."}, {"id": 631, "domain": "analyze", "question": "The Pareto chart ranks five order defects from a distribution center. Missed delivery leads with 48 cases, followed by wrong item at 30, damaged box at 22, wrong address at 14, and other at 6. Where should the team focus its first effort?", "options": {"A": "On the other category, since it groups all the leftover defect types.", "B": "On wrong addresses, since that category is the easiest one to correct.", "C": "On the five defect categories together, since each one adds a share of total defects.", "D": "On missed deliveries, since that one defect type is the largest single cause."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (On missed deliveries, since that one defect type is the largest single cause): The counts total 120, and missed deliveries at 48 make up 48 divided by 120, which is 40 percent, the tallest bar. The top two categories together, 48 plus 30, equal 78, which is 65 percent of the total, so the first effort belongs on the largest bar. Option A targets the smallest group, option B targets an easy but minor category, and option C spreads the effort instead of using the ranking.", "image": "question_images/q_631.png"}, {"id": 632, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A machine keeps stopping. The team asks why and learns a sensor trips. Asking why again shows metal chips reach the sensor, then that a guard is missing, then that the broken guard was not replaced. What has this sequence uncovered?", "options": {"A": "A chain of causes, traced back toward a workable root cause.", "B": "A single cause, because the first answer explains the whole problem.", "C": "A list of symptoms that each need their own improvement project.", "D": "A statistical sample, because each answer counts as one data point."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A chain of causes, traced back toward a workable root cause): Repeatedly asking why turns a visible problem into a chain of causes, and the team keeps going until it reaches a cause it can act on, such as the broken guard that was not replaced. Option B assumes the first answer is the whole story, which stops the search too early. Option C treats each answer as a separate project, and option D confuses a cause chain with a data sample."}, {"id": 636, "domain": "improve", "question": "A workshop has tools scattered across benches, unmarked storage, and no routine for cleaning. The team sorts what is needed, sets a place for each item, shines the area, and writes a standard for keeping it that way. Which method are they applying, and what is the missing step?", "options": {"A": "They are using 5S, and the missing step is the fifth one, sustain.", "B": "They are using kanban, and the missing step is a card based signal.", "C": "They are using poka yoke, and the missing step is a fail safe device.", "D": "They are using kaizen, and the missing step is a weekly improvement event."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (They are using 5S, and the missing step is the fifth one, sustain): The five steps are sort, set in order, shine, standardize, and sustain, and the team has completed the first four. Sustain is the step that keeps the first four in place through habits, audits, and leadership attention. Kanban uses cards to pull work, poka yoke prevents errors with a device, and kaizen is ongoing improvement activity, so none of those names this workplace organization method."}, {"id": 640, "domain": "improve", "question": "An assembly station installs a connector that must face one way. Workers sometimes fit it backward, and the fault is found only at final test. The team wants a device that makes the backward fit impossible rather than a reminder. Which improvement fits this request?", "options": {"A": "A poka yoke fixture that only accepts the connector in the correct way.", "B": "A sign at the station that reminds workers to check the direction.", "C": "An added final inspection step that catches the backward connectors before shipping.", "D": "A retraining class that reviews the correct orientation each month."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A poka yoke fixture that only accepts the connector in the correct way): A poka yoke is a device that prevents an error from happening or makes it obvious at once, so a fixture that physically blocks the backward fit removes the defect at the source. A sign still depends on the worker noticing and remembering, which is the weakness the team wants to remove. A final test catches the fault after the work, and retraining depends on memory, so neither one prevents the backward fit."}, {"id": 642, "domain": "improve", "question": "A machine runs for 400 of 500 scheduled minutes. It produces 380 units against a design rate of one unit per minute for the time it runs. Of those, 361 pass inspection on the first try. What is the overall equipment effectiveness of this machine?", "options": {"A": "About 76 percent, from availability 80 percent, performance 95 percent, and quality 100 percent.", "B": "About 90 percent, from availability 100 percent, performance 95 percent, and quality 95 percent.", "C": "About 72 percent, from availability 80 percent, performance 95 percent, and quality 95 percent.", "D": "About 68 percent, from availability 80 percent, performance 90 percent, and quality 95 percent."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 72 percent, from availability 80 percent, performance 95 percent, and quality 95 percent): Availability is 400 running minutes out of 500 scheduled minutes, which equals 0.80. Performance is 380 units produced divided by the 400 units the design rate allows in 400 minutes, which equals 0.95. Quality is 361 good units divided by 380 produced, which equals 0.95, and 0.80 times 0.95 times 0.95 equals 0.722, or about 72 percent. Option A assumes perfect quality, option B assumes perfect availability, and option D lowers performance to 90 percent."}, {"id": 648, "domain": "control", "question": "Six months after a project closes, the defects creep back up. The team finds that new hires were not taught the revised method, the checklist was dropped from the work packet, and no one reviews the measure. Which action best sustains the gain?", "options": {"A": "Standardize the method, keep the checklist, and assign ongoing measure reviews.", "B": "Reopen the project and run another round of improvement experiments.", "C": "Add an inspection step at the end so fewer bad units reach customers.", "D": "Raise the specification limits so the creeping defects stay within the accepted range."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Standardize the method, keep the checklist, and assign ongoing measure reviews): The gain faded because the revised method was not built into training, the checklist left the work packet, and nobody owned the measure, so the fix is to lock those three in place. Reopening the project and running more experiments adds cost without addressing the missing standard. Adding an inspection step detects defects after the fact, and raising the specification limits hides the problem instead of holding the gain."}, {"id": 650, "domain": "design_for_six_sigma", "question": "A design team must set a tolerance on a shaft diameter. Wider tolerances cost less to make but allow more variation in the assembled product. Narrower tolerances reduce variation but raise cost. Which approach helps the team choose a tolerance that keeps the product working?", "options": {"A": "Design for test, which adds inspection points for each part in the final assembly.", "B": "Poka yoke, which makes the assembly step impossible to perform incorrectly.", "C": "Statistical tolerancing, which combines part variation to set a working limit.", "D": "Design for maintainability, which plans how the assembly will be serviced."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Statistical tolerancing, which combines part variation to set a working limit): Statistical tolerancing uses the way individual part variations combine in an assembly, often by a root sum square method, to set limits that keep the product working without demanding the tightest possible dimensions. Design for test adds inspection points, poka yoke prevents assembly errors, and design for maintainability plans service and repair, so none of those addresses how tolerances stack up. Choosing tolerances this way balances cost against the variation the assembly can absorb."}, {"id": 655, "domain": "organizational_process_management_and_measures", "question": "A distribution center tracks customer complaints and on-time shipment rate. Complaints arrive after the fact, so leaders want an earlier signal. The team already reviews both measures each month in the operations meeting. Which measure should the Black Belt add as a leading indicator?", "options": {"A": "The number of customer complaints that were closed during the current month", "B": "The percentage of orders picked on the first pass without a recheck", "C": "The total shipping cost recorded at the end of the fiscal quarter", "D": "The count of late deliveries reported by customers after arrival"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The percentage of orders picked on the first pass without a recheck): First pass pick accuracy is measured during the work, so a drop warns leaders before customers ever feel it. Complaints and late deliveries are lagging measures because they record harm that already happened. Option A counts complaints already closed, which is still backward looking. Option C is a financial total reported weeks later, and option D is another customer-reported outcome."}, {"id": 657, "domain": "team_management", "question": "A process improvement team has formed, but members are quietly testing who leads and which ideas will be accepted. Meetings are polite and little work gets done. The sponsor wants the team to reach productive work quickly. Which stage of team development is this group in?", "options": {"A": "Forming, because members have not yet begun testing who leads the group", "B": "Performing, because the team meets regularly and works together without any open conflict", "C": "Storming, because members are testing roles, influence, and how decisions get made", "D": "Adjourning, because the group has finished the tasks in its charter"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Storming, because members are testing roles, influence, and how decisions get made): Storming is the stage where members jockey for influence and challenge how the group will work, which matches the quiet testing described. Forming in option A is polite orientation where the group is still learning its purpose, but here members have already begun testing roles and influence. Performing in option B requires settled roles and productive output. Adjourning in option D happens when the work ends, and this team has not started it."}, {"id": 661, "domain": "control", "question": "An X-bar and R chart tracks the fill weight of a bottling line. Subgroup seven plots at 13.6 grams, and the upper control limit is 13.0 grams. The R chart stays in control and no operator or material change was recorded. What should the Black Belt conclude about that subgroup?", "options": {"A": "The specification limits have been exceeded, so the product is out of spec", "B": "The chart is over-sensitive, so the control limits should be widened to avoid false alarms", "C": "A special cause is present, so the process should be investigated at that point", "D": "The point is normal variation, so no action is needed until a trend appears"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A special cause is present, so the process should be investigated at that point): Subgroup seven sits above the upper control limit of 13.0 grams, so the chart signals a special cause. Control charts compare each point with control limits, not with specification limits, so option A uses the wrong reference. Widening limits to suppress a signal is tampering and hides the problem, so option B is wrong. A single point beyond a control limit is not normal variation, and option D would ignore a real signal.", "image": "question_images/q_661.png"}, {"id": 662, "domain": "improve", "question": "A parts cell uses a pull system and holds two hours of demand as buffer stock. Hourly usage is 60 parts, replenishment takes 90 minutes, and the container holds 30 parts. How many kanban cards does the cell need at minimum?", "options": {"A": "3 cards, based on the replenishment time without any buffer stock", "B": "5 cards, based on demand during replenishment and a half buffer", "C": "6 cards, based on the total parts after subtracting one full container", "D": "7 cards, based on demand during replenishment plus the buffer stock"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (7 cards, based on demand during replenishment plus the buffer stock): During the 90 minute replenishment, the cell uses 60 times 1.5, which equals 90 parts. The two hour buffer adds 60 times 2, which equals 120 parts. The total is 90 plus 120, or 210 parts, and 210 divided by 30 parts per container equals 7 cards. Option A ignores the buffer and returns 3 cards, option B uses only half the buffer, and option C takes a full container off the total demand."}, {"id": 663, "domain": "team_management", "question": "A Black Belt notices that two senior members dominate every team meeting while quieter members rarely speak, so data decisions stall. The team has eight members and six weeks left before the pilot begins. Which facilitation move would improve the team's participation?", "options": {"A": "Ask each member for input in turn and record all ideas before judging", "B": "Shorten the meetings so the senior members have less time to speak", "C": "Assign the quiet members written reports instead of meeting attendance", "D": "Let the senior members lead the meeting and summarize the decisions"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ask each member for input in turn and record all ideas before judging): Round robin input lets every member contribute before the senior voices shape the decision, which is a standard facilitation method. Shortening the meeting in option B reduces talk time but does not change who dominates. Sending quiet members to written reports in option C removes them from the discussion. Letting the senior members summarize in option D keeps the same imbalance that stalled the decisions."}, {"id": 666, "domain": "organization_wide_planning_and_deployment", "question": "A company can fund one project this quarter. Project A saves $180,000 and can start now. Project B saves $95,000 and needs two new hires first. Project C saves $320,000 but depends on a system change that is not approved. Which project should rank first?", "options": {"A": "Project C, because the largest savings figure should drive the ranking", "B": "Project B, because adding staff builds capacity for more projects later", "C": "Project A, because it combines solid savings with a ready start date", "D": "No project should start before a full cost benefit ratio is complete"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Project A, because it combines solid savings with a ready start date): Ranking weighs the benefit against feasibility and risk. Project C offers the largest savings, but the benefit cannot be captured this quarter because it depends on an unapproved system change. Project B saves less and needs new hires before it can begin. Project A delivers solid savings and can start now, so it ranks first on the evidence given."}, {"id": 667, "domain": "control", "question": "A hospital tracks the share of hand hygiene observations that pass each day, and the daily count of observations varies between 60 and 140. The team records a pass or fail for each observation and wants limits that adjust with the sample size. Which control chart fits this data?", "options": {"A": "A p chart, because it plots the proportion defective with a varying sample size", "B": "A c chart, because it plots the number of defects found in samples of constant size", "C": "An X-bar and R chart, because it handles continuous measurement data like diameters", "D": "An np chart, because it plots the number defective with a constant sample"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A p chart, because it plots the proportion defective with a varying sample size): The measure is a proportion, pass or fail, and the number of observations changes from day to day. A p chart computes limits that follow the sample size, which is exactly what the team needs. The c chart in option B counts defects in constant sized samples, and the np chart in option D counts defective items in a constant sample. The X-bar and R chart in option C is for continuous measurements, not pass or fail data."}, {"id": 669, "domain": "improve", "question": "A team maps an order entry process with a 42 minute lead time. The map shows 30 minutes of waiting, including a 12 minute credit check, and 12 minutes of work. Which improvement target does the map best support for the pilot?", "options": {"A": "Cut the value added work first, because it is 12 minutes of the lead time", "B": "Reduce the credit check to zero, because approval steps are a form of waste", "C": "Attack the 30 minutes of waiting, starting with the 12 minute credit check", "D": "Add staff to the credit check so more orders can be approved each hour"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Attack the 30 minutes of waiting, starting with the 12 minute credit check): The map shows 30 minutes of waiting against 12 minutes of work, so the largest chance to cut the 42 minute lead time sits in the waiting. The 12 minute credit check is the single largest waiting block, which makes it the natural first target. Option A works on value added time, which is the part the customer pays for and the smallest share. Option B sets an unrealistic target, and option D adds capacity to a step that is already a form of waste.", "image": "question_images/q_669.png"}, {"id": 670, "domain": "define", "question": "During a project to reduce billing errors, a manager asks the team to also fix the phone system and redesign the customer portal. Both requests sit outside the scope written in the approved charter. What should the Black Belt do first?", "options": {"A": "Accept both requests so the team keeps its reputation with the manager", "B": "Assign the requests to the finance analyst and keep the project moving", "C": "Postpone both requests until the control phase of the project and then decide together", "D": "Discuss the requests with the sponsor and follow the charter change process"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Discuss the requests with the sponsor and follow the charter change process): Both requests sit outside the approved scope, and only the sponsor can authorize a change to the charter. Raising the requests there protects the project from scope creep while keeping the manager's concerns visible. Option A expands scope without approval. Option B hands the work to someone outside the team without a charter decision, and option C delays the decision until the control phase, when the team is closing out."}, {"id": 673, "domain": "control", "question": "A machine operator adjusts the process after every reading that lands near a control limit, even though the chart shows only common cause variation. The operator wants tighter results and believes the adjustments will help. What is the likely effect of this practice?", "options": {"A": "The process variation will shrink toward the control limits over time", "B": "The process mean will move closer to the specification target with each passing week", "C": "The added variation will make the process less predictable and less capable", "D": "The control limits will widen as the operator collects more subgroups"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The added variation will make the process less predictable and less capable): Reacting to common cause variation is tampering, and tampering adds variation to a stable process. Deming's funnel experiment showed that adjusting after every point grew the spread instead of shrinking it. Option A describes the result the operator expected, but the opposite happens. Option B assumes the adjustments steer the mean, which they cannot do when the variation is common cause. Option D is wrong because control limits come from the process, not from the number of subgroups."}, {"id": 675, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A scatter diagram plots line speed against fill weight for twelve recent runs. The points trend downward steadily from left to right, and the correlation is negative and fairly strong. The team wants to know whether the two variables are related before designing an experiment. What should the Black Belt conclude?", "options": {"A": "Fill weight tends to fall as line speed rises, so speed merits further study", "B": "Line speed and fill weight are independent, so no further study of the process is needed", "C": "The scatter pattern proves that a faster line speed causes lower fill weight", "D": "Fill weight drives the line speed, because the fill weight is the plotted response"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Fill weight tends to fall as line speed rises, so speed merits further study): A downward trend with a fairly strong negative correlation shows the two variables move in opposite directions, and scatter plots reveal relationship, not cause. The result justifies a designed experiment that can test line speed while holding other factors steady. Option B ignores the visible pattern. Option C treats an observed relationship as proof of cause, and option D reverses the direction of the relationship shown in the chart.", "image": "question_images/q_675.png"}, {"id": 676, "domain": "improve", "question": "A pharmacy wants to stop the wrong drug from being loaded into an automated dispensing cabinet. Staff suggest a checklist that a technician signs before loading each tray. Managers want the control to work even when the technician is rushed. Which error proofing approach does the checklist represent?", "options": {"A": "A warning device, because the checklist alerts the staff to a risky loading step", "B": "A physical interlock, because the tray cannot be loaded out of order", "C": "A procedural control, because it relies on a person following a check", "D": "A design change, because the cabinet now prevents the wrong pick"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A procedural control, because it relies on a person following a check): A signed checklist depends on a person remembering and choosing to complete each step, which makes it a procedural control. It works only as well as the person's attention in the moment, and the manager's concern about rushed work points directly at that weakness. Option A describes a device that alerts, and no device is used. Option B describes a hard interlock, and option D describes a design change, and neither exists in this situation."}, {"id": 682, "domain": "measure", "question": "A gauge can only read in steps of 0.05 millimeters, and the process spread is 0.20 millimeters. The rule of tens compares the resolution with the process spread or the tolerance. What does this relationship suggest about the measurement system?", "options": {"A": "The gauge resolution is adequate, because the process spread is four times larger", "B": "The gauge resolution is adequate, because the readings vary by whole steps", "C": "The gauge resolution is too fine, because it splits the spread into many parts", "D": "The gauge resolution is too coarse, because it gives only four increments"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The gauge resolution is too coarse, because it gives only four increments): Divide the process spread of 0.20 by the reading step of 0.05 and the gauge gives only four increments across the spread. The rule of tens looks for at least ten increments, so this gauge cannot see the process detail well enough. Option A and option B treat four increments as adequate, which ignores the rule. Option C reverses the problem, because a fine gauge would give more increments rather than fewer."}, {"id": 686, "domain": "measure", "question": "A Black Belt computes Cpk from three months of data and gets 1.10. A control chart of the same data shows an upward shift in the last two weeks. What should the Belt do first before trusting the capability result?", "options": {"A": "Study the shift and restore stability, then recompute capability on stable data", "B": "Accept the result as valid, because the Cpk value sits well above the 1.00 threshold", "C": "Widen the control limits so the shift is no longer treated as a signal", "D": "Collect more data from the same period to confirm the value of 1.10"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Study the shift and restore stability, then recompute capability on stable data): Capability figures are meaningful only when the process is in statistical control. A control chart that shows a shift means the process changed during the study period, so the data mix two different processes and the Cpk value does not describe either one. Option B accepts a number that rests on unstable data. Option C widens limits to hide the shift, and option D collects more data from the same unsettled period."}, {"id": 687, "domain": "analyze", "question": "An engineer compares the mean strength of parts from three machines using a one-way ANOVA and obtains a p-value of 0.004. The test assumptions were checked and the residuals look acceptable. Leaders want to know which machine differs from the others. What is the correct next step?", "options": {"A": "Conclude that all three machines produce parts with equal mean strength", "B": "Raise the significance level to 0.01 and rerun the same ANOVA test", "C": "Use a follow-up comparison to find which machine means differ from the rest", "D": "Report the result as a correlation between the machine type and the part strength"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use a follow-up comparison to find which machine means differ from the rest): The ANOVA p-value of 0.004 is below 0.05, so at least one machine mean differs from the others. The test does not say which machine, so the next step is a follow-up comparison that controls the error rate across the pairs. Option A states the null that the test just rejected. Option B changes alpha after the result, and option D uses a correlation method for a comparison of three group means."}, {"id": 688, "domain": "define", "question": "A Black Belt starts a project on loan approval delays and needs a quick view of the process boundaries and the groups that touch the work. The team has not yet mapped the steps and wants agreement on where the process starts and ends. Which tool should be used first?", "options": {"A": "A SIPOC diagram, which lists suppliers, inputs, process steps, outputs, and customers", "B": "A control chart, which shows whether the delay times remain stable across a longer period", "C": "A failure mode study, which ranks the ways the approval step can fail", "D": "A design of experiments, which tests how approval rules affect cycle time"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A SIPOC diagram, which lists suppliers, inputs, process steps, outputs, and customers): SIPOC gives a high level view of the process and marks where it starts and ends, which is what the team needs before mapping steps in detail. The customer and supplier columns also name the groups that touch the work. Option B is used later to judge stability over time. Option C ranks failure modes, and option D tests factors, so neither fits the first step of defining a process."}, {"id": 689, "domain": "improve", "question": "A plant improves the speed of three upstream workstations, but overall output does not change because the packing station cannot keep up. The team wants the whole line to produce more units per hour. What should the team do next?", "options": {"A": "Speed up the upstream workstations further so they build a buffer of parts", "B": "Focus improvement on the packing station, the current constraint on output", "C": "Add staff to the upstream stations so they can run during every break", "D": "Slow the upstream stations so they match the packing station's current output rate"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Focus improvement on the packing station, the current constraint on output): Speeding up non-constraint work does not raise output, because the line can only move as fast as its constraint. The packing station limits the flow, so improving it lifts throughput for the whole line. Option A adds capacity ahead of a step that cannot absorb it. Option C adds more capacity at the wrong place, and option D slows the line below its current rate."}, {"id": 691, "domain": "team_management", "question": "Two team members disagree about the root cause, and the discussion is turning personal. The team has data from the last eight weeks of production and a pilot due in three weeks. Which action by the Black Belt best follows good facilitation practice?", "options": {"A": "End the discussion and choose the root cause that the majority prefers", "B": "Let the two members settle the issue outside the meeting on their own", "C": "Ask the sponsor to decide which member is right about the root cause", "D": "Refocus both members on the data and the process, not on each other"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Refocus both members on the data and the process, not on each other): Good facilitation moves a personal dispute back to evidence and to the process question the team is there to answer. The team has eight weeks of production data, so the disagreement can be tested rather than argued. Option A ends the discussion without using the evidence. Option B pushes the conflict outside the meeting, and option C asks the sponsor to settle a technical question that the data can answer."}, {"id": 692, "domain": "define", "question": "Customers say they want a hotel check-in that is quick and simple. The project team needs a specification it can test at the front desk during the pilot. Which action best turns that voice of the customer statement into a measurable requirement?", "options": {"A": "Train the front desk staff on friendly service during the morning shift", "B": "Ask customers to describe their check-in experience in their own words", "C": "Define check-in time as 3 minutes or less from arrival to room key", "D": "Add a survey question about check-in to the monthly guest survey"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Define check-in time as 3 minutes or less from arrival to room key): A measurable requirement states what will be observed and the level that counts as success. Option C turns quick and simple into a specific time span with a start and end point that a front desk observer can time. Option A trains behavior without setting a requirement. Option B gathers more voice of the customer input, and option D adds a survey question rather than a specification."}, {"id": 694, "domain": "organization_wide_planning_and_deployment", "question": "A company's Six Sigma effort is stalling because middle managers see the projects as extra work with no reward. Senior leaders want projects to keep flowing and the belts to stay busy on real work. Which deployment action best addresses this problem?", "options": {"A": "Assign more improvement projects to each Black Belt so the workload stays balanced", "B": "Publish a monthly report that ranks managers by project participation", "C": "Move the program under the quality department and remove manager input", "D": "Link project results to the manager's goals and give recognition for support"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Link project results to the manager's goals and give recognition for support): Middle managers support projects when the work counts toward the results they are measured on. Tying project outcomes to their goals makes support part of the job, and recognition reinforces it. Option A adds more work without changing the incentive. Option B uses a ranking report as pressure rather than as alignment. Option C removes manager input, which would weaken the deployment further."}, {"id": 695, "domain": "define", "question": "A project team claims it will save $400,000 this year by cutting rework. The team has three months of rework records and the unit labor rate. Finance asks for the basis of that number. Which method best supports a credible benefit estimate?", "options": {"A": "Multiply verified rework hours by the standard labor rate and annual volume", "B": "Ask each team member to estimate the savings based on past projects", "C": "Use the largest rework figure reported in any month of the year", "D": "Round the estimate up so the improvement project ranks well against the other projects"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Multiply verified rework hours by the standard labor rate and annual volume): A credible benefit estimate ties the savings to measured data and a standard rate that finance already accepts. Using three months of records to set an average, then applying the labor rate and expected annual volume, gives finance a number it can check. Option B relies on opinion. Option C picks the largest month, which overstates the year, and option D adjusts the estimate for ranking reasons rather than for accuracy."}, {"id": 696, "domain": "measure", "question": "A sampling plan accepts a lot only when no defects appear in a sample of 20 parts. If the lot defect rate is 5 percent, what is the probability that the lot is accepted? Assume the sample is random and the lot is large compared with the sample size.", "options": {"A": "About 5 percent, because the defect rate in the lot sets the answer", "B": "About 64 percent, because it is the complement of the acceptance figure", "C": "About 95 percent, because each part has a 95 percent chance of being good", "D": "About 36 percent, based on the chance that all twenty sampled parts pass"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (About 36 percent, based on the chance that all twenty sampled parts pass): The plan accepts the lot only if every sampled part is good, and one part is good with probability 0.95. That makes the acceptance probability 0.95 raised to the twentieth power, which is 0.3585, or about 36 percent. Option A treats the lot defect rate as the answer. Option B takes a complement that has no basis here, and option C applies the single part probability to the whole sample."}, {"id": 701, "domain": "organization_wide_planning_and_deployment", "question": "A regional bank sets an annual goal to halve account opening time, then works backward to quarterly targets, departmental actions, and the measures that will show progress at each level of the organization every month. Which deployment approach does this describe for the bank?", "options": {"A": "Kaizen, which improves a workstation through small rapid team changes.", "B": "Benchmarking, which compares the bank's own results against the best outside performer.", "C": "Hoshin planning, which cascades top-level goals into linked actions and measures.", "D": "Six Sigma DMAIC, which reduces defects in an existing process step."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hoshin planning, which cascades top-level goals into linked actions and measures.): Hoshin planning sets a small number of breakthrough goals and works backward into targets, actions, and measures at each level of the organization. That matches the movement from the annual goal to quarterly targets and departmental actions. Kaizen works at a much smaller scale, benchmarking compares the bank with an outside performer without setting internal targets, and DMAIC fixes an existing process rather than deploying a strategy across the business."}, {"id": 702, "domain": "organization_wide_planning_and_deployment", "question": "A software firm reviews revenue and profit each quarter and nothing else. Its chief executive now worries that long-time clients are quietly moving to a cheaper rival while the sales reports still look healthy. Which balanced scorecard perspective is the firm failing to track?", "options": {"A": "The customer perspective, which tracks satisfaction, retention, and service quality.", "B": "The financial perspective, which tracks revenue, margin, and return on capital employed.", "C": "The internal process perspective, which tracks cycle time and defect rates.", "D": "The learning and growth perspective, which tracks skills, tools, and culture."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The customer perspective, which tracks satisfaction, retention, and service quality.): The balanced scorecard asks leaders to watch several views of the business, and the customer view covers how well the organization keeps and satisfies the people who buy from it. Quietly losing long-time clients is the signal that view is designed to catch. The financial view is the one the firm already watches, the internal process view would show slow or defective work rather than client departures, and the learning and growth view covers employee skills and culture."}, {"id": 704, "domain": "organization_wide_planning_and_deployment", "question": "A hospital improvement council is reviewing six candidate projects with only enough Black Belt time for two of them. Members score each idea on cost of poor quality, strategic fit, risk of failure, and time to see results, then combine the scores into a ranked list. What are they doing?", "options": {"A": "Chartering a single large team to study all six problems together.", "B": "Prioritizing the project portfolio against agreed selection criteria.", "C": "Running a statistical hypothesis test across the six candidate projects.", "D": "Building a control plan for each of the six candidate projects."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Prioritizing the project portfolio against agreed selection criteria.): Portfolio selection scores candidate projects on benefits and feasibility, then ranks them so limited Black Belt time goes to the best options. Combining impact, fit, risk, and speed is that kind of decision. Putting six problems into one team would spread effort thin, a hypothesis test compares data rather than ranking proposals, and a control plan is written after a process change rather than before a project is chosen."}, {"id": 709, "domain": "team_management", "question": "A newly formed cross functional team working on pharmacy dispensing errors has started arguing about who owns each task, and two members openly distrust the data another department shared last week. Which stage of team development has the group reached?", "options": {"A": "Forming, where members are polite and are still learning about the task.", "B": "Norming, where the group has agreed on roles and methods.", "C": "Storming, where members test roles and work through open disagreement.", "D": "Performing, where the group works smoothly toward its shared goal."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Storming, where members test roles and work through open disagreement.): Teams normally move from forming into storming, and storming shows up as conflict about roles, methods, and whose information can be trusted. The arguing and distrust in this team are the classic signs of that stage. Forming is the polite early stage before real work begins, norming comes after the group settles its rules, and performing is the steady stage that follows."}, {"id": 712, "domain": "team_management", "question": "After a brainstorming session produces thirty improvement ideas, the facilitator asks every member to write down the five best ideas privately, then the team tallies the results and discusses the top items. Which tool is the team using at this point?", "options": {"A": "A cause and effect diagram, which sorts possible causes into six groups.", "B": "The nominal group technique, which ranks ideas without group pressure.", "C": "A force field analysis, which weighs driving and restraining forces.", "D": "A process decision program chart, which maps possible countermeasures."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The nominal group technique, which ranks ideas without group pressure.): The nominal group technique asks members to generate and rank ideas in writing before any discussion, which keeps a talkative or senior member from steering the list. The team then tallies the private rankings and discusses the leaders. A cause and effect diagram sorts causes rather than ranking ideas, force field analysis weighs forces for and against a change, and a process decision program chart maps countermeasures for a chosen plan."}, {"id": 714, "domain": "define", "question": "A hospital team trying to explain why patients choose a rival clinic reads complaint letters, runs two focus groups, and tracks how many patients return for follow up care. Why is this mix of sources more useful than a single survey?", "options": {"A": "It combines stated needs with observed behavior, giving a fuller customer view.", "B": "It removes the need to define measurable customer requirements and targets later.", "C": "It shows which single complaint category matters most to the patients.", "D": "It removes the influence that interviewers have on the answers given."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It combines stated needs with observed behavior, giving a fuller customer view.): Voice of the customer work is stronger when it pairs what customers say with what they actually do, so complaint letters and focus groups supply stated needs while the return rate supplies behavior. Together they show where the service fails and how much it matters. A single survey would capture only stated opinion, and no mix of sources removes the duty to translate needs into measurable requirements."}, {"id": 722, "domain": "measure", "question": "In a gauge study at a bottle plant, two operators each measure ten bottles twice. The gauge variation works out to 0.4 grams and the total study variation to 0.8 grams. What is the percent gauge repeatability and reproducibility for this measurement system?", "options": {"A": "Two hundred percent, which divides the total variation by the gauge variation.", "B": "Sixty seven percent, which divides total variation by the sum of both variances.", "C": "Fifty percent, which divides 0.4 by 0.8 and multiplies the result by 100.", "D": "Twenty five percent, which divides gauge variation by twice the total variation."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Fifty percent, which divides 0.4 by 0.8 and multiplies the result by 100.): Percent gauge repeatability and reproducibility compares the variation the measurement system adds with the total variation the study sees, so 0.4 divided by 0.8 equals 0.5, and 0.5 times 100 gives 50 percent. Common guidance treats a value under 10 percent as acceptable and a value above 30 percent as needing work, so 50 percent points to a measurement system that needs attention. Dividing total variation by gauge variation inverts the ratio, and the other options divide by the wrong quantity."}, {"id": 724, "domain": "measure", "question": "A bottling line histogram of 100 fill weights uses specification limits of 97 and 103 grams. The chart shows four fills below 97 grams and five fills above 103, with the rest inside the limits. What does the chart show?", "options": {"A": "Five percent of the fills are outside the specification limits.", "B": "The process is centered on the target, so it is a capable process.", "C": "The distribution is skewed, with a longer tail on the low side.", "D": "Nine percent of the fills are outside the specification limits."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Nine percent of the fills are outside the specification limits.): Four fills sit below the lower limit and five sit above the upper limit, so nine of the hundred fills fall outside the limits, which is 9 percent. That rate is far above what a capable process should produce. The five percent figure counts only the high side, centering the peak near the target does not make the process capable while nine fills sit outside the limits, and the two tails are close enough in size that the shape is not described as skewed.", "image": "question_images/q_724.png"}, {"id": 725, "domain": "measure", "question": "A box plot compares sample hold times across three shifts in minutes. The evening shift has a median of 9, an upper quartile of 12, a top whisker at 15, and one plotted point at 19. What should the team conclude from the chart?", "options": {"A": "The evening shift has an unusual high value that the team should investigate.", "B": "The evening shift distribution is centered noticeably lower than the other two shifts.", "C": "The measurement system is at fault because one value looks unusual.", "D": "The evening shift chart should have its top whisker shortened."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The evening shift has an unusual high value that the team should investigate.): A box plot draws the whiskers to the most extreme values inside the calculated fences, and a point beyond them is plotted on its own as a possible outlier. One high value of 19 minutes deserves a look at the records to see whether it is a real event or a data error. The evening median of 9 is the highest of the three shifts, so the center is not low, and a plotted point is a signal to investigate rather than proof of a faulty gauge or a reason to trim the chart.", "image": "question_images/q_725.png"}, {"id": 726, "domain": "measure", "question": "A quality engineer wants to estimate the average bag weight on a line that runs three shifts with different operators, so she draws a random sample inside each shift in proportion to that shift's output. Which sampling plan is this?", "options": {"A": "A simple random sample, which selects the required bags from one combined shipping list.", "B": "A systematic sample, which picks every tenth bag from the line.", "C": "A stratified random sample, which samples each shift in proportion to output.", "D": "A convenience sample, which uses the bags nearest the packing station."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A stratified random sample, which samples each shift in proportion to output.): A stratified sample splits the population into groups that may behave differently, then draws randomly inside each group, which keeps every shift represented in the result. That matters here because the shifts run with different operators. A simple random sample ignores the shift structure, a systematic sample follows a fixed interval along the line, and a convenience sample uses whichever bags are easiest to reach."}, {"id": 729, "domain": "measure", "question": "A supplier reports 3.4 defects per million opportunities on a new connector line. The customer asks the Black Belt to state the process sigma level using the usual Six Sigma shift convention. What should the Black Belt report on the scorecard for the supplier?", "options": {"A": "Four sigma, since the defect rate is well under one percent.", "B": "Five sigma, since the shift adds 1.5 to the short term value.", "C": "Three sigma, since 3.4 defects per million is a small daily total.", "D": "Six sigma, since 3.4 defects per million equals that level."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Six sigma, since 3.4 defects per million equals that level.): The sigma scale links a defect rate per million opportunities to a sigma level, and 3.4 defects per million is the long term benchmark for six sigma. That benchmark comes from the usual 1.5 sigma shift, which allows for the drift a stable process shows over the long term. The supplier reported 3.4 defects per million opportunities, so the level is six sigma, and the other options name levels that do not match that rate."}, {"id": 730, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A scatter diagram plots fill weight against line speed for six batches. Points fall in a tight band that slopes downward from left to right, and the correlation is about minus 0.99. What does the chart show about the two variables?", "options": {"A": "A strong positive association, so faster lines fill each container much more heavily overall.", "B": "No real association, because both variables are measured in different units.", "C": "A strong negative association, so higher line speeds come with lower fill weights.", "D": "A proven cause, because the line speed was changed before the fill weight."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A strong negative association, so higher line speeds come with lower fill weights.): A correlation near minus one describes two variables that move in opposite directions inside a tight band, which is what the downward slope shows. The pattern gives the team a strong candidate for further study. A positive association would slope upward, different units do not erase an association, and a scatter diagram shows a relationship but cannot establish that speed caused the change, because other factors could move along with speed.", "image": "question_images/q_730.png"}, {"id": 734, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A market research team sorts 500 survey responses into three age bands and two preference groups, then asks whether a person's preference is related to that person's age band. Which statistical test should the team use at this stage of the study?", "options": {"A": "A two sample t test of the difference between two average scores.", "B": "A one way analysis of variance of the three different age band averages.", "C": "A chi square test of independence between two categorical variables.", "D": "A gauge repeatability study of the survey question wording effects."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A chi square test of independence between two categorical variables.): Both variables here are counts in categories, age band and preference group, and the question asks whether the two classifications are related. A chi square test of independence compares the counts observed in each cell with the counts expected if the variables were unrelated. The t test and analysis of variance compare averages that this data does not contain, and a gauge study examines a measurement system rather than a relationship between survey variables."}, {"id": 741, "domain": "improve", "question": "A plant improves three workstations, yet total shipments do not rise because the painting booth keeps a long queue of waiting parts and runs behind schedule each afternoon. Where should the team focus the next improvement effort to raise output?", "options": {"A": "On the three improved stations, which now have spare capacity to use.", "B": "On the shipping dock, which handles the finished parts leaving the plant.", "C": "On every station at once, so that no station holds back the others.", "D": "On the painting booth, which limits the output of the entire plant."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (On the painting booth, which limits the output of the entire plant.): Under the theory of constraints, the throughput of a system is set by its bottleneck, so improving stations that are not the bottleneck adds cost without adding shipments. The painting queue is holding back parts that would otherwise leave the plant, so it deserves the next improvement effort. Adding capacity at stations with spare time, improving the dock, or spreading effort across all stations leaves the real limit untouched."}, {"id": 742, "domain": "improve", "question": "A two-factor experiment tests temperature at 150 and 170 degrees and a catalyst at two levels. The four runs give responses of 42.0, 48.5, 44.0, and 50.5, and the temperature effect is 6.5. What does that effect mean in this study?", "options": {"A": "The high temperature run produced the single largest response of 50.5.", "B": "Temperature accounts for 6.5 percent of the variation in the response.", "C": "Raising temperature from low to high lifts the average response by 6.5 units.", "D": "The two factors interact, so the temperature effect changes with the catalyst level."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Raising temperature from low to high lifts the average response by 6.5 units.): A main effect is the average change in the response when a factor moves from its low level to its high level, averaged over the levels of the other factors. The runs at 170 degrees average 49.5 and the runs at 150 degrees average 43.0, so the difference is 6.5. The largest single run, the share of variation, and an interaction between factors are different ideas that this number does not measure.", "image": "question_images/q_742.png"}, {"id": 744, "domain": "control", "question": "A hospital records whether each of about 200 daily admissions had at least one paperwork error, then plots the daily proportion of admissions with errors against the calendar day of the study. Which control chart fits this data for this measure?", "options": {"A": "An X bar and R chart, which tracks averages and ranges of measurements.", "B": "A p chart, which tracks the proportion of defective units in each subgroup.", "C": "An individuals and moving range chart, which plots single readings over time.", "D": "A c chart, which counts defects in a constant area of opportunity."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A p chart, which tracks the proportion of defective units in each subgroup.): The hospital records whether each admission had an error, which makes each admission a unit that either passes or fails, and the daily proportion of failures is plotted on a p chart. A p chart handles subgroups that change in size, which suits daily admission counts. The X bar and R chart needs measured values, the individuals chart plots single measurements, and a c chart counts defects in a constant area rather than a proportion."}, {"id": 745, "domain": "control", "question": "A supervisor sees points bouncing inside the control limits and asks the team to adjust the machine after every reading to remove the natural variation in the process. What should the Black Belt explain to the supervisor about this request?", "options": {"A": "The variation is special cause, so the machine needs an immediate overhaul of its setup.", "B": "The limits are set too wide, so the team should tighten them first.", "C": "The variation is common cause, so adjusting after every reading would add trouble.", "D": "The readings are out of control, so the process must be stopped today."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The variation is common cause, so adjusting after every reading would add trouble.): Points that stay inside the control limits are behaving as a stable process should, and that variation belongs to the system rather than to any single reading or operator. Reacting to each reading is tampering, and it usually widens variation instead of narrowing it. Special cause appears as a point outside a limit or a nonrandom pattern, control limits come from the process data rather than from a preference, and a stable chart does not justify stopping the line."}, {"id": 753, "domain": "measure", "question": "A bottling line fills 200 bottles during a shift. The histogram shows fill volumes against a lower limit of 495 mL and an upper limit of 505 mL. The Black Belt counts the bars that sit outside the limits. What does the chart show about the fill process?", "options": {"A": "The fill process is centered on the target and scattered quite well within both limits.", "B": "The distribution is roughly centered, but 17 of 200 bottles sit outside the limits.", "C": "The distribution is far off center, with most bottles below the lower limit.", "D": "The spread is too narrow, so the process needs wider control limits."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The distribution is roughly centered, but 17 of 200 bottles sit outside the limits.): The bars peak near 500 mL, so the fill process is roughly centered, yet 8 bottles fall below 495 mL and 9 rise above 505 mL. That total of 17 out of 200 is 8.5 percent outside the specification limits. The other options deny the out-of-spec bottles, misread the direction of the shift, or confuse specification limits with control limits, which come from the process itself.", "image": "question_images/q_753.png"}, {"id": 757, "domain": "improve", "question": "A claims processing team needs 6 working days to finish a batch of 300 claims, and each claim spends only 20 minutes of that time being worked. The manager wants faster output without adding staff. Which improvement target offers the largest gain here?", "options": {"A": "Tighten the accuracy audit standard for every processed claim before release.", "B": "Buy faster desktop computers so each clerk can key claims more quickly.", "C": "Add a second review layer so errors get caught before the claim leaves.", "D": "Cut the waiting time between the steps where claims sit untouched."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cut the waiting time between the steps where claims sit untouched.): Six working days is 2,880 minutes, and the claim is worked for only 20 of them, so about 99 percent of the lead time is waiting. Speeding up keying or adding a review layer touches the small part of the time, and a tighter audit standard can add more delay. Removing queue time attacks the largest share of the lead time and raises output without more staff."}, {"id": 758, "domain": "control", "question": "A team improves a soldering process and writes a control plan. The plan lists the solder temperature as the key input, with a target, a tolerance, a check frequency, and a named owner. The Black Belt asks what the plan still needs so operators respond the same way to a shift?", "options": {"A": "A list of every defect the team removed during the improvement phase.", "B": "A reaction plan naming the response when a reading goes out of limits.", "C": "A revised project charter that records the new process capability data.", "D": "A copy of the improvement data saved on the team's shared drive."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A reaction plan naming the response when a reading goes out of limits.): A control plan needs a reaction step that tells the operator what to do, and whom to tell, when a reading leaves the control limits, or each person invents a different response. The defect list, the revised charter, and the saved data file are project records, not live instructions that guide the process after handover."}, {"id": 762, "domain": "define", "question": "A hotel chain wants to improve check-in. Guests mention three things: a fast check-in, a friendly greeting, and a free room upgrade. The team finds that guests only notice the greeting when it is missing. Which Kano category fits the friendly greeting?", "options": {"A": "A delight attribute, because guests enjoy it and it lifts satisfaction.", "B": "A must-be attribute, because its absence causes strong dissatisfaction.", "C": "A performance attribute, because more friendliness raises satisfaction steadily.", "D": "A reverse attribute, because some guests prefer a quick silent check-in."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A must-be attribute, because its absence causes strong dissatisfaction.): A must-be attribute is taken for granted when it is present and draws complaints when it is missing, which matches a greeting that only gets noticed when it disappears. A delight attribute would raise satisfaction when delivered, and a performance attribute would improve satisfaction in step with how well it is done. Neither of those patterns fits a feature that only registers when it is absent."}, {"id": 763, "domain": "measure", "question": "Two inspectors measure the same 10 parts twice each. The averages are close, but the repeat readings from each inspector differ by 0.8 mm while the part-to-part spread is 0.6 mm. Which conclusion about the measurement system is correct in this study?", "options": {"A": "The system is acceptable because the two inspectors average the same.", "B": "The system needs a wider specification limit so the readings can fit inside it.", "C": "The system is fine because repeatability is smaller than the tolerance.", "D": "The system is poor because the repeat variation swamps the part variation."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The system is poor because the repeat variation swamps the part variation.): Repeatability should be small next to the part-to-part spread so the gauge can separate good parts from bad, and here 0.8 mm of repeat variation is larger than the 0.6 mm between parts, so much of what the gauge reports is noise. Matching averages only shows that reproducibility is fine and says nothing about repeatability. Widening the specification hides the problem, and a tolerance comparison belongs to capability rather than gauge acceptance."}, {"id": 766, "domain": "organizational_process_management_and_measures", "question": "A finance team finds that each warranty repair costs $200 in parts and labor. Last year the company paid for 4,000 of these repairs. The Black Belt is asked which category of quality cost this spending represents for the plant?", "options": {"A": "Prevention cost, because the repair work prevents future failures.", "B": "Appraisal cost, because each repair is inspected before it ships.", "C": "External failure cost, because the defect reached the customer.", "D": "Internal failure cost, because the repair happens inside the company."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (External failure cost, because the defect reached the customer.): Costs of poor quality split into prevention, appraisal, internal failure, and external failure, and a warranty repair is an external failure because the customer received the faulty product before the problem was found. The yearly spend is 4,000 repairs times $200, which equals $800,000. Internal failure covers defects caught before shipment, appraisal covers inspection and testing, and prevention covers work that stops defects from happening."}, {"id": 771, "domain": "organization_wide_planning_and_deployment", "question": "A hospital wants to cut central line infections and learns that a rival hospital has the lowest rate in the region. The Black Belt is asked how to use this information without copying the wrong things. Which benchmarking approach is sound?", "options": {"A": "Adopt the rival's exact procedures and then expect to get the same infection rate.", "B": "Report the rival's rate to staff and ask them to work harder.", "C": "Study the rival's practices, then adapt the ones that fit this hospital.", "D": "Wait until the rival publishes a full set of outcome data."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Study the rival's practices, then adapt the ones that fit this hospital.): Benchmarking compares practices and results, and the useful step is to understand how the better performer works, then adapt those methods to local conditions and constraints. Copying procedures without context assumes the two sites are identical, sharing a number does not change a process, and waiting for more data delays learning that is already available."}, {"id": 776, "domain": "control", "question": "A hospital tracks the share of hand hygiene observations that were done correctly on each unit. The number of observations differs from day to day, and the team wants to chart the proportion over time. Which control chart fits this data?", "options": {"A": "An X-bar and R chart, because the data are measured in pairs.", "B": "A c chart, because each day counts a fixed number of defects.", "C": "An individuals and moving range chart, because each day gives one value.", "D": "A p chart, because it plots a proportion with a varying sample size."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A p chart, because it plots a proportion with a varying sample size.): Attribute data recorded as a share of units that pass need a p chart, and the p chart is built for the case where the number inspected changes from day to day. X-bar and R charts and individuals charts are for measured values such as length or weight, and a c chart counts defects in a constant-sized unit."}, {"id": 777, "domain": "define", "question": "A regional bank has four candidate projects: cut statement errors, replace the phone system, add a new branch, and redesign the intranet. Leadership asks the Black Belt which project best fits a Six Sigma improvement portfolio at the bank this year?", "options": {"A": "The statement error project, because it cuts defects in an existing process.", "B": "The branch project, because it adds new capacity for future growth.", "C": "The phone system project, because new hardware removes the old problems.", "D": "The intranet project, because better communication improves every department."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The statement error project, because it cuts defects in an existing process.): Six Sigma projects target defects in processes that already run, so a project to cut statement errors has a measurable defect rate, a known process, and a clear before and after. Buying hardware, adding a branch, and redesigning an intranet are capital or information technology projects whose outcomes are harder to express as defect reduction."}, {"id": 778, "domain": "measure", "question": "A process produces 4,500 defects per million opportunities, a rate the team measures over three months. Using the common 1.5 sigma shift convention, the Black Belt converts this to a sigma level. Which sigma level is closest to this defect rate?", "options": {"A": "About 3.0 sigma, because the rate is far below the six sigma target.", "B": "About 3.5 sigma, because the rate sits near twenty thousand per million.", "C": "About 4.0 sigma, because the rate is nearest six thousand two hundred.", "D": "About 4.5 sigma, because the rate is nearest one thousand three hundred."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 4.0 sigma, because the rate is nearest six thousand two hundred.): A table with the 1.5 sigma shift puts 4.0 sigma at about 6,210 defects per million and 4.5 sigma at about 1,350, so 4,500 sits nearer the 4.0 row, about 1,710 away against 3,150. The 3.0 row sits near 66,807 defects and the 3.5 row near 22,750, both far from the measured rate. The 4.5 row is closer in sigma number but describes a much lower defect rate."}, {"id": 780, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A hospital's pharmacy team wants to cut late medication deliveries on the evening shift. They build a fishbone with the standard six branches and place causes under each one. Which use of the diagram will most improve the team's analysis of the problem?", "options": {"A": "Lay out the causes under all six branches, then test the likely ones with data.", "B": "Vote on the branch the team likes best and study only that one.", "C": "Treat every cause that appears on the diagram as proven until the data say otherwise.", "D": "Move the diagram into the control plan and stop the analysis there."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Lay out the causes under all six branches, then test the likely ones with data.): A cause and effect diagram lists many possible causes, so the team should keep the full set visible and then use data to separate the real drivers from the guesses. Choosing one branch by preference skips the search, treating every entry as proven skips the test, and filing the diagram in a control plan ends the work before any cause is confirmed.", "image": "question_images/q_780.png"}, {"id": 781, "domain": "organization_wide_planning_and_deployment", "question": "A manufacturing company spends $300,000 a year on inspection and testing, its largest appraisal activity, plus $900,000 on warranty claims. A training program that would prevent defects costs $50,000. Which statement about this spending pattern is correct in the coming year?", "options": {"A": "Appraisal spending is the single largest category, so quality costs are under control.", "B": "Prevention spending is high, which explains the size of the warranty bill.", "C": "Failure costs are far larger than prevention costs, so shifting money is worthwhile.", "D": "The three amounts are balanced, so no change in the quality budget is needed."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Failure costs are far larger than prevention costs, so shifting money is worthwhile.): Warranty claims of $900,000 sit in the external failure category and dwarf the $50,000 prevention item, which is a sign that money is spent after defects happen instead of before. Appraisal spending of $300,000 is not the largest amount once failure costs are counted, prevention spending is small rather than high, and the three figures are far apart rather than balanced."}, {"id": 786, "domain": "control", "question": "A packing line charts subgroup averages with a center line of 12.0, an upper limit of 13.0, and a lower limit of 11.0. The chart shows one point at 13.6 in subgroup 7. What does the chart signal to the team?", "options": {"A": "The process is stable because the other nine points sit inside the limits.", "B": "The variation is common cause, so the team should leave the line alone.", "C": "The specification is too tight and the upper limit should be raised.", "D": "A special cause is present, so the team should find its source."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A special cause is present, so the team should find its source.): A point beyond a control limit is the standard signal of a special cause, and at 13.6 it sits well above the upper limit of 13.0, so the team should search for an assignable source such as a material lot, an operator, or a setup change. One proven special cause does not leave the process stable, and a single point does not make the variation common cause. Control limits come from the process and are not loosened to hide a point.", "image": "question_images/q_786.png"}, {"id": 789, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A team finds that scratches cause most customer returns on a metal panel line. Asking why five times leads from the scratches to a worn guide rail, and then to a missing preventive maintenance task. Which analysis approach did the team use here?", "options": {"A": "A Pareto analysis that ranks defect types by their counts.", "B": "A five whys chain that moves from the symptom to a root cause.", "C": "An FMEA that scores severity, occurrence, and detection for each failure.", "D": "A control chart that separates common cause from special cause variation."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A five whys chain that moves from the symptom to a root cause.): The five whys method asks why in sequence, peeling back layers from the visible symptom until the team reaches a cause it can act on, which is how scratches led to the missing maintenance task. Pareto analysis ranks problems but does not dig into one chain, FMEA scores risks before failures happen, and a control chart monitors variation over time."}, {"id": 790, "domain": "team_management", "question": "A Black Belt leads a project team whose members work in three different time zones. Attendance at the live meeting drops, and decisions get remade because absent members were not consulted. Which practice best improves the team's decision quality going forward?", "options": {"A": "Publish each decision with its rationale and give absent members a set response window.", "B": "Require every team member to attend the live meeting at the scheduled starting hour each week.", "C": "Let the members who attend make the decisions and inform the rest later.", "D": "Move all of the team decisions to email and cancel the regular live meetings."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Publish each decision with its rationale and give absent members a set response window.): When some members cannot join live, recording the decision, the reasoning, and an agreed window for objections keeps everyone in the loop and stops the same item from being reopened. Forcing attendance across time zones strains the team, and deciding without the absent members or dropping meetings loses the input that makes a decision stick."}, {"id": 796, "domain": "improve", "question": "A clinic department has a small, contained problem with appointment scheduling, and the likely fix is clear. The change touches one team and can be tested in a week. Leadership asks whether to run a full DMAIC project or a quicker approach. Which choice fits best here?", "options": {"A": "Run a full DMAIC project because every improvement needs the full roadmap.", "B": "Take no action until the department can fund a Black Belt project.", "C": "Launch a design project because the process does not exist yet.", "D": "Run a kaizen event that tests the fix in a short, focused session."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Run a kaizen event that tests the fix in a short, focused session.): When the scope is small, the cause is understood, and one team can act quickly, a kaizen event delivers the change in days instead of months, which is what the DMAIC roadmap is scaled for. Claiming that every improvement needs the full roadmap overstates the method, waiting for funding delays a simple fix, and the process clearly exists, so a design project does not apply."}, {"id": 797, "domain": "control", "question": "A team wants to report Cpk for a filling process, but the control chart shows several points beyond the limits and a run of eight points above the center line. Which step should come before any capability calculation for the process?", "options": {"A": "Calculate Cpk with the out-of-control points removed from the process data set.", "B": "Calculate Cp instead, because Cp does not depend on whether the process is stable.", "C": "Widen the control limits until every plotted point falls inside them.", "D": "Find and remove the special causes, then confirm the process is stable."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Find and remove the special causes, then confirm the process is stable.): A capability index describes a stable process, so special causes must be found and removed first and the chart must then show only common-cause variation. Removing inconvenient points or widening the limits hides the problem instead of solving it. Cp assumes stability just as much as Cpk does."}, {"id": 800, "domain": "improve", "question": "A team has tested a new workflow in one department and it met the target. Before rolling it out to 12 sites, the Black Belt is asked what the next step should be. Which step best reduces risk for the rollout?", "options": {"A": "Send the new procedure to all 12 sites on the same day.", "B": "Wait a full year to see whether the pharmacy department keeps the gain.", "C": "Run a pilot at a second site with different conditions and review the results.", "D": "Train all of the supervisors at once so the change arrives at every site together."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Run a pilot at a second site with different conditions and review the results.): A second pilot at a site with different conditions tests whether the fix depends on the first site's people, equipment, or volume, which is the main risk in a wide rollout. Launching everywhere at once multiplies the cost of a failure, waiting a year delays a proven gain, and training everyone before the process is confirmed can spread a faulty method."}, {"id": 804, "domain": "organization_wide_planning_and_deployment", "question": "A bank will replace a manual loan review with an automated scoring tool. The Black Belt's team has built the tool and the training deck, but two regional managers have told staff the change is optional. What should the Black Belt do first?", "options": {"A": "Escalate the two managers to the executive sponsor and ask for a written directive", "B": "Move the pilot to a region where the managers already support the change", "C": "Add more training sessions so staff can see how the tool works", "D": "Run a stakeholder analysis and readiness assessment, then plan the communication"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Run a stakeholder analysis and readiness assessment, then plan the communication): Change management in the body of knowledge starts with stakeholder analysis and a readiness assessment, which show who can block the change and what they need to hear. Escalating first uses authority before the cause of the resistance is understood. Moving the pilot hides the problem instead of solving it, and more training does not answer managers who call the change optional."}, {"id": 807, "domain": "organizational_process_management_and_measures", "question": "A project team reports 200,000 dollars of benefits. Of that sum, 150,000 dollars comes from avoided overtime that the finance team has already removed from next year's budget, and 50,000 dollars comes from a survey estimate of improved customer goodwill. What should the Black Belt say?", "options": {"A": "Both figures are hard savings because both of them appear in the project benefit file", "B": "Both figures are soft benefits because neither was traced on a control chart", "C": "The overtime figure is a hard saving, and the goodwill figure is a soft benefit", "D": "The goodwill figure is hard because finance removed it from the budget"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The overtime figure is a hard saving, and the goodwill figure is a soft benefit): Hard savings come from profit and loss accounts, and the overtime money has already left the budget, so finance can count it. Soft benefits such as estimated goodwill are real but harder to verify. Calling both figures hard overstates the case, and calling both soft understates it. Treating the goodwill estimate as hard because of a budget entry mixes the two categories."}, {"id": 811, "domain": "team_management", "question": "In a design review, the plant's senior engineer interrupts every proposal with a reason it cannot work. Other members have stopped offering ideas, and the meeting ends early. Which technique should the Black Belt use to fix the group dynamic?", "options": {"A": "Let the engineer speak first each time so the objections arrive early", "B": "Move the discussion to a written ballot so no one speaks during review", "C": "Meet the engineer privately, then set a ground rule that every idea gets airtime", "D": "Replace the engineer with a team member who has fewer years of plant floor experience"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Meet the engineer privately, then set a ground rule that every idea gets airtime): A dominant participant can shut down a group, and the body of knowledge lists intervention as a conflict tool. A private conversation avoids a public fight, and a ground rule on airtime protects the quieter members. Letting the engineer speak first rewards the interruption. A silent ballot removes the discussion that a design review needs."}, {"id": 814, "domain": "define", "question": "A payroll project will change how timesheets flow from plant supervisors to the payroll department. The sponsor asks the Black Belt to list everyone the change will affect. Besides the employees paid, who else should be treated as a customer?", "options": {"A": "Only the employees whose paychecks arrive on time each pay period", "B": "Only the payroll clerks who run the process and fix the errors", "C": "The software vendor that supplies the payroll system and its license team", "D": "The plant supervisors and the payroll clerks who both feed the process"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The plant supervisors and the payroll clerks who both feed the process): Customers include the internal groups whose work depends on the process, so the supervisors who submit timesheets and the clerks who receive them both count. The employees paid are also customers, but naming only them misses the internal chain. The software vendor supplies the tool rather than using the process."}, {"id": 816, "domain": "define", "question": "The exhibit summarizes customer interviews for a meal delivery service. It lists the customers interviewed, the question that was asked, and one direct quote about cold food arriving late. The team asks what the next Define step should be with this input.", "options": {"A": "Translate the quoted needs into measurable customer requirements for the project", "B": "Ask the same interviewees to repeat the interview so the quotes can be reconfirmed", "C": "Move straight to the measure phase and collect delivery time data by route", "D": "Send the quotes to the marketing team so the service can be advertised better"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Translate the quoted needs into measurable customer requirements for the project): Customer data collection in the Define phase ends by turning what customers said into requirements the team can measure. Repeating the interviews adds no new information. Jumping to the measure phase skips the translation step, and sending the quotes to marketing uses the data for promotion rather than for the project.", "image": "question_images/q_816.png"}, {"id": 819, "domain": "define", "question": "A project to cut invoice errors is in its fourth month. The sales director now asks the team to also fix the pricing master file, a system the charter never mentioned. What should the Black Belt do about this request?", "options": {"A": "Add the pricing work to the team backlog and keep the current deadline", "B": "Assign the pricing request to a team member who has spare time this month", "C": "Take the request to the sponsor so the scope and charter can be reviewed", "D": "Refuse the request and tell the director that the charter cannot change"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Take the request to the sponsor so the scope and charter can be reviewed): The body of knowledge calls for periodic charter reviews with stakeholders, which is how a change in scope gets an owner's decision. Adding the work quietly abandons the charter the sponsor approved, and assigning it to spare time does the same. Refusing outright blocks a request that the sponsor may well accept."}, {"id": 820, "domain": "measure", "question": "A utility measures daily gas usage for six weeks in January to set a planning baseline. The analyst plans to use the average as the yearly figure. What data collection problem should the Black Belt point out before the figure is published?", "options": {"A": "The sample covers one season, so it may not represent usage across the year", "B": "The sample is much too large for a utility that serves many thousands of homes", "C": "The sample mixes two data types, since gas usage is measured in therms", "D": "The sample was taken daily, when only monthly totals are useful to planners"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The sample covers one season, so it may not represent usage across the year): Data collection plans have to account for seasonality, and January gas use in a cold month is a poor stand-in for a yearly average. Sample size is not the problem, since six weeks of daily readings give plenty of points. Gas usage is one measurement type, and daily readings can be summed into monthly totals."}, {"id": 822, "domain": "measure", "question": "A plant compares variation in two characteristics. Fill weight has a standard deviation of 2 grams on a mean of 200 grams, and cap torque has a standard deviation of 0.6 newton meters on a mean of 6. Which measure lets the team compare the two fairly?", "options": {"A": "The coefficient of variation, found by dividing each standard deviation by its mean", "B": "The full range, found by subtracting the smallest single reading from the largest reading", "C": "The median, found by arranging each data set and taking the middle value", "D": "The count, found by adding up all of the readings in the two sets"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The coefficient of variation, found by dividing each standard deviation by its mean): The coefficient of variation expresses spread as a share of the mean, so it lets a team compare measures that use different units. Fill weight gives 2 divided by 200, or 1 percent, and cap torque gives 0.6 divided by 6, or 10 percent. The range, the median, and the count do not put the two spreads on a common scale."}, {"id": 826, "domain": "measure", "question": "A maintenance team records the time between failures of a pump. Many failures happen soon after a repair, and the times form a long right tail. The team wants a distribution that models time to failure. Which family fits their data?", "options": {"A": "The binomial distribution, which counts successes in a fixed number of trials", "B": "The Poisson distribution, which counts rare events in a fixed interval of time", "C": "The chi square distribution, which compares the observed counts with the expected counts", "D": "The Weibull distribution, which models time to failure with changing hazard rates"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The Weibull distribution, which models time to failure with changing hazard rates): The body of knowledge lists the Weibull among the distributions for reliability data, and its shape parameter describes a failure rate that rises or falls over time. The binomial counts successes in a fixed number of trials and the Poisson counts rare events in an interval, so neither models time to failure. The chi square distribution compares observed and expected counts."}, {"id": 828, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A regional manager charts each plant's defect count against the number of inspectors and finds a strong positive correlation. The manager concludes that adding inspectors causes more defects. What error does this conclusion make about the relationship between the two variables?", "options": {"A": "The manager used the wrong correlation formula for the number of inspectors", "B": "The manager treated two categorical counts as if they were continuous measures", "C": "The manager read causation into a correlation that could run either direction", "D": "The manager compared plants of different sizes without adjusting for volume"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The manager read causation into a correlation that could run either direction): A correlation of plus 0.9 says the two counts move together, and the cause can run the other way, with plants that find more defects hiring more inspectors. The formula is not the problem, since the correlation comes from the data. Both counts are numerical, and plant size is one possible explanation, but the core error is the jump from a correlation to a cause."}, {"id": 831, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A team tests whether a new seal reduces leaks. The team sets alpha at 0.05, and the test returns a p-value of 0.03, so the team rejects the null hypothesis. What risk did the 0.05 setting control in this decision?", "options": {"A": "The chance of declaring an effect when the process has not actually changed", "B": "The chance of missing a real effect that the test should have found", "C": "The chance that the sample mean differs from the true process mean", "D": "The chance that the p-value would change if this test is run again next week"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The chance of declaring an effect when the process has not actually changed): Alpha is the chance of a type I error, which means rejecting the null hypothesis when it is true, so the 0.05 setting limits how often the team claims an improvement that is not real. Missing a real effect is a type II error, which beta controls. The distance between the sample mean and the process mean is sampling error, and the p-value will move with new data for reasons this setting does not control."}, {"id": 832, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A plant compares three suppliers on four correlated strength measures at the same time. Running four separate tests would raise the chance of at least one false alarm. Which analysis fits this situation and keeps the overall error rate under control?", "options": {"A": "A one-way analysis of variance run separately on each of the four strength measures", "B": "A multivariate analysis of variance that tests the four measures together", "C": "A chi square test that compares the observed and expected defect counts", "D": "A simple linear regression of strength on the supplier grouping code"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A multivariate analysis of variance that tests the four measures together): A multivariate analysis of variance, or MANOVA, compares groups on several correlated responses at once and holds the overall error rate in line. Four separate one-way tests would raise the chance of at least one false alarm. A chi square test works with counts, and a regression on a grouping code treats the categories as numbers."}, {"id": 833, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A design team studies how a new valve could fail before any production tooling is built, and a manufacturing team studies how the assembly line could install the valve backward. What does the body of knowledge call these two studies?", "options": {"A": "A fault tree study and a Pareto study of the same valve design", "B": "A design review for the valve and a process capability study for the line", "C": "A design FMEA for the valve and a process FMEA for the assembly line", "D": "A process FMEA for the valve and a design FMEA for the assembly line"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A design FMEA for the valve and a process FMEA for the assembly line): A design FMEA studies how a product can fail before the design is frozen, and a process FMEA studies how the manufacturing process can fail. The line that installs the valve backward is a process concern, so swapping the two names in option D is wrong. A fault tree, a Pareto chart, a design review, and a capability study are different tools with different purposes."}, {"id": 841, "domain": "improve", "question": "An assembly line installs two similar bolts. One proposal adds a sensor that flags a wrong bolt after the bracket is already fixed, and another enlarges the hole so only the correct bolt fits. Which proposal gives the stronger error proofing?", "options": {"A": "The sensor, since it records each wrong bolt for later review by the team", "B": "The sensor, since detection tools cost less than changing the part design", "C": "Both proposals give the same protection, since each one catches wrong bolts", "D": "The enlarged hole, since prevention stops the error before it can happen"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The enlarged hole, since prevention stops the error before it can happen): Error proofing is strongest when the wrong action becomes physically impossible, which is what the redesigned hole does. A sensor that flags a wrong bolt after the bracket is fixed detects the error after the work is done, so the part still needs rework. Detection and prevention are not equally strong, and the sensor option carries no cost advantage that changes the ranking."}, {"id": 842, "domain": "improve", "question": "A team must screen six factors and can afford only sixteen runs. The Black Belt explains that some two-factor interactions will be entangled with each other in the chosen plan. Which design principle explains this trade to the project sponsor?", "options": {"A": "Randomization, which protects the experiment against the order of the runs", "B": "Blocking, which groups runs that share an unmeasured nuisance factor", "C": "Resolution, which describes the aliasing built into a fractional design", "D": "Replication, which repeats each treatment to give a better error estimate"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Resolution, which describes the aliasing built into a fractional design): A fractional factorial runs only part of the full set of combinations, so some effects are aliased, or entangled, with each other, and the resolution of the design states how severe that entanglement is. Six factors in a full two-level factorial would need sixty-four runs, so a sixteen-run plan is a quarter fraction. A design of resolution IV keeps main effects clear while letting some two-factor interactions share an estimate. Randomization, blocking, and replication are protections in a design, but none describes which effects are entangled."}, {"id": 849, "domain": "design_for_six_sigma", "question": "A company is creating a new logistics service. The team has defined requirements, measured what matters, and analyzed options, and it has produced a design. Stakeholders now want the design pushed to its best settings before launch. Which DFSS roadmap adds that step?", "options": {"A": "DMADV, which defines, measures, analyzes, designs, and validates the service", "B": "DMADOV, which adds an optimize step between the design and validate phases", "C": "DMAIC, which defines, measures, analyzes, improves, and controls the service", "D": "PDCA, which plans, does, checks, and acts on the service in repeated cycles"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (DMADOV, which adds an optimize step between the design and validate phases): DMADOV extends the design roadmap with an optimization step, which is what the stakeholders are asking for before launch. DMADV stops at validate, so it does not include that step. DMAIC improves an existing process rather than designing a new service, and PDCA is a general improvement cycle rather than a DFSS roadmap."}, {"id": 850, "domain": "design_for_six_sigma", "question": "A design team is choosing between two pump layouts. Layout one needs a full teardown to reach the seal, while layout two lets a technician replace the seal through a side panel in ten minutes. Which design for X goal does layout two serve?", "options": {"A": "Design for cost, which reduces the number of parts in the pump assembly", "B": "Design for test, which makes the pump easier to check before it is shipped", "C": "Design for producibility, which makes the pump easier to build on the assembly line", "D": "Design for maintainability, which makes service work faster after the sale"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Design for maintainability, which makes service work faster after the sale): Design for X covers constraints such as maintainability, which shapes how quickly and cheaply a product can be serviced. A side panel that lets a technician replace a seal in ten minutes instead of a full teardown is a maintainability choice. Design for cost, design for test, and design for producibility address assembly cost, inspection, and building the product rather than service access."}, {"id": 853, "domain": "organization_wide_planning_and_deployment", "question": "A Black Belt finishes a project and hands the improved process to the department manager, who now owns the results, the control plan, and the response when a chart signals. Which Six Sigma role does this manager fill after the project closes?", "options": {"A": "The champion, who sponsors the project and removes the barrier for the team.", "B": "The process owner, who sustains the gains and answers for the process results.", "C": "The master black belt, who coaches the belts and teaches them the statistical methods.", "D": "The green belt, who collects data and runs small projects on the side."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The process owner, who sustains the gains and answers for the process results.): After the project closes, the person who runs the process takes responsibility for the control plan, the charts, and the response when a signal fires. The champion sponsors the project and removes barriers while it runs. The master black belt provides coaching and statistical support, and the green belt executes smaller projects. Neither role owns the day to day process results after handover."}, {"id": 860, "domain": "team_management", "question": "A Black Belt forms a cross functional team with members from four departments. Before the first working session, the team agrees on how decisions get made and how members will treat one another. Which simple tool captures these agreements for the whole team?", "options": {"A": "A control plan that the process owner signs at the formal project close.", "B": "A work breakdown structure that lists every task and owner.", "C": "A cause and effect diagram that sorts the team ideas.", "D": "A team charter or ground rules list that the members write and follow."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A team charter or ground rules list that the members write and follow.): A charter states the team purpose, the decision rules, and the behaviors members agree to follow, and writing it together builds ownership. A control plan and a work breakdown structure serve other purposes, and a cause and effect diagram sorts causes rather than setting team agreements."}, {"id": 861, "domain": "team_management", "question": "Two team members disagree strongly about the root cause of a defect and the discussion turns personal. The Black Belt asks each person to restate the other's position before responding. Which conflict resolution approach is the Black Belt using here?", "options": {"A": "Smoothing, which plays down the disagreement and moves quickly to the next discussion topic.", "B": "Active listening and reflection, which lowers the heat before the team resolves the issue.", "C": "Forcing, which uses rank to decide the question and ends the debate quickly.", "D": "Withdrawal, which postpones the topic until one member leaves the team."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Active listening and reflection, which lowers the heat before the team resolves the issue.): Asking each person to restate the other's view forces real listening and separates the technical disagreement from the personal tone. Smoothing hides the issue, forcing uses rank, and withdrawal defers the topic. All three leave the underlying dispute unresolved, which is why the reflective approach fits better here."}, {"id": 865, "domain": "define", "question": "A Black Belt is writing a problem statement for a project on late deliveries at a furniture plant. The sponsor wants a version that names the gap, its size, and the time frame. Which written statement meets that clear standard?", "options": {"A": "Drivers are careless, which is why so many furniture orders arrive late every week now.", "B": "On time delivery fell from 94 to 87 percent between January and June this year.", "C": "Install a new dispatch system so that all furniture orders ship on schedule.", "D": "Delivery is a problem that the plant should fix as soon as possible."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (On time delivery fell from 94 to 87 percent between January and June this year.): A useful problem statement names the gap, its size, and the time frame, and it avoids a guessed cause or a chosen fix. Option A names a cause, option C prescribes a solution, and option D is too vague to measure. Only option B states the observable gap with numbers and dates."}, {"id": 866, "domain": "define", "question": "A project charter for a claims team lists the goal only as improve quality. The champion asks the Black Belt to rewrite it as a measurable target with an owner and a deadline. Which rewritten goal meets the SMART standard?", "options": {"A": "Improve claims quality soon so that the department looks better to leadership.", "B": "Raise the quality awareness of the claims team through a new training program.", "C": "Cut claims rework from 11 to 5 percent by June 30, with an owner.", "D": "Reduce claims rework to a much lower level as soon as the team can manage."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cut claims rework from 11 to 5 percent by June 30, with an owner.): A SMART goal is specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time bound, and option C carries a baseline, a target, an owner, and a date. The other options ask for vague improvement, add training without a measure, or set no real target and no deadline."}, {"id": 867, "domain": "define", "question": "A Black Belt builds a business case for a quality project and lists scrap, rework, warranty claims, and returned goods as the main costs the project will attack. The finance partner wants each item placed in the right bucket. Which cost category do these items represent?", "options": {"A": "The cost of poor quality, which covers the waste created when work fails to meet requirements.", "B": "The cost of good quality, which covers the prevention and appraisal spending on the whole process.", "C": "The total cost of ownership, which covers the purchase price and the operating costs.", "D": "The standard cost of goods, which covers raw material and direct labor only."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The cost of poor quality, which covers the waste created when work fails to meet requirements.): Scrap, rework, warranty claims, and returned goods are all costs created when the process fails to meet requirements, which is the cost of poor quality. The cost of good quality covers prevention and appraisal spending, and total cost of ownership and standard cost of goods describe other accounting views."}, {"id": 870, "domain": "define", "question": "The champion hands the Black Belt a one page document that states the business case, the problem statement, the goal, the scope, the team members, and the key milestones, with approval signatures. Which define phase deliverable is this signed document called?", "options": {"A": "The control plan, which lists how the improved process will be monitored.", "B": "The measurement system study, which quantifies the variation from the gauge.", "C": "The stakeholder register, which lists the interests and influence of each involved party.", "D": "The project charter, which authorizes the project and sets its boundaries and goals."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The project charter, which authorizes the project and sets its boundaries and goals.): The charter is the define phase document that names the business case, the problem, the goal, the scope, the team, and the milestones, and it carries the sponsor's authorization. The control plan and the measurement study belong to later phases, and the stakeholder register is a separate tool that supports the charter."}, {"id": 873, "domain": "measure", "question": "Two inspectors measure the same fifty parts and disagree on several readings. Before studying process capability, the Black Belt wants to know how much of the variation comes from the measurement system. Which measurement system study best answers this question?", "options": {"A": "A gage repeatability and reproducibility study, which separates equipment and appraiser variation.", "B": "A process capability study, which compares the process spread with the customer specification limits.", "C": "A control chart study, which plots the subgroup averages over a period of time.", "D": "A design of experiments, which tests how factors change the process response."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A gage repeatability and reproducibility study, which separates equipment and appraiser variation.): A gage repeatability and reproducibility study has several appraisers measure the same parts more than once, which separates the variation from the gauge itself, the variation between appraisers, and the part to part variation. Capability, control chart, and experiment studies answer different questions and do not separate those measurement components."}, {"id": 874, "domain": "measure", "question": "A Black Belt needs a sample from a warehouse with three shifts. Because defect rates may differ by shift, the team draws a random sample from each shift in proportion to its size. Which sampling method is the team using?", "options": {"A": "Simple random sampling, which draws from the whole warehouse without subgroups.", "B": "Systematic sampling, which takes every tenth item from the production line.", "C": "Convenience sampling, which takes the items that are easiest to reach today.", "D": "Stratified sampling, which draws from each subgroup in proportion to its size."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Stratified sampling, which draws from each subgroup in proportion to its size.): Stratified sampling splits the population into subgroups and draws a random sample from each one in proportion to its size, which keeps every shift represented. Simple random sampling ignores the subgroups, systematic sampling follows a fixed interval, and convenience sampling takes whatever is easiest to reach."}, {"id": 877, "domain": "measure", "question": "A filling process has a mean fill of 50 and a standard deviation of 2. The lower specification limit is 44 grams and the upper specification limit is 56 grams. Which statement about the capability of this centered process is correct?", "options": {"A": "Both Cp and Cpk equal 2, since the tolerance width covers six sigma.", "B": "Both Cp and Cpk equal 1, since the process is centered three sigma from each limit.", "C": "Cp equals 1 but Cpk equals 0.5, since the process mean sits off center.", "D": "Cp equals 1.33 but Cpk equals 0.75, since the process spread is much too wide overall."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Both Cp and Cpk equal 1, since the process is centered three sigma from each limit.): Cp equals the tolerance width divided by six sigma, which here is 12 divided by 12 and equals 1. The mean of 50 sits 6 units from each limit, which is 3 times the standard deviation of 2, and 6 divided by 6 gives a Cpk of 1 as well, so the process is centered and the two indices agree. The other options misstate either the spread or the centering."}, {"id": 878, "domain": "measure", "question": "A production process has a defect rate of about 6,210 defects per million opportunities. Analysts often convert that figure into a sigma level by applying the common 1.5 sigma shift convention. Which sigma level does this level of performance represent?", "options": {"A": "Three sigma, because the defect rate sits just under the four sigma mark.", "B": "Five sigma, because the rate is far below the six sigma target level.", "C": "Four sigma, because 6,210 defects per million matches that level in the table.", "D": "Six sigma, because only rates near 3.4 defects per million can reach the six sigma level."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Four sigma, because 6,210 defects per million matches that level in the table.): The standard sigma conversion table with the 1.5 sigma shift lists 6,210 defects per million at four sigma. Three sigma is about 66,807, five sigma is about 233, and six sigma is 3.4, so only the four sigma figure matches the given rate."}, {"id": 885, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A call center logs 500 customer complaints for the quarter. A Pareto chart ranks the reasons as billing errors 240, long wait times 135, agent attitude 65, wrong information 40, and other causes 20. Which action follows from this chart?", "options": {"A": "Work on every listed cause at once, since each one adds to the complaint total.", "B": "Work on billing errors first, since they make up 48 percent of the complaints.", "C": "Ignore the top cause and start with the smallest, since small gains come faster.", "D": "Work on long wait times first, since they are the second largest single reason."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Work on billing errors first, since they make up 48 percent of the complaints.): Billing errors account for 240 of the 500 complaints, and 240 divided by 500 equals 0.48, or 48 percent. A Pareto chart directs effort to the largest bar first because it offers the biggest reduction. Spreading effort across every cause at once, starting with the smallest, or jumping to the second largest all work against that logic.", "image": "question_images/q_885.png"}, {"id": 890, "domain": "improve", "question": "An assembly cell loses time because tools are missing, parts are mixed together, and operators walk long distances to find what they need. Which lean program would sort the workplace, set it in order, and sustain those gains over time?", "options": {"A": "The 5S method, which sorts, sets in order, shines, standardizes, and sustains.", "B": "The kanban method, which uses cards to pull material through the cell.", "C": "The kaizen blitz, which runs a short intense improvement event.", "D": "The theory of constraints, which finds and then lifts the main system bottleneck."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The 5S method, which sorts, sets in order, shines, standardizes, and sustains.): The 5S method organizes the workplace so tools and parts have a defined place, which removes searching, mixing, and extra walking. Kanban controls material flow with signals, a kaizen blitz is a short improvement event, and the theory of constraints targets a bottleneck. The described problems are housekeeping and organization issues."}, {"id": 891, "domain": "improve", "question": "A supplier ships parts in weekly batches, and the assembly line often runs out between deliveries. The team wants each workstation to signal upstream whenever it needs a small batch of parts. Which lean tool fits this need for a pull system?", "options": {"A": "Heijunka, which levels the mix and volume of production across the day.", "B": "Kanban, which uses a card or signal to authorize and pull the next batch.", "C": "SMED, which cuts the time needed to change over between products.", "D": "Andon, which alerts the team when a defect or problem appears on the line."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Kanban, which uses a card or signal to authorize and pull the next batch.): Kanban uses a card or another signal to tell upstream work when to produce the next batch, which replaces the push of weekly shipments with a pull that matches actual use. Heijunka levels production, SMED shortens changeovers, and andon alerts the team to a problem. Only kanban is the signal to replenish."}, {"id": 896, "domain": "control", "question": "After a project cuts defects, the Black Belt writes a one page document that lists the process steps, the key measures, the chart to use, the sample size, and who responds when a signal appears. Which control phase deliverable is this?", "options": {"A": "A project charter, which authorizes the work and sets the scope and goal.", "B": "A value stream map, which shows the flow of work and the waiting time.", "C": "A control plan, which names each measure, the chart, the sample, and the response.", "D": "A measurement system study, which quantifies the variation that comes from the gauge."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A control plan, which names each measure, the chart, the sample, and the response.): The control plan is the control phase document that lists what to measure, the chart to use, the sample size and frequency, and who acts when a signal appears. A charter authorizes work, a value stream map shows flow, and a measurement study quantifies gauge variation. Only the control plan gathers the monitoring details."}, {"id": 897, "domain": "control", "question": "A process is improved and the team confirms the gains with a capability study. Six months later the same gauge is used now on a new product. Which control action should the team take to keep the measurement data still trustworthy?", "options": {"A": "Repeat the gage study, since a new product can change the measurement variation.", "B": "Retrain the operators, since new products require fresh training on every gauge.", "C": "Buy a second gauge, since two gauges often reduce the measurement error.", "D": "Continue the capability study, since the earlier results still describe the gauge."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Repeat the gage study, since a new product can change the measurement variation.): A new product or a changed measurement situation can alter the repeatability and reproducibility of the gauge, so the study should be repeated before the data is trusted. Retraining may help but does not measure the system, a second gauge does not by itself reduce error, and the old capability results describe the old product."}, {"id": 898, "domain": "control", "question": "A plant fixes a recurring jam and posts a photo of the correct setup beside the machine, along with a daily check sheet that operators sign each shift. Which reliable control approach keeps this fix in place over the long run?", "options": {"A": "A tighter specification, which forces the machine to hold a narrower range.", "B": "A larger sample, which gives the team more data about the recurring jam.", "C": "Visual controls and standard work, which make the correct method plain to the operator.", "D": "A second shift inspection, which checks the machine after every production run of the shift."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Visual controls and standard work, which make the correct method plain to the operator.): A photo of the correct setup and a signed daily check sheet are visual controls that support standard work, which is how a fix stays in place after the project ends. A tighter specification changes the requirement, a larger sample only adds data, and extra inspection catches problems rather than preventing them."}, {"id": 901, "domain": "organization_wide_planning_and_deployment", "question": "A manufacturer has run isolated improvement events for three years, but each department picks its own tools and results fade after the events end. A consultant describes the firm as being at an early maturity level for Lean Six Sigma. What does a maturity model describe in this setting?", "options": {"A": "The number of certified belts the company has trained each year.", "B": "The stage of capability reached in deploying methods across the whole organization.", "C": "The total training budget spent on quality programs during the last year.", "D": "The percentage of completed projects that reported verified savings in the current fiscal period."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The stage of capability reached in deploying methods across the whole organization): A maturity model describes how far an organization has progressed in making Lean Six Sigma part of normal work, from scattered local events to a managed system with trained people, standard methods, and governance. The manufacturer sits at an early stage because each department chooses its own tools and gains disappear when an event ends. Options A, C, and D each describe one number such as belts trained, budget spent, or savings booked. Those numbers can rise at any maturity level, so none of them shows how deeply the methods are embedded in daily operations."}, {"id": 905, "domain": "organizational_process_management_and_measures", "question": "The value stream map shows the claims intake process with value added time and non value added time at each step. Total value added work is 14 minutes and total waiting is 26 minutes. What share of the 40 minute lead time adds value for the customer?", "options": {"A": "About 35 percent, since 14 of the 40 minutes add value.", "B": "About 65 percent, since 26 of the 40 minutes add value.", "C": "About 54 percent, since 14 of the 26 waiting minutes add value.", "D": "About 100 percent, since the map records each step as value added."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 35 percent, since 14 of the 40 minutes add value): Value added time is the work the customer would pay for, so the calculation is 14 divided by 40, which equals 0.35, or 35 percent. The remaining 26 minutes is waiting and does not add value. Option B counts the waiting time as value added, which reverses the meaning of the map. Options C and D use wrong totals or treat every recorded step as value added, and both ignore the lead time of 40 minutes that the chart reports.", "image": "question_images/q_905.png"}, {"id": 919, "domain": "define", "question": "A team collects 80 written improvement ideas during a workshop. The ideas cover scheduling, training, tooling, and supplier issues, and the list is too long to sort by debate. Which analytical tool should the Black Belt use first to group the ideas?", "options": {"A": "A Gantt chart that schedules all the ideas over several working weeks.", "B": "An affinity diagram that groups ideas into natural themes.", "C": "A Pareto chart that ranks the ideas by expected cost.", "D": "A control chart that tracks idea counts over time."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (An affinity diagram that groups ideas into natural themes): An affinity diagram sorts a large set of ideas into natural groups by theme, which is exactly the task when 80 ideas cover several areas and debate would take too long. Option A schedules work rather than grouping ideas, and option C ranks items by a numeric value such as cost. Option D tracks a count over time. The affinity diagram is the tool used in the define phase to turn a long list into a small number of themes."}, {"id": 920, "domain": "define", "question": "A draft problem statement reads: quality is poor in the shipping department and must get better soon. A reviewer rejects the draft and asks the team to rewrite it before the charter is approved. Which flaw is the main reason the statement fails review?", "options": {"A": "It names a whole department instead of a specific customer segment.", "B": "It reports a process capability index in place of the actual defect count.", "C": "It uses a target that is too easy for the team to reach.", "D": "It lacks a baseline, a target, and a time frame for the problem."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It lacks a baseline, a target, and a time frame for the problem): A usable problem statement states the metric, the baseline, the target, and the time frame, and this draft says only that quality is poor and must get better soon. Naming the shipping department is not the defect, because a problem statement may name the area where the work happens. Option B describes content the draft never presents, since no capability index and no defect count appear in it. The draft also proposes no target at all, so option C cannot be too easy. The missing measurement detail is what forces the rewrite."}, {"id": 924, "domain": "measure", "question": "A filling process has a lower specification limit of 20.0 ounces, an upper specification limit of 30.0 ounces, an observed mean of 25.5 ounces, and a process standard deviation of 1.5 ounces. What is the Cpk value for this filling process?", "options": {"A": "About 1.00, because the upper side is the closer of the two.", "B": "About 1.11, because the spread fits within the tolerance width.", "C": "About 1.22, because the mean sits closer to the lower limit.", "D": "About 0.89, because the process centering shifts the index down."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (About 1.00, because the upper side is the closer of the two): Cpk compares the mean with each specification limit. The upper side uses 30.0 minus 25.5, which equals 4.5, and three times the standard deviation of 1.5 is 4.5, so the upper index is 4.5 divided by 4.5, which equals 1.00. The lower calculation is 25.5 minus 20.0, which equals 5.5, divided by 4.5, which equals 1.22. Cpk is the smaller of the two values, so Cpk is 1.00. Option B is Cp, found by dividing the tolerance width of 10.0 by six sigma, and options C and D name the lower side or an incorrect figure."}, {"id": 929, "domain": "measure", "question": "A machine runs with a natural spread of plus or minus three sigma around its mean. The specifications sit four sigma above and below the same mean, yet operators compare each reading with the specification limits and adjust the machine after every result. What is wrong with this practice?", "options": {"A": "Control limits should be set equal to the specification limits before charting.", "B": "Specifications apply to sample means, not to individual readings.", "C": "The machine needs a wider natural spread to match the specifications.", "D": "Adjusting to every reading adds variation from tampering with the process."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Adjusting to every reading adds variation from tampering with the process): The process shows common cause variation inside its natural three sigma spread, and adjusting after every reading reacts to that normal variation, which adds variation rather than removing it. This mistake is called tampering, and it makes the process worse over time. Option A confuses control limits with specification limits, which measure different things. Option B misstates where specifications apply, and option C asks for a change that the capability already covers, since a three sigma spread against specifications four sigma away is adequate."}, {"id": 930, "domain": "analyze", "question": "Monthly data show that ice cream sales and drowning deaths rise and fall together across the year. A leader concludes that buying ice cream causes people to drown. What should the Black Belt explain about this conclusion and the statistical relationship?", "options": {"A": "The correlation proves the leader's causal claim once sales lead deaths.", "B": "Correlation shows association, and a third factor such as warm weather may drive both.", "C": "The two measures move together, so the leader should accept the stated causal relationship.", "D": "Causation runs the other way, so drowning deaths increase ice cream sales."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Correlation shows association, and a third factor such as warm weather may drive both): Correlation measures whether two variables move together, not whether one causes the other. Warm weather raises ice cream sales and also sends more people to swim, so a third factor can drive both patterns. Option A treats the correlation as proof of cause, which the data cannot supply. Option C repeats that error, and option D swaps the direction of causation without evidence. A designed study or a controlled comparison is what would test the causal claim."}, {"id": 933, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A hospital tests every incoming blood unit, and the null hypothesis is that the unit is safe for use. A test result wrongly flags a safe unit and sends it to the trash. Which type of error has occurred in this case?", "options": {"A": "A type II error, since a false null hypothesis was rejected.", "B": "A type I error, since a false null hypothesis was accepted.", "C": "A type II error, since a true null hypothesis was accepted.", "D": "A type I error, since a true null hypothesis was rejected."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A type I error, since a true null hypothesis was rejected): A type I error rejects a null hypothesis that is true, so flagging a safe unit as unsafe is a type I error, and it costs the hospital a usable unit. Option A describes a rejection of a false null hypothesis, which is the correct decision rather than an error. Option B calls that same case a type I error, which mislabels it. Option C describes a type II error, which is failing to reject a true null hypothesis, and the scenario does the opposite by rejecting it."}, {"id": 936, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A team maps a production cell and finds large stacks of unfinished parts sitting on the floor between stations. The stacks hide defects, lengthen the lead time, and tie up working capital. Which of the seven classic wastes does this situation describe?", "options": {"A": "Inventory waste, because stored work in process represents tied up value.", "B": "Motion waste, because operators walk to reach the stacked parts.", "C": "Overproduction waste, because the cell makes parts faster than the next step needs.", "D": "Waiting waste, because parts sit idle while machines run elsewhere."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Inventory waste, because stored work in process represents tied up value): Inventory waste is stock, including work in process, that waits between steps and ties up cash while hiding defects and adding lead time. The stacks described here match that definition. Option B applies when people must walk or reach, and the scenario does not describe operator movement. Option C applies when a process makes more than the next step needs, and the scenario does not mention excess output. Option D applies to idle people or machines, while the parts here are the waiting item."}, {"id": 937, "domain": "improve", "question": "A team tests a coating process. They vary oven temperature at two settings, cure time at two settings, and measure the peel strength of each sample. In DOE terms, what are temperature, cure time, and peel strength called in this experiment?", "options": {"A": "Factors, factors, and the response variable, in the order listed.", "B": "Treatments, blocks, and replications, in the exact order listed above.", "C": "Responses, factors, and levels, in the order listed above.", "D": "Levels, responses, and factors, in the order listed above."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Factors, factors, and the response variable, in the order listed): In a designed experiment, factors are the inputs the team deliberately changes, and the response is the output being measured. Temperature and cure time are both inputs, so both are factors, and peel strength is the measured output, so it is the response. Option B swaps in treatments, blocks, and replications, which are different DOE concepts. Option C calls the inputs responses, and option D calls the response a factor. The order in the question sets which label fits each term."}, {"id": 938, "domain": "improve", "question": "The DOE table shows a two factor experiment on a chemical yield. Temperature was changed from 150 to 170 degrees and catalyst amount from low to high. The main effect for temperature is 6.5 and for catalyst is 1.8. What does the table show?", "options": {"A": "The catalyst changed the yield more than the temperature did here.", "B": "Temperature changed the yield more than the catalyst did here.", "C": "Both factors changed the yield by the same amount in the runs.", "D": "Neither factor changed the yield enough to matter for the process."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Temperature changed the yield more than the catalyst did here): The main effect is the average change in the response when a factor moves from its low level to its high level, so the temperature effect of 6.5 is larger than the catalyst effect of 1.8. The table responses show the same pattern, with the temperature runs separating more than the catalyst runs. Option A reverses the comparison. Option C claims equal effects, which the numbers contradict, and option D dismisses both effects even though 6.5 is a meaningful change in yield.", "image": "question_images/q_938.png"}, {"id": 939, "domain": "improve", "question": "A team plans a 16 run experiment. The furnace drifts warmer through the day, and the team worries that the drift will be confused with the effects of the factors under study. Which DOE principle should the team apply to address this concern directly?", "options": {"A": "Replication of each run so the sample size grows steadily over the full study.", "B": "Confounding so the drift merges with the main effects being studied.", "C": "Blocking so that the drift is separated from the factor effects.", "D": "Randomizing the run order so the drift is spread across the factor levels."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Randomizing the run order so the drift is spread across the factor levels.): Randomization assigns runs to times or positions by chance, so a slow drift such as a warming furnace is spread across the factor levels instead of being confused with one of them. Blocking groups runs that share a known condition, such as a shift or a lot, which does not remove a drift that grows through the day. Replication raises precision but does not separate the drift, and confounding deliberately merges the drift with the effects, which is the problem to avoid."}, {"id": 941, "domain": "improve", "question": "A cell uses 250 parts per day. Replenishment of the parts takes three days, and the team adds 10 percent as a safety buffer. Each container holds 75 parts. How many kanban containers should the team plan to use on the line?", "options": {"A": "About 10 containers, from the daily use times the replenishment days.", "B": "About 13 containers, from adding one day of demand as a buffer.", "C": "About 11 containers, from use times lead time with the buffer.", "D": "About 3 containers, from the replenishment lead time in days."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (About 11 containers, from use times lead time with the buffer): The kanban count starts with daily use times replenishment time, so 250 parts times 3 days equals 750 parts. Adding a 10 percent buffer gives 750 times 1.10, which equals 825 parts, and dividing by 75 parts per container gives 11 containers. Option A uses the demand over the lead time but leaves out the safety buffer, which gives 10 containers. Option B adds a full extra day of demand instead of the 10 percent buffer, which gives 13 containers, and option D uses the lead time alone. The formula keeps enough containers in the loop to cover demand while replenishment runs."}, {"id": 944, "domain": "control", "question": "A billing team counts the number of incorrect invoices each day. The number of invoices handled changes from day to day, and the team wants to track the proportion that is wrong. Which control chart fits this situation best for the team?", "options": {"A": "An np chart, because the number of invoices changes each day.", "B": "A c chart, because each invoice offers many chances for error.", "C": "A p chart, because the sample size changes from day to day.", "D": "An X bar and R chart, because invoices are measured in pairs."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A p chart, because the sample size changes from day to day): A p chart tracks the proportion of defective units when the sample size can change from period to period, which matches a team that handles a different number of invoices each day. Option A uses an np chart, which needs a constant sample size and therefore does not fit. Option B uses a c chart, which counts defects in a constant inspection unit rather than the proportion of defective invoices. Option D applies to a continuous measurement, while this data is a count of wrong invoices."}, {"id": 946, "domain": "control", "question": "A Black Belt samples five consecutive parts from one machine every hour and treats them as one subgroup. The five parts come from the same short time window, so they share the same conditions. Why is this subgrouping useful for the chart?", "options": {"A": "It keeps short term variation inside subgroups so shifts show between them.", "B": "It mixes many different conditions inside each subgroup to average out every cause.", "C": "It makes each subgroup large enough to reduce the need for limits.", "D": "It places parts from different shifts together to widen the limits."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It keeps short term variation inside subgroups so shifts show between them): Rational subgrouping puts parts that share conditions in the same subgroup, so the variation inside a subgroup reflects short term common cause only. Any change between subgroups, such as a shift in the mean, then stands out on the chart. Option B mixes conditions inside a subgroup, which blurs the signal the chart is meant to show. Option C confuses subgroup size with control limits, and option D combines different shifts, which hides a shift to shift change rather than revealing it."}, {"id": 947, "domain": "control", "question": "After an improvement, a plant wants operators to notice a worn drive belt before it fails. The team paints a wear mark on the belt and posts a photo of the correct belt tension at the station. Which control approach does this describe?", "options": {"A": "A control plan that lists who audits the belt each month.", "B": "A preventive maintenance schedule that the manufacturer sets for the belt.", "C": "A gauge study that measures how well operators read the belt.", "D": "Visual controls that make the normal and worn states easy to see."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Visual controls that make the normal and worn states easy to see): Visual controls use pictures, marks, and signals so the normal condition and the abnormal condition can be compared at a glance. A wear mark on the belt and a photo of correct tension make the difference visible without a measurement. Option A relies on an audit schedule, which acts after the fact. Option B uses the maker's maintenance schedule and does not show wear on the floor, and option C studies how well operators read the belt rather than making the belt easy to read."}, {"id": 948, "domain": "control", "question": "A project team cuts defects on a molding line and hands the process back to the production supervisor. Before the handoff, the team must document how the new settings are monitored and what to do when a point goes out of control. Which document does this?", "options": {"A": "A lessons learned log that lists what the team would change next time.", "B": "A project charter that restates the original problem and the agreed business goal.", "C": "A control plan that names the measures, limits, and reactions to take.", "D": "A work breakdown structure that divides the handoff into tasks."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A control plan that names the measures, limits, and reactions to take): A control plan documents what is monitored, how often, which limits apply, and what the operator should do when a signal appears, so it is the document that carries the improved process into routine use. Option A records lessons for the future but does not direct daily monitoring. Option B restates the project purpose, and option D divides the handoff into tasks without defining limits or reactions. The control plan is the standard tool for the handoff to the process owner."}, {"id": 949, "domain": "design_for_six_sigma", "question": "A firm wants to launch a new disposable sensor that customers will use for the first time. No production process exists yet, and the design must meet a tight reliability target at launch. Which improvement roadmap should the Black Belt use?", "options": {"A": "DMADV, because it designs a new product and its process.", "B": "DMAIC, because it improves a process that already exists today.", "C": "PDCA, because it cycles through planning and checking repeatedly.", "D": "FMEA, because it ranks failure modes by risk priority number."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (DMADV, because it designs a new product and its process): Design for Six Sigma uses DMADV for a new product or process that does not yet exist, moving from define and measure through analyze and design to validate. A brand new sensor with no production process and a reliability target fits that situation. Option B applies DMAIC to an existing process, which is not the case here. Option C describes a general improvement cycle, and option D names a risk tool rather than a roadmap. The missing process is the signal to design rather than improve."}, {"id": 951, "domain": "organization_wide_planning_and_deployment", "question": "A plant makes custom industrial pumps in low volumes with wide product variety, and the site wants faster flow with less inventory rather than tighter defect reduction. A Black Belt is asked which improvement approach fits the work best. What should the Black Belt recommend?", "options": {"A": "Start a Six Sigma project aimed at cutting dimensional defects on every pump model.", "B": "Use statistical process control charts on every machining operation before any change.", "C": "Apply lean methods to reduce queue time, batch size, and travel distance.", "D": "Run a design of experiments study on the pump housing casting process."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Apply lean methods to reduce queue time, batch size, and travel distance.): The plant faces a flow problem with high variety and low volume, and lean tools target waiting, batch size, and travel, which is exactly the waste described. Six Sigma suits chronic defects in a stable process, and the stem points to flow rather than defect levels. Control charts need a stable process before they help, and a design of experiments study fits tuning factors once the flow problem is settled."}, {"id": 958, "domain": "organizational_process_management_and_measures", "question": "A project forecasts overtime for a packaging line but finishes the work in regular hours instead, and the budget still carries the forecast amount. The finance partner refuses to count the avoided overtime as a hard saving. The Black Belt asks why finance sees it that way. Which explanation of cost benefit analysis is correct?", "options": {"A": "Avoided costs count as hard savings once the Black Belt documents them.", "B": "Avoided costs are soft benefits because they reduce the reported revenue on the statement.", "C": "Avoided costs are hard savings because overtime is a recorded expense line.", "D": "Avoided costs are soft benefits because no cash appears in the ledger."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Avoided costs are soft benefits because no cash appears in the ledger.): Hard savings show up as real changes in the accounts, while avoided costs are soft benefits because the money was not spent and no cash entry appears. Documentation by a Black Belt does not turn a soft benefit into a hard one. Overtime is a recorded expense, but the forecast amount was never worked and the budget still carries it, so no saving books; avoided costs also do not reduce reported revenue."}, {"id": 962, "domain": "team_management", "question": "A team must rank eight competing improvement ideas, but the most senior member speaks first at every meeting and the rest agree quickly. The Black Belt wants a ranking that reflects the whole group. Which decision method fits this situation best?", "options": {"A": "Let the senior member rank the ideas and have the team accept the list.", "B": "Vote in one round and give each member a single vote on an idea.", "C": "Discuss the ideas openly until all the members agree on the same ranked list.", "D": "Use the nominal group technique so members write and rank ideas privately."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Use the nominal group technique so members write and rank ideas privately.): The nominal group technique collects written ideas and rankings before discussion, which reduces the pull of a senior voice and gives every member equal input. Letting the senior member rank first repeats the problem. A single round of voting gives one vote per person without the silent idea step, and talking until everyone agrees can still be steered by the loudest member."}, {"id": 964, "domain": "define", "question": "A hotel wants to know why guests avoid using the self-service kiosks in the lobby. Surveys and interviews give vague answers, so the team suggests watching guests use the kiosks. Which voice of the customer method is the team proposing?", "options": {"A": "A focus group where guests discuss the kiosk experience together.", "B": "A written survey mailed to guests after they complete their stay.", "C": "Direct observation of guests as they interact with the kiosk.", "D": "A complaint log that records every kiosk problem reported by staff."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Direct observation of guests as they interact with the kiosk.): Watching people use the kiosk is observation, which shows what customers actually do rather than what they say they do. A focus group and a written survey gather opinions and can repeat the vague answers already found. A complaint log records problems that someone else chose to report, so it misses quiet failures."}, {"id": 966, "domain": "define", "question": "A charter states that the warehouse is too slow and that the team will fix it. The Black Belt must rewrite the problem statement so it can be measured against a baseline. Which rewrite meets the requirements for a problem statement?", "options": {"A": "Order picking takes too long, and the team should improve the picking process.", "B": "The warehouse needs a better layout so that picking becomes faster overall.", "C": "Picking errors and slow picking routinely cause customer complaints about late shipments.", "D": "Median order picking time is 74 minutes, and customers expect under 45 minutes."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Median order picking time is 74 minutes, and customers expect under 45 minutes.): A usable problem statement gives a measured baseline and an expected level, so the team can show the size of the gap. Saying the warehouse is too slow or needs a better layout states no measurement. Naming possible causes such as errors and layout moves into analysis before the problem has been sized."}, {"id": 971, "domain": "measure", "question": "Nurses walk a long distance to fetch supplies during a clinic visit, and nobody can see the wasted travel on the current flowchart. The Black Belt wants a picture that traces the actual path people walk. Which process analysis tool fits this need?", "options": {"A": "A value stream map that totals value added and waiting time.", "B": "A spaghetti diagram that traces the physical path of the walk.", "C": "A work instruction that lists the steps for stocking each room.", "D": "A gemba walk that asks staff where they think the delays are."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A spaghetti diagram that traces the physical path of the walk.): A spaghetti diagram draws the actual path of people or parts and makes travel easy to see, which is the waste named in the stem. A value stream map totals time across a process but does not show the walking path. A work instruction tells someone how to stock a room, and a gemba walk gathers views from staff rather than tracing the route."}, {"id": 972, "domain": "measure", "question": "A Black Belt records the order in which five suppliers rank on delivery speed, from fastest to slowest, without recording the actual number of days. The analyst asks what measurement scale this ranking uses. Which scale does the ranking represent?", "options": {"A": "An ordinal scale, because the ranks show order but not equal gaps.", "B": "A nominal scale, because supplier names are labels with no order.", "C": "An interval scale, because the gap between any two ranks is the same size.", "D": "A ratio scale, because one supplier sits at the top of the list."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (An ordinal scale, because the ranks show order but not equal gaps.): Ordinal data place items in a ranked order without promising that the space between rank one and rank two equals the space between rank two and rank three. Nominal data carry no order. Interval and ratio scales both need real numeric distance, and the stem records no actual days, so those scales do not fit."}, {"id": 973, "domain": "measure", "question": "A bottling line fills 200 bottles during a shift. The histogram shows fill volumes grouped in one mL bins against a lower specification limit of 97 and an upper limit of 103. Which statement correctly describes what the chart shows?", "options": {"A": "The process is centered on the target with a spread well inside the limits.", "B": "The process sits far above the target, with most readings near the upper limit.", "C": "The process sits slightly below center, with 9 readings below the lower limit.", "D": "The spread is too narrow for the limits, so the process needs wider control limits."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The process sits slightly below center, with 9 readings below the lower limit.): The two lowest bins hold 2 and 7 readings, which puts 9 of 200 bottles under the 97 mL lower limit, and the bulk of the distribution sits below the 100 mL midpoint of the specification. The chart does not show a centered process or a pile of readings at the upper limit. A narrow spread would be good news rather than a reason to widen control limits.", "image": "question_images/q_973.png"}, {"id": 981, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A team plots fill weight against line speed on a scatter diagram. The points fall in a downward band from the upper left to the lower right. The Black Belt is asked what the chart shows about the two variables. Which reading of the scatter diagram is correct?", "options": {"A": "Fill weight clearly rises as line speed rises, showing a positive relationship.", "B": "Fill weight stays flat as line speed rises, showing no relationship.", "C": "Fill weight falls as line speed rises, showing a negative relationship.", "D": "Fill weight changes randomly, so the two variables are unrelated."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Fill weight falls as line speed rises, showing a negative relationship.): A band that runs from upper left to lower right marks a negative relationship, where higher line speed pairs with lower fill weight. A positive relationship would rise to the right, and a flat band would mean no link. Random scatter would fill the plot without a direction, which does not match a clear downward band.", "image": "question_images/q_981.png"}, {"id": 984, "domain": "analyze", "question": "A finance team builds a fishbone diagram to study why the month end close runs late each period. The diagram groups candidate causes into six branches such as methods and people. Which statement best describes what this diagram gives the team?", "options": {"A": "It proves which single root cause produces the late month end close each period.", "B": "It ranks each branch by the count of defects that branch produced.", "C": "It organizes possible causes for testing, but it does not prove any one cause.", "D": "It shows the process steps in order so the team can spot the delay."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It organizes possible causes for testing, but it does not prove any one cause.): A cause and effect diagram sorts candidate causes into branches so the team can plan tests, and it does not prove which cause is at work on its own. A single proven cause would need data. Ranking branches by defect counts belongs to a Pareto chart, and showing process steps in order belongs to a process map.", "image": "question_images/q_984.png"}, {"id": 986, "domain": "improve", "question": "A team maps a loan approval process and finds files waiting in a tray for four days, clerks walking to a printer across the floor, and two approval steps that repeat the same check. Which pair of the classic wastes appears here?", "options": {"A": "Inventory and defects, because files stack up and checks repeat twice.", "B": "Overproduction and waiting, because the team starts too many loans.", "C": "Waiting and motion, because files sit idle and clerks walk far.", "D": "Transportation and over-processing, because the printer sits far away."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Waiting and motion, because files sit idle and clerks walk far.): Files sitting in a tray for four days is waiting, and clerks walking across the floor for printouts is motion, so those two wastes are both present. Inventory and defects do not match the walking. Overproduction would mean starting more loans than demand. Transportation covers moving the product itself, and the repeated approval step is better described as over-processing than the printer distance."}, {"id": 991, "domain": "improve", "question": "A plant improves a polishing cell so it now finishes work faster, but the shipping rate does not change because the paint booth cannot keep up at the current pace. The Black Belt explains this result. Which improvement principle does this situation illustrate?", "options": {"A": "Every process step must be improved at once before shipping volume can rise.", "B": "Faster polishing cuts the lead time for the polishing step alone.", "C": "The paint booth should add inspection so fewer units need rework.", "D": "Improving a non-constraint adds little, so focus on the paint booth."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Improving a non-constraint adds little, so focus on the paint booth.): The theory of constraints says the system moves at the speed of its bottleneck, so speeding up the polishing cell adds output only until the paint booth fills up. Improving every step at once spends effort where it may not matter. A faster polishing step shortens its own lead time but not the plant rate, and adding inspection at the booth does not add capacity."}, {"id": 994, "domain": "control", "question": "An X bar and R chart tracks the fill volume of a bottle line. The chart has a center line at 12.0, an upper control limit of 13.0, and a lower control limit of 11.0. One subgroup sits above the upper limit. Which statement about the chart is correct?", "options": {"A": "The process is in control, since only one point sits above the limit.", "B": "Subgroup 7 is out of control, so a special cause needs a look.", "C": "The limits should be raised to 13.6 so the chart shows control again.", "D": "Subgroup 7 shows a specification violation that rejects the whole batch."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Subgroup 7 is out of control, so a special cause needs a look.): A point above the upper control limit is a signal of a special cause, so the team should investigate what changed at that time rather than adjust the limits. Control limits are calculated from the process, and raising them to hide a point is tampering. The chart compares a point with control limits, not specification limits, so it cannot condemn the batch on its own.", "image": "question_images/q_994.png"}, {"id": 998, "domain": "control", "question": "Six months after a successful process improvement on the shop floor, defect rates creep back up because new hires were not taught the revised work method, and no refresher training exists. Which gap in sustaining the improvement does this situation show?", "options": {"A": "The control chart limits were set too narrow for the improved process.", "B": "The training plan and standard work were not updated for the process.", "C": "The measurement system was not capable enough to detect the change.", "D": "The project team chose the wrong improvement tools during the project."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The training plan and standard work were not updated for the process.): Sustaining a gain depends on documenting the new method and training everyone who will use it, so new hires work the same way as the project team did. Control chart limits and gauge capability concern how the process is measured, not how people were taught. Choosing different improvement tools would have shown up during the project rather than six months later."}];
